/root/.codex/sessions/2026/09/06/rollout-2026-09-06T18-02-21-01a07774-da5a-7751-beb8-16e2ba629dff.jsonl:10419:{"timestamp":"2026-09-07T18:56:03.237Z","ordinal":10418,"type":"compacted","payload":{"message":"","replacement_history":[{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a0781c-a0e1-7110-9da8-76fc5d78598d","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Provision the real Android WireGuard peer and continue\nPATCH-NET-004 client validation.\n\nAndroid client PUBLIC KEY:\njxZ2fEyoynXMrhNPZitfKY1kqMZBZ/6bYLbyZb6XCXM=\n\nPeer configuration:\n\nName:\nworm-os-test-01\n\nPublicKey:\njxZ2fEyoynXMrhNPZitfKY1kqMZBZ/6bYLbyZb6XCXM=\n\nAllowed IP:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nOPNsense:\nwg0 = 10.60.0.1/24\nlisten UDP = 51821\n\nIMPORTANT:\nThis is a PUBLIC key, not private material.\n\nDo not recreate…108 tokens truncated…rd tunnel.\n\nWhile the Android tunnel is enabled, monitor:\n- latest handshake\n- RX\n- TX\n\nIf handshake succeeds, continue PATCH-NET-004 validation:\n- NAT\n- DNS 10.60.0.1\n- Internet egress\n- expected public IPv4 2.28.34.27\n- private routing 10.90.0.0/16\n- ADMIN isolation\n- SSH/admin isolation\n- IPv6 leak protection\n\nDo not mark PATCH-NET-004 PASS until the live Android test succeeds.\n\nNever print private keys."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0781c-a0c7-7810-b039-292148a03e8e","create_time":1788721537.2495158,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07832-8145-7f40-82ff-37a98fe01b6e","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Android WireGuard configuration has been visually verified:\n\nClient public key:\njxZ2fEyoynXMrhNPZitfKY1kqMZBZ/6bYLbyZb6XCXM=\n\nAddress:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nOPNsense peer public key:\nVJtONCHbxMj3d7SAsPD5OFkTD7NcAoNgjIfmTcinE04=\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51821\n\nAllowedIPs:\n0.0.0.0/0\n\nPersistentKeepalive:\n25\n\nDNS:\n10.60.0.1\n\nDo not modify the Android configuration.\n\nUse private SSH to OPNsense.\n\nStart a 90-second simultaneous live monitor:\n\n1. wg show wg0 repeatedly\n2. packet capture on vtnet0 for UDP port 51821\n3. packet capture on wg0 if useful\n4. verify pf WAN rule counters for UDP/51821\n\nPrint exactly:\n\nMONITOR READY - ENABLE ANDROID WIREGUARD NOW\n\nThen monitor for the full 90 seconds.\n\nDo not change NAT, DNS, keys or firewall during the capture.\n\nReport:\n\nWAN_UDP_51821_PACKETS=<count>\nWG_HANDSHAKE=RECENT/NEVER\nWG_RX=<value>\nWG_TX=<value>\nPF_RULE_MATCHES=<value>\n\nIf WAN_UDP_51821_PACKETS=0:\ninvestigate only the path before OPNsense:\n- Hetzner public networking\n- WAN address 2.28.34.27\n- UDP reachability\n- Android/mobile carrier path\n\nDo not alter internal NAT/DNS.\n\nIf packets arrive but handshake remains NEVER:\ninvestigate WireGuard server public/private key correspondence and peer\nconfiguration without printing private keys.\n\nDo not regenerate any key."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07832-812a-72e1-8d21-49a387041c95","create_time":1788722970.9494007,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07836-e305-7153-b425-a311ffb6591d","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Do NOT regenerate or change any WireGuard key.\n\nThe reported key mismatch may be a false finding.\nPerform an exact byte-for-byte comparison.\n\nOver private SSH to OPNsense:\n\nACTUAL=\"$(wg show wg0 public-key)\"\nEXPECTED='VJtONCHbxMj3d7SAsPD5OFkTD7NcAoNgjIfmTcinE04='\n\nprintf 'ACTUAL=%s\\n' \"$ACTUAL\"\nprintf 'EXPECTED=%s\\n' \"$EXPECTED\"\n\nif [ \"$ACTUAL\" = \"$EXPECTED\" ]; then\n    echo \"SERVER_KEY_MATCH=YES\"\nelse\n    echo \"SERVER_KEY_MATCH=NO\"\nfi\n\nAlso verify the configured Android peer on OPNsense:\n\nwg show wg0 peers\nwg show wg0 allowed-ips\n\nExpected Android public key:\njxZ2fEyoynXMrhNPZitfKY1kqMZBZ/6bYLbyZb6XCXM=\n\nExpected AllowedIP:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nDo not print private keys.\n\nIf SERVER_KEY_MATCH=YES and Android peer also matches,\ndiscard the previous \"key mismatch\" finding.\n\nThen diagnose the next stage without modifying configuration:\n\n1. Confirm wg0 UDP socket is bound to 51821.\n2. Capture UDP/51821 on WAN while Android tunnel is enabled.\n3. Check whether pf firewall passes or blocks those packets.\n4. Check wg show wg0 immediately after packets arrive.\n5. Check OPNsense WireGuard service/logs for handshake/authentication errors.\n\nDo not modify NAT or DNS yet.\nNAT/DNS are downstream of the handshake.\n\nReport exactly:\n\nSERVER_KEY_MATCH=YES/NO\nANDROID_PEER_MATCH=YES/NO\nWAN_PACKETS=<count>\nPF_PASS=YES/NO/UNKNOWN\nHANDSHAKE=RECENT/NEVER"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07836-e2d9-74f0-b9c4-0a759dea102d","create_time":1788723258.1177952,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a0783b-a93b-7bc3-bc17-440c1782b842","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"The Android WireGuard key has been corrected to the ACTUAL OPNsense key.\n\nAndroid now shows:\nTX approximately 1.88 KiB\nRX 0 B\n\nSo the client is transmitting but receiving nothing.\n\nDo not change Android configuration.\nDo not regenerate any keys.\nDo not modify NAT/DNS yet.\n\nUse private SSH to OPNsense and start a live 90-second monitor.\n\nCheck simultaneously:\n- wg show wg0\n- tcpdump on vtnet0 UDP port 51821\n- pf firewall/rule state for UDP 51821\n\nPrint:\n\nMONITOR READY - ENABLE ANDROID WIREGUARD NOW\n\nThen I will enable the Android tunnel.\n\nDetermine:\n\nWAN_RX_UDP51821=<count>\nWAN_TX_UDP51821=<count>\nHANDSHAKE=RECENT/NEVER\nWG_RX=<bytes>\nWG_TX=<bytes>\nPF_WAN_51821=PASS/BLOCK/UNKNOWN\n\nIf packets reach vtnet0 but wg0 handshake remains NEVER:\ninspect whether UDP/51821 is actually passed by pf to the local\nWireGuard socket.\n\nVerify the WAN rule is:\nUDP -> WAN address -> port 51821 -> PASS\n\nDo not open TCP 22/80/443 on WAN.\n\nIf handshake becomes RECENT:\nimmediately continue testing:\nDNS -> NAT -> WAN egress.\n\nExpected public IPv4:\n2.28.34.27\n\nDo not mark PATCH-NET-004 PASS until Android has working Internet."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0783b-a8fe-7800-8ee6-fac57526e679","create_time":1788723571.0038002,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a0783f-9e5a-7fa3-86c2-4d76c73f99b8","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Repair OPNsense PF ruleset before continuing PATCH-NET-004.\n\nCurrent evidence:\n- Android UDP/51821 reaches OPNsense vtnet0.\n- WAN_RX_UDP51821 > 0.\n- OPNsense sends no UDP response.\n- wg0 handshake remains NEVER.\n- Android peer is correctly provisioned.\n- Android now uses the ACTUAL OPNsense public key.\n- /tmp/rules.debug contains the intended UDP/51821 PASS rule.\n- pfctl -sr -v shows no visible loaded rules.\n- firewall logs contain previous PF ruleset load failures caused by\n  generated syntax errors.\n\nThis is now a PF/ruleset repair task.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nUse private SSH 10.90.20.10 -> 10.90.10.1.\nDo not expose SSH/WebGUI on WAN.\nDo not regenerate WireGuard keys.\nDo not change Android configuration.\nDo not change NAT or DNS unless proven necessary later.\nDo not start PATCH-NET-005.\n\n1. BACKUP\n\nBefore modification:\n- backup /conf/config.xml with timestamp\n- backup current generated firewall configuration where appropriate\n- record current pfctl status\n- record current private SSH session\n- prepare rollback\n\n2. DIAGNOSE PF\n\nRun read-only diagnostics first:\n\npfctl -s info\npfctl -sr\npfctl -sn\npfctl -sa\n\nValidate generated ruleset WITHOUT loading it:\n\npfctl -nf /tmp/rules.debug\n\nCapture the exact syntax error, line number and offending generated rule.\n\nTrace that generated rule back to the corresponding OPNsense\nconfig.xml firewall/NAT entry.\n\nDo not manually patch /tmp/rules.debug as the permanent fix.\n\n3. REPAIR SOURCE CONFIG\n\nRepair only the malformed OPNsense config entry/entries responsible\nfor invalid PF syntax.\n\nPreserve:\n- private SSH from 10.90.20.10/32\n- private HTTPS\n- WireGuard UDP/51821 WAN allow\n- worm-os isolation\n- outbound NAT 10.60.0.0/24 -> WAN\n- DNS policy\n- WAN TCP 22/80/443 deny\n- ADMIN isolation\n\nDo not introduce broad PASS rules.\n\n4. VALIDATE BEFORE LOAD\n\nRegenerate firewall configuration using the proper OPNsense mechanism.\n\nThen:\n\npfctl -nf /tmp/rules.debug\n\nMUST return success before any live reload.\n\nIf syntax validation fails:\nSTOP.\nDo not reload PF.\n\n5. SAFE RELOAD\n\nOnly after syntax validation succeeds, reload using the normal OPNsense\nfirewall/config mechanism.\n\nImmediately verify:\n\npfctl -s info\npfctl -sr\npfctl -sn\n\nConfirm PF is enabled and rules are actually loaded.\n\n6. MANAGEMENT SAFETY\n\nImmediately verify from worm-robot:\n\n10.90.10.1:22 private SSH = OPEN\n10.90.10.1:443 private HTTPS = HTTP 200\n\nVerify WAN:\n\n2.28.34.27:22 CLOSED/FILTERED\n2.28.34.27:80 CLOSED/FILTERED\n2.28.34.27:443 CLOSED/FILTERED\n\nIf private management is lost or WAN management becomes exposed:\nROLL BACK immediately.\n\n7. WIREGUARD RULE\n\nConfirm loaded PF rules contain an effective rule equivalent to:\n\nUDP any -> WAN address port 51821 PASS\n\nDo not rely only on config.xml or /tmp/rules.debug.\nVerify the LIVE PF ruleset.\n\n8. LIVE TEST\n\nWhen ready print exactly:\n\nPF FIXED - ENABLE ANDROID WIREGUARD NOW\n\nThen monitor for 90 seconds:\n\ntcpdump WAN UDP/51821\nwg show wg0\nPF rule counters/logs\n\nExpected:\n\nWAN packets > 0\nWG handshake RECENT\nWG RX > 0\nWG TX > 0\n\n9. ONLY AFTER HANDSHAKE\n\nOnly after WireGuard handshake succeeds continue testing:\n- DNS 10.60.0.1\n- NAT\n- Internet\n- public IPv4 2.28.34.27\n- private 10.90.0.0/16\n- ADMIN isolation\n- IPv6 leak protection\n\n10. OUTPUT\n\nUpdate PATCH-NET-004 documentation.\n\nReport:\n\nPF_SYNTAX=PASS/FAIL\nPF_ENABLED=YES/NO\nPF_RULES_LOADED=YES/NO\nPRIVATE_SSH=PASS/FAIL\nPRIVATE_HTTPS=PASS/FAIL\nWAN_22_80_443=CLOSED/FAIL\nWG_51821_RULE_LOADED=YES/NO\nWG_HANDSHAKE=RECENT/NEVER\n\nDo not mark PATCH-NET-004 PASS until the real Android full-tunnel test\npasses.\n\nNever print private keys, passwords, PSKs or tokens."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0783f-9e3e-7ee1-950d-227eeb005287","create_time":1788723830.3622594,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07858-3cf6-7170-8e1f-ccb5093a67ce","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-NET-005-DISABLE-PUBLIC-WEB.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nThe network architecture is FROZEN.\nDo not redesign addressing, routing, WireGuard, OPNsense or vSwitch.\n\nFROZEN TOPOLOGY:\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nworm-os PUBLIC:\n2.28.34.27\n\nOPNsense:\n- dedicated to worm-os WireGuard/privacy/egress\n- WG: 10.60.0.1\n- PRIVATE: 10.90.10.1\n\nworm-os:\n10.60.0.x\n\nHetzner private network:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\n==================================================\nGOAL\n==================================================\n\nDisable ALL public Worm web distribution on worm-robot.\n\nPUBLIC INTERNET must NOT be able to access:\n\n- Worm Web Installer\n- WebUSB installer\n- image.zip\n- OTA metadata\n- OTA payloads\n- OTA archives\n- Worm OS downloads\n- Apps downloads\n- private Git\n- backup endpoints\n- internal Worm APIs\n\nOTA/download infrastructure must remain available ONLY through the\nprivate Worm network according to later/private service policy.\n\nDo NOT delete release artifacts.\nDo NOT delete OTA files.\nDo NOT destroy Web Installer source code.\nDisable PUBLIC delivery only.\n\n==================================================\n1. AUDIT FIRST\n==================================================\n\nAudit worm-robot for all web exposure.\n\nInspect:\n\n- Caddy active configuration\n- Caddy imports/includes\n- systemd Caddy unit\n- web roots\n- reverse proxies\n- ports 80/443\n- QUIC/HTTP3\n- Web Installer deployment\n- WebUSB deployment\n- OTA endpoints\n- download directories\n- Apps endpoints\n- Git HTTP endpoints\n- backup endpoints\n- internal APIs\n\nSearch for:\n\nworm.coffee.pm\nworm.estixari.com\nimage.zip\nmetadata\nota\nupdates\ndownload\ninstaller\nwebusb\napps\nbackup\ngit\n\nAlso inspect:\n\nss -lntup\nCaddy effective configuration\nFireHOL effective rules\n\nDocument every currently public HTTP/HTTPS endpoint before changing it.\n\n==================================================\n2. PUBLIC WEB POLICY\n==================================================\n\nTarget on worm-robot PUBLIC interface 162.55.6.173:\n\nTCP 80:\nDENY\n\nTCP 443:\nDENY\n\nUDP 443 / QUIC:\nDENY\n\nNo Worm website, Web Installer, OTA or download service may be\nreachable through the public interface.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo not rely only on removing DNS.\n\nThe service itself must not be publicly reachable by:\n- hostname\n- direct IPv4\n- IPv6\n- alternate Host header\n- HTTP\n- HTTPS\n- HTTP/3\n\n==================================================\n3. CADDY\n==================================================\n\nDo not uninstall Caddy if it is required for private Worm services.\n\nReconfigure Caddy so Worm services bind ONLY to the intended private\ninterface/address where technically appropriate.\n\nPreferred private bind:\n\n10.90.20.10\n\nDo not bind private Worm services to:\n\n0.0.0.0\n[::]\n162.55.6.173\n\nAudit each site/reverse proxy individually.\n\nIf a service cannot safely bind only to 10.90.20.10:\nkeep FireHOL as a second enforcement layer and document the limitation.\n\nValidate configuration before reload:\n\ncaddy validate\n\nNever reload an invalid configuration.\n\n==================================================\n4. WEBUSB / WEB INSTALLER\n==================================================\n\nPublic WebUSB/Web Installer must be DISABLED.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- no public Web Installer page\n- no public WebUSB flashing UI\n- no public release archive fetch\n- no public image.zip\n- no public flashing metadata\n\nDo NOT delete source repositories.\n\nKeep source/build infrastructure intact for controlled administrative\nor future private provisioning.\n\nDocument how to re-enable it deliberately if ever required.\n\n==================================================\n5. OTA\n==================================================\n\nPUBLIC OTA = DENY.\n\nPublic Internet must not retrieve:\n\n- OTA metadata\n- update metadata\n- payloads\n- image.zip\n- release archives\n- update manifests\n\nDo not delete OTA artifacts.\n\nPrivate target path:\n\nworm-os 10.60.0.x\n        |\n        | WireGuard\n        v\nOPNsense\n        |\n        | private routing\n        v\nworm-robot 10.90.20.10\n\nOTA will be allowed only through this private path.\n\nDo not implement Android OTA migration in this patch.\nOnly enforce public denial and prepare/document private availability.\n\n==================================================\n6. FIREHOL\n==================================================\n\nFireHOL remains REQUIRED on worm-robot.\n\nRole:\n- host firewall\n- SSH policy\n- blocklists\n- public default deny\n\nTarget PUBLIC interface:\n\nALLOW:\n- SSH TCP/22 according to existing administrative policy\n- ADMIN WireGuard UDP/51820 only if still required\n- established/related\n\nDENY:\n- TCP 80\n- TCP 443\n- UDP 443\n- all Worm web/download/OTA services\n\nPreserve existing SSH access.\n\nPreserve existing ADMIN WireGuard.\n\nDo not enable IP forwarding.\n\nnet.ipv4.ip_forward must remain 0.\n\n==================================================\n7. BLOCKLIST\n==================================================\n\nPreserve the FireHOL blocklist design.\n\nDo not remove blocklists.\n\nDo not introduce untrusted/random blocklist sources.\n\nVerify blocklist policy still applies to PUBLIC ingress before exposed\nadministrative services where appropriate.\n\n==================================================\n8. IPV6\n==================================================\n\nCRITICAL:\n\nPublic web must also be disabled over IPv6.\n\nAudit worm-robot public IPv6.\n\nVerify no:\n\nTCP 80\nTCP 443\nUDP 443\n\nis publicly reachable through IPv6.\n\nDo not create an IPv6 bypass around the IPv4 policy.\n\n==================================================\n9. PRIVATE SERVICES\n==================================================\n\nPrivate interface:\n\n10.90.20.10\n\nPrivate services may remain listening only when required for:\n\n- OTA\n- Worm downloads\n- Apps\n- private APIs\n- future backup\n- controlled Git\n\nDo NOT allow worm-os access to:\n\n- SSH\n- ADMIN network 10.77.0.0/24\n- server dashboard\n- databases\n- management APIs\n- root/admin services\n\nPrivate does NOT mean trusted-any.\n\n==================================================\n10. OPNsense\n==================================================\n\nDo not redesign or repurpose OPNsense.\n\nOPNsense remains:\n\nworm-os PUBLIC:\n2.28.34.27\n\nWireGuard:\n10.60.0.1\n\nPRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nOPNsense role:\nONLY worm-os WireGuard/privacy/NAT/firewall/routing.\n\nDo not move Worm Web Installer or OTA hosting onto OPNsense.\n\n==================================================\n11. PUBLIC EXPOSURE TESTS\n==================================================\n\nFrom an external/public perspective verify worm-robot:\n\n162.55.6.173:80\nCLOSED/FILTERED\n\n162.55.6.173:443\nCLOSED/FILTERED\n\n162.55.6.173 UDP/443\nCLOSED/FILTERED\n\nVerify IPv6 equivalents.\n\nTest known public hostnames.\n\nExpected:\nno Worm web content served publicly.\n\nDirect-IP + Host-header tests must not bypass the restriction.\n\n==================================================\n12. PRIVATE TESTS\n==================================================\n\nFrom the authorized private infrastructure verify:\n\n10.90.20.10\n\nRequired private services still work where intentionally retained.\n\nFor OTA/download endpoints verify:\n\nPUBLIC = DENY\nPRIVATE = AVAILABLE only where explicitly configured\n\nDo not mark PRIVATE PASS if no actual test was performed.\n\n==================================================\n13. REGRESSION\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\nPASS worm-robot public SSH remains available according to policy\nPASS ADMIN WireGuard remains unchanged\nPASS FireHOL active\nPASS blocklist policy preserved\nPASS ip_forward = 0\nPASS worm-robot private IP = 10.90.20.10\nPASS OPNsense private = 10.90.10.1\nPASS OPNsense WG = 10.60.0.1\nPASS OPNsense WAN = 2.28.34.27\nPASS frozen network topology unchanged\n\n==================================================\n14. ROLLBACK\n==================================================\n\nBefore modifying:\n\n- backup Caddy configuration\n- backup FireHOL configuration\n- record listening sockets\n- record effective firewall state\n\nProvide exact rollback.\n\nDo not delete previous working configuration.\n\nDo not reboot worm-robot automatically.\n\n==================================================\n15. SECURITY\n==================================================\n\nSecret scan all generated patch/report files.\n\nNever include:\n\n- WireGuard private keys\n- PSKs\n- Hetzner API token\n- passwords\n- SSH private keys\n- signing private keys\n- backup encryption keys\n\n==================================================\n16. PATCH OUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nPATCH-NET-005-DISABLE-PUBLIC-WEB/\n\nInclude:\n\nREADME.md\nARCHITECTURE.md\nPUBLIC-POLICY.md\nCADDY.md\nFIREHOL.md\nWEBUSB.md\nOTA.md\nIPV6.md\nPRIVATE-SERVICES.md\nTESTS.md\nROLLBACK.md\nmodified-files.txt\ntest-results.txt\n\nDependencies:\n\nPATCH-NET-001-OPNSENSE-PRIVATE-IP\nPATCH-NET-002-WORM-ROBOT-PRIVATE-IP\nPATCH-NET-003-WORM-OS-PRIVATE-IP\nPATCH-NET-004-WORM-OS-PUBLIC-EGRESS\n\n==================================================\n17. PASS CRITERIA\n==================================================\n\nDo not mark PASS unless:\n\nPASS public Web Installer disabled\nPASS public WebUSB disabled\nPASS public image.zip denied\nPASS public OTA metadata denied\nPASS public OTA payload denied\nPASS public Worm downloads denied\nPASS public TCP/80 denied\nPASS public TCP/443 denied\nPASS public UDP/443 denied\nPASS IPv6 public web denied\nPASS direct-IP bypass denied\nPASS Host-header bypass denied\nPASS FireHOL active\nPASS SSH policy preserved\nPASS blocklist policy preserved\nPASS ADMIN WG preserved\nPASS ip_forward=0\nPASS private IP 10.90.20.10 preserved\nPASS OPNsense topology unchanged\nPASS no secrets\n\nPrivate OTA/download validation may be PENDING if the corresponding\nprivate service migration has not yet been implemented.\n\n==================================================\nFINAL OUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nPrint:\n\nPATCH-NET-005-DISABLE-PUBLIC-WEB STATUS:\nPASS / PARTIAL / BLOCKED\n\nPUBLIC WEB:\nDISABLED / FAIL\n\nPUBLIC WEBUSB:\nDISABLED / FAIL\n\nPUBLIC OTA:\nDISABLED / FAIL\n\nPUBLIC DOWNLOADS:\nDISABLED / FAIL\n\nPRIVATE OTA:\nAVAILABLE / PENDING / FAIL\n\nFIREHOL:\nACTIVE / FAIL\n\nSSH:\nPRESERVED / FAIL\n\nBLOCKLIST:\nPRESERVED / FAIL\n\nIPV6 WEB:\nBLOCKED / FAIL\n\nFROZEN TOPOLOGY:\nUNCHANGED / FAIL\n\nDo not start another patch automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07858-3cdb-7a22-9138-5686d1b1b02f","create_time":1788725443.830993,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07862-0aee-7071-97eb-583031b524bf","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-NET-006-WORM-PRIVATE-OTA-DOWNLOADS.\n\nPRECONDITIONS:\n- PATCH-NET-001-OPNSENSE-PRIVATE-IP: PASS\n- PATCH-NET-002-WORM-ROBOT-PRIVATE-IP: PASS\n- PATCH-NET-003-WORM-OS-PRIVATE-IP: PASS\n- PATCH-NET-004-WORM-OS-PUBLIC-EGRESS: PASS or client-validated\n- PATCH-NET-005-DISABLE-PUBLIC-WEB: PASS\n\nNETWORK TOPOLOGY IS FROZEN.\nDO NOT redesign addressing or roles.\n\n==================================================\nFROZEN TOPOLOGY\n==================================================\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nOPNsense PUBLIC / worm-os egress:\n2.28.34.27\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nOPNsense WireGuard:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nworm-os devices:\n10.60.0.x/32\n\nHetzner private backbone:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nADMIN WG:\n10.77.0.0/24\n\n==================================================\nGOAL\n==================================================\n\nMove Worm OS distribution to PRIVATE NETWORK ONLY.\n\nworm-os path:\n\nworm-os 10.60.0.x\n        |\n        | WireGuard\n        v\nOPNsense 10.60.0.1\n        |\n        | private routing\n        v\nworm-robot 10.90.20.10\n\nPrivate services:\n\n- OTA metadata\n- OTA payloads\n- image.zip\n- Worm OS downloads\n- release metadata\n- Apps metadata/downloads where applicable\n\nPUBLIC INTERNET MUST NOT receive these artifacts.\n\n==================================================\n1. AUDIT FIRST\n==================================================\n\nLocate current production endpoints and files for:\n\n- image.zip\n- OTA metadata\n- OTA payload\n- release archives\n- update manifests\n- apps metadata\n- apps packages\n- download directories\n\nAudit:\n- Caddy\n- filesystem roots\n- symlinks\n- reverse proxies\n- updater URLs\n- Apps URLs\n- hardcoded public URLs\n- release scripts\n- publishing scripts\n\nDo not delete anything.\n\n==================================================\n2. PRIVATE BINDING\n==================================================\n\nPreferred serving address:\n\n10.90.20.10\n\nPrivate Worm services must not bind to:\n\n0.0.0.0\n[::]\n162.55.6.173\n\nwhere practical.\n\nIf binding cannot be restricted safely:\nuse FireHOL as the second enforcement layer and document it.\n\n==================================================\n3. PRIVATE HOSTNAME / DNS\n==================================================\n\nPrepare private resolution for:\n\nworm.coffee.pm\n\nTarget for worm-os private access:\n\nworm.coffee.pm -> 10.90.20.10\n\nThis resolution must be available to worm-os through the protected\nnetwork path.\n\nDo NOT expose private DNS records publicly if avoidable.\n\nDo not break external DNS unexpectedly.\n\nIf split-horizon DNS is used, document it.\n\n==================================================\n4. OTA PRIVATE ENDPOINTS\n==================================================\n\nPrivate OTA endpoint should be reachable only from worm-os path.\n\nExamples:\n\nhttps://worm.coffee.pm/updates/\nhttps://worm.coffee.pm/worm-os/\n\nPrivate content includes:\n- metadata\n- payloads\n- image.zip\n- release archives\n\nPUBLIC:\nDENY\n\nWORM-OS:\nALLOW\n\n==================================================\n5. APPS PRIVATE ENDPOINT\n==================================================\n\nPrepare:\n\nhttps://worm.coffee.pm/apps/\n\nOnly if Apps migration is in scope and existing app client can consume it.\n\nDo not break production GrapheneOS/Worm Apps unexpectedly.\n\nIf Apps migration requires Android client changes:\ndocument dependency and leave client switch PENDING.\n\n==================================================\n6. FIREHOL\n==================================================\n\nFireHOL remains active on worm-robot.\n\nPUBLIC enp9s0:\n- SSH/admin policy only\n- no OTA\n- no apps\n- no downloads\n- no Web Installer\n- no private APIs\n\nPRIVATE enp9s0.4000:\nallow only explicitly required service ports.\n\nFor worm-os sourced traffic routed through OPNsense:\nallow only required destinations/services.\n\nExplicitly deny worm-os access to:\n- SSH\n- admin dashboard\n- databases\n- internal management APIs\n- 10.77.0.0/24\n\nDo not create a trusted-any private interface.\n\n==================================================\n7. CADDY\n==================================================\n\nConfigure private sites/listeners safely.\n\nRequirements:\n- no public listener for Worm distribution\n- no directory listing\n- no arbitrary filesystem browsing\n- no .git exposure\n- no config-file exposure\n- no backup-file exposure\n- correct MIME types\n- HTTPS only where practical\n- validate before reload\n\nRun:\ncaddy validate\n\nDo not reload invalid config.\n\n==================================================\n8. OTA INTEGRITY\n==================================================\n\nWireGuard is transport security, not update authenticity.\n\nPreserve or strengthen:\n- OTA signature verification\n- metadata verification\n- hash verification\n- anti-downgrade\n- rollback protection\n\nDo not weaken GrapheneOS/AOSP OTA verification.\n\n==================================================\n9. WORM-OS CLIENT POLICY\n==================================================\n\nDo not implement broad public fallback.\n\nWorm OS OTA/download clients must use the protected private endpoint.\n\nRequired behavior:\n\nWireGuard AVAILABLE:\nprivate OTA/download works\n\nWireGuard UNAVAILABLE:\nprivate OTA/download FAILS CLOSED\n\nDo not retry over:\n162.55.6.173\npublic mirrors\nHTTP\nalternate public URLs\n\nIf Android client changes are required:\ndocument dependency on PATCH-WORM-OS-SYSTEM-WIREGUARD-V1.\n\n==================================================\n10. TESTS\n==================================================\n\nPUBLIC TESTS:\n- 162.55.6.173:80 denied\n- 162.55.6.173:443 denied for Worm distribution\n- public hostname cannot retrieve image.zip\n- public hostname cannot retrieve OTA metadata\n- public hostname cannot retrieve OTA payload\n- public hostname cannot retrieve apps private files\n\nPRIVATE TESTS:\nfrom authorized private path:\n- 10.90.20.10 private HTTPS works\n- image.zip retrievable\n- OTA metadata retrievable\n- OTA payload retrievable\n- hashes/signatures verify\n\nWORM-OS TEST:\nthrough WireGuard 10.60.0.x:\n- private hostname resolves correctly\n- OTA/download succeeds\n- SSH worm-robot denied\n- ADMIN 10.77.0.0/24 denied\n- OPNsense management denied\n\nFAIL-CLOSED TEST:\n- disable WireGuard\n- verify Worm OTA/download does NOT fall back publicly\n\n==================================================\n11. IPV6\n==================================================\n\nVerify no IPv6 public bypass.\n\nPublic IPv6 must not expose:\n- OTA\n- downloads\n- apps private content\n- image.zip\n\nDocument IPv6 private-routing status.\n\n==================================================\n12. PUBLIC / PRIVATE SEPARATION\n==================================================\n\nMust remain true:\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n= admin/recovery only according to FireHOL policy\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n= Worm service plane\n\nOPNsense:\n2.28.34.27\n= worm-os privacy egress only\n\nDo not move application hosting onto OPNsense.\n\n==================================================\n13. PATCH OUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nPATCH-NET-006-WORM-PRIVATE-OTA-DOWNLOADS/\n\nInclude:\n\nREADME.md\nARCHITECTURE.md\nPRIVATE-DNS.md\nCADDY.md\nFIREHOL.md\nOTA.md\nAPPS.md\nFAIL-CLOSED.md\nSECURITY.md\nIPV6.md\nTESTS.md\nROLLBACK.md\nmodified-files.txt\ntest-results.txt\n\nDependencies:\n\nPATCH-NET-005-DISABLE-PUBLIC-WEB\nPATCH-WORM-OS-SYSTEM-WIREGUARD-V1\nwhere applicable.\n\n==================================================\n14. PASS CRITERIA\n==================================================\n\nPASS public image.zip denied\nPASS public OTA metadata denied\nPASS public OTA payload denied\nPASS public Worm downloads denied\nPASS public IPv6 bypass denied\nPASS private 10.90.20.10 serving works\nPASS worm-os WireGuard private access works\nPASS OTA integrity verification unchanged\nPASS no public fallback\nPASS worm-robot SSH denied from worm-os\nPASS ADMIN network denied from worm-os\nPASS FireHOL active\nPASS frozen topology unchanged\nPASS no secrets\n\nIf Android client migration is not yet applied:\nmark CLIENT INTEGRATION PENDING,\nbut server-side private distribution may still PASS.\n\n==================================================\nFINAL OUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nPATCH-NET-006 STATUS:\nPASS / PARTIAL / BLOCKED\n\nPUBLIC OTA:\nDENIED / FAIL\n\nPRIVATE OTA:\nAVAILABLE / FAIL\n\nPUBLIC DOWNLOADS:\nDENIED / FAIL\n\nPRIVATE DOWNLOADS:\nAVAILABLE / FAIL\n\nPRIVATE DNS:\nREADY / PENDING / FAIL\n\nFAIL-CLOSED:\nPASS / PENDING / FAIL\n\nFROZEN TOPOLOGY:\nUNCHANGED / FAIL\n\nDo not start another patch automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07862-0ad0-7ba1-b4c8-ce69e4937e12","create_time":1788726086.382277,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07879-1b3f-7c21-b41f-b44c4730d006","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Perform WORM PRE-CHECK AUDIT against the FROZEN architecture.\n\nAUDIT ONLY.\nDO NOT modify configuration.\nDO NOT restart services.\nDO NOT reload FireHOL.\nDO NOT reboot.\nDO NOT change WireGuard keys.\nDO NOT change OPNsense.\nDO NOT change routing.\nDO NOT change DNS.\nDO NOT change Caddy.\n\n==================================================\nFROZEN ARCHITECTURE\n==================================================\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nOPNsense PUBLIC / worm-os egress:\n2.28.34.27\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nOPNsense WireGuard:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nworm-os peers:\n10.60.0.x/32\n\nHetzner private backbone:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nADMIN WireGuard:\n10.77.0.0/24\n\nvSwitch:\n#85469\n\nVLAN:\n4000\n\n==================================================\nFROZEN RESPONSIBILITIES\n==================================================\n\nOPNsense:\n- WireGuard worm-os\n- worm-os firewall\n- worm-os NAT\n- worm-os public egress\n- private routing\n- public egress IP = 2.28.34.27\n\nworm-robot:\n- Worm private services\n- private IP 10.90.20.10\n- dedicated public IP 162.55.6.173\n- FireHOL host firewall\n- SSH/admin policy\n- blocklists\n\nworm-os:\n- WireGuard required\n- private identity 10.60.0.x/32\n- public IPv4 through OPNsense\n- expected public IPv4 = 2.28.34.27\n\n==================================================\nPUBLIC POLICY\n==================================================\n\nworm-robot public:\n\nALLOW only according to existing admin policy:\n- SSH\n- ADMIN WireGuard if still required\n\nDENY public:\n- Web Installer\n- WebUSB\n- image.zip\n- OTA metadata\n- OTA payloads\n- Worm downloads\n- Apps private distribution\n- Backup\n- private APIs\n\nOPNsense WAN:\n\nALLOW:\nUDP/51821 WireGuard\n\nDENY:\nTCP/22\nTCP/80\nTCP/443\n\n==================================================\nOPNSENSE ACCESS FROM WORM-OS\n==================================================\n\nThrough WireGuard only:\n\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.60.0.1:22   ALLOW\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.60.0.1:80   ALLOW\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.60.0.1:443  ALLOW\n\nPublic WAN management remains DENIED.\n\n==================================================\nPRIVATE DISTRIBUTION\n==================================================\n\nExpected path:\n\nworm-os\n10.60.0.x\n   |\nWireGuard\n   |\nOPNsense\n10.60.0.1\n   |\nprivate routing\n   |\nworm-robot\n10.90.20.10\n\nPrivate-only services:\n- OTA metadata\n- OTA payload\n- image.zip\n- Worm downloads\n- Apps\n- Backup\n\n==================================================\n1. HOST / NETWORK PRE-CHECK\n==================================================\n\nVerify worm-robot:\n\n- enp9s0 present\n- enp9s0.4000 UP\n- VLAN ID 4000\n- MTU 1400\n- private IP 10.90.20.10/24\n- public IP 162.55.6.173\n- default route unchanged\n- route 10.90.0.0/16 via private path\n- wg0 ADMIN still present\n- net.ipv4.ip_forward = 0\n\n==================================================\n2. OPNsense PRE-CHECK\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\n- server running\n- WAN = 2.28.34.27\n- PRIVATE = 10.90.10.1\n- WG = 10.60.0.1/24\n- UDP 51821 listening\n- real worm-os peer exists\n- peer AllowedIP is /32\n- latest handshake state\n- RX/TX counters\n\nNever print private keys.\n\n==================================================\n3. PUBLIC EXPOSURE PRE-CHECK\n==================================================\n\nVerify from public side where possible:\n\nworm-robot:\n162.55.6.173:22 -> according to SSH policy\n162.55.6.173:80 -> CLOSED/FILTERED\n162.55.6.173:443 -> CLOSED/FILTERED\nUDP/443 -> CLOSED/FILTERED\n\nOPNsense:\n2.28.34.27:22 -> CLOSED/FILTERED\n2.28.34.27:80 -> CLOSED/FILTERED\n2.28.34.27:443 -> CLOSED/FILTERED\nUDP/51821 -> EXPECTED OPEN\n\nCheck IPv6 equivalents.\n\n==================================================\n4. FIREHOL PRE-CHECK\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\n- installed\n- enabled\n- active\n- syntax valid\n- SSH policy intact\n- blocklist configuration intact\n- blocklist refresh mechanism\n- no broad public allow\n- no trusted-any PRIVATE rule\n- no broad forwarding\n\nDo NOT reload FireHOL.\n\n==================================================\n5. PRIVATE CONNECTIVITY\n==================================================\n\nFrom worm-robot verify:\n\n10.90.10.1 reachable\nHTTPS private OPNsense reachable\n\nVerify source selection:\n10.90.20.10\n\n==================================================\n6. WORM-OS POLICY\n==================================================\n\nIf a live worm-os test peer is connected, verify:\n\n- handshake recent\n- public egress IPv4 = 2.28.34.27\n- DNS works\n- private 10.90.0.0/16 routing works\n- 10.60.0.1:22 reachable\n- 10.60.0.1:80 reachable\n- 10.60.0.1:443 reachable\n- public WAN 22/80/443 still denied\n- no IPv6 leak\n- no fallback outside WireGuard for Worm services\n\nIf no live peer:\nmark these tests PENDING.\nDo not fake PASS.\n\n==================================================\n7. PUBLIC WEB / OTA CHECK\n==================================================\n\nVerify PUBLIC DENY for:\n\n- Web Installer\n- WebUSB\n- image.zip\n- OTA metadata\n- OTA payload\n- Worm downloads\n\nVerify PRIVATE availability only where already implemented.\n\n==================================================\n8. LISTENERS\n==================================================\n\nClassify all listening sockets on worm-robot:\n\nPUBLIC_REQUIRED\nPRIVATE_REQUIRED\nWG_ADMIN\nLOOPBACK\nUNEXPECTED\n\nFlag wildcard listeners:\n0.0.0.0\n[::]\n\n==================================================\n9. FIREWALL / NAT RESIDUE\n==================================================\n\nAudit:\n- iptables\n- nftables\n- NAT\n- FORWARD\n\nFlag stale troubleshooting rules.\n\nDo not delete them.\n\n==================================================\n10. SECRETS\n==================================================\n\nScan relevant patch/config documentation for:\n\n- PrivateKey\n- PresharedKey\n- passwords\n- Hetzner token\n- SSH private keys\n- signing keys\n- backup encryption keys\n\nNever print secret values.\n\n==================================================\n11. PATCH STATUS\n==================================================\n\nAudit status of:\n\nPATCH-NET-001-OPNSENSE-PRIVATE-IP\nPATCH-NET-002-WORM-ROBOT-PRIVATE-IP\nPATCH-NET-003-WORM-OS-PRIVATE-IP\nPATCH-NET-004-WORM-OS-PUBLIC-EGRESS\nPATCH-NET-005-DISABLE-PUBLIC-WEB\nPATCH-NET-006-WORM-PRIVATE-OTA-DOWNLOADS\nPATCH-WORM-OS-SYSTEM-WIREGUARD-V1\n\nReport:\nPASS\nPARTIAL\nPENDING\nFAIL\n\nfor each existing patch.\n\n==================================================\n12. OUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\n/root/WORM-PRECHECK-FROZEN/\n\nFiles:\n\n00-SUMMARY.md\n01-FROZEN-TOPOLOGY.md\n02-WORM-ROBOT.md\n03-OPNSENSE.md\n04-WIREGUARD.md\n05-FIREHOL.md\n06-PUBLIC-EXPOSURE.md\n07-PRIVATE-SERVICES.md\n08-IPV6.md\n09-FORWARDING-NAT.md\n10-PATCH-STATUS.md\n11-SECRET-SCAN.md\n12-FINDINGS.md\n13-GATE.md\n\nAlso:\nfindings.json\n\n==================================================\n13. PASS GATE\n==================================================\n\nFinal output must be EXACTLY one of:\n\nWORM PRE-CHECK: PASS\n\nor\n\nWORM PRE-CHECK: FAIL\n\nPASS requires:\n\nPASS frozen topology unchanged\nPASS worm-robot public IP\nPASS worm-robot private IP\nPASS OPNsense public IP\nPASS OPNsense private IP\nPASS OPNsense WG address\nPASS FireHOL active\nPASS ip_forward=0 on worm-robot\nPASS public Web disabled\nPASS public OTA/download denied\nPASS OPNsense WAN 22/80/443 denied\nPASS UDP/51821 available\nPASS private routing healthy\nPASS ADMIN network unchanged\nPASS no critical secret leak\n\nClient-dependent checks may be PENDING only if no live client exists.\n\nIf FAIL:\nprint exact P0/P1 blockers.\n\nDo not remediate automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07879-1b24-70c0-b281-8a3ace71c7b2","create_time":1788727597.887731,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a0787f-afc5-7011-82ef-72fe1db9b75e","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-NET-004B-OPNSENSE-WORMOS-MANAGEMENT.\n\nThis is remediation for:\n\nP1-OPN-WORMOS-MGMT-001\n\nFROZEN POLICY:\n\nworm-os network:\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nOPNsense WireGuard:\n10.60.0.1\n\nALLOW through WireGuard ONLY:\n\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.60.0.1 TCP/22\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.60.0.1 TCP/80\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.60.0.1 TCP/443\n\nPUBLIC WAN MUST REMAIN:\n\n2.28.34.27:22  DENY\n2.28.34.27:80  DENY\n2.28.34.27:443 DENY\n\nUDP/51821 remains ALLOW for WireGuard.\n\nDo not change frozen addressing.\nDo not change WireGuard keys.\nDo not change NAT.\nDo not change worm-robot.\nDo not change FireHOL.\n\nFirst audit the current OPNsense management service bindings and PF rules.\n\nConfigure OPNsense management services so the required services are\nactually reachable on the WireGuard address 10.60.0.1.\n\nDo not bind management services to the WAN.\n\nPrefer explicit interface/address bindings rather than wildcard\nlisteners.\n\nAdd the minimum PF rules required on the worm-os WireGuard interface.\n\nSource MUST be:\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nDestination MUST be:\n10.60.0.1\n\nPorts:\nTCP 22\nTCP 80\nTCP 443\n\nDo not create:\n10.60.0.0/24 -> any management allow\n\nKeep management restricted to the OPNsense WireGuard address.\n\nSECURITY:\n\n- WAN management remains inaccessible.\n- IPv6 WAN management remains inaccessible.\n- No access to worm-robot SSH is added.\n- No access to ADMIN 10.77.0.0/24 is added.\n- No broad trusted-any rule.\n- Preserve private SSH recovery from worm-robot 10.90.20.10.\n- Preserve UDP/51821.\n- Preserve full-tunnel NAT.\n\nAfter applying, test from the REAL worm-os WireGuard client:\n\n10.60.0.1:22  REACHABLE\n10.60.0.1:80  REACHABLE\n10.60.0.1:443 REACHABLE\n\nFor HTTPS verify an actual OPNsense response, not merely TCP connect.\n\nThen test from public side:\n\n2.28.34.27:22  CLOSED/FILTERED\n2.28.34.27:80  CLOSED/FILTERED\n2.28.34.27:443 CLOSED/FILTERED\n\nVerify:\n\nWireGuard handshake remains recent\nInternet egress remains 2.28.34.27\nDNS remains functional\nUDP/51821 remains operational\n10.90.10.1 private management remains operational\nworm-robot FireHOL unchanged\nworm-robot ip_forward=0\n\nCreate:\n\nPATCH-NET-004B-OPNSENSE-WORMOS-MANAGEMENT/\n\nREADME.md\nSECURITY.md\nFIREWALL.md\nTESTS.md\nROLLBACK.md\nmodified-files.txt\ntest-results.txt\n\nPASS requires:\n\nPASS 10.60.0.1:22 from worm-os\nPASS 10.60.0.1:80 from worm-os\nPASS 10.60.0.1:443 from worm-os\nPASS 2.28.34.27:22 denied\nPASS 2.28.34.27:80 denied\nPASS 2.28.34.27:443 denied\nPASS UDP/51821\nPASS WireGuard handshake\nPASS NAT/public egress unchanged\nPASS frozen topology unchanged\nPASS no secrets\n\nAfter successful remediation rerun:\n\nWORM PRE-CHECK AUDIT\n\nExpected final gate:\n\nWORM PRE-CHECK: PASS\n\nDo not start another patch automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0787f-afab-7ed3-886b-e8fd47d75637","create_time":1788728029.1252968,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a0788c-2293-78c2-a93c-82ebeea9ec74","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-NET-007-PRIVATE-NETWORK-HARDENING.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nThe frozen Worm network architecture MUST NOT change.\n\nFROZEN TOPOLOGY:\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nOPNsense PUBLIC / worm-os egress:\n2.28.34.27\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nOPNsense WireGuard:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nworm-os:\n10.60.0.x/32\n\nHetzner private backbone:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nADMIN WireGuard:\n10.77.0.0/24\n\nvSwitch:\n85469\n\nVLAN:\n4000\n\n==================================================\nGOAL\n==================================================\n\nComplete and harden all PRIVATE IP networking.\n\nDo NOT redesign addresses.\nDo NOT migrate public IPs.\nDo NOT remove OPNsense.\nDo NOT repurpose worm-robot.\nDo NOT enable broad forwarding.\n\n==================================================\n1. OPNsense MANAGEMENT ON WORM-OS WG\n==================================================\n\nRequired policy:\n\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.60.0.1 TCP/22  ALLOW\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.60.0.1 TCP/80  ALLOW\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.60.0.1 TCP/443 ALLOW\n\nWAN remains:\n\n2.28.34.27:22  DENY\n2.28.34.27:80  DENY\n2.28.34.27:443 DENY\n\nWireGuard:\nUDP/51821 ALLOW\n\nPrefer management services bound to private/WG addresses only.\n\n==================================================\n2. PRIVATE BACKBONE\n==================================================\n\nVerify and harden:\n\nOPNsense:\n10.90.10.1\n\nworm-robot:\n10.90.20.10\n\nNetwork:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nworm-robot VLAN:\nenp9s0.4000\nVLAN 4000\nMTU 1400\n\nExpected route:\n\n10.90.0.0/16 via 10.90.20.1 dev enp9s0.4000 onlink\n\nVerify deterministic source selection:\n10.90.20.10\n\nNo duplicate routes.\nNo stale routes.\n\n==================================================\n3. WORM-OS ADDRESSING\n==================================================\n\nNetwork:\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nOPNsense:\n10.60.0.1\n\nEach worm-os device:\nunique /32\n\nExample:\n10.60.0.10/32\n10.60.0.11/32\n10.60.0.12/32\n\nEach device MUST have:\n- unique keypair\n- unique AllowedIP\n- independent revocation\n\nNo shared private keys.\n\n==================================================\n4. FULL-TUNNEL\n==================================================\n\nworm-os production policy:\n\nAllowedIPs:\n0.0.0.0/0\n\nTraffic path:\n\nworm-os\n ->\nWireGuard\n ->\nOPNsense\n ->\nNAT\n ->\n2.28.34.27\n ->\nInternet\n\nVerify:\n- NAT source 10.60.0.0/24\n- translation WAN address\n- public IPv4 = 2.28.34.27\n\nDo not NAT private traffic unnecessarily between:\n10.60.0.0/24\nand\n10.90.0.0/16\n\n==================================================\n5. PRIVATE WORM SERVICES\n==================================================\n\nRequired path:\n\nworm-os 10.60.0.x\n ->\nOPNsense\n ->\nworm-robot 10.90.20.10\n\nPrivate services may include:\n- OTA metadata\n- OTA payloads\n- image.zip\n- apps\n- downloads\n- backup\n- Worm APIs\n\nDo not allow trusted-any.\n\nUse explicit service ACLs.\n\n==================================================\n6. ADMIN ISOLATION\n==================================================\n\nADMIN:\n10.77.0.0/24\n\nworm-os MUST NOT gain access to:\n- worm-robot SSH\n- ADMIN WireGuard\n- admin dashboard\n- databases\n- management APIs\n\nException:\nOPNsense management on 10.60.0.1 ports 22/80/443 is explicitly allowed\nby frozen policy.\n\n==================================================\n7. FIREHOL\n==================================================\n\nFireHOL remains REQUIRED on worm-robot.\n\nRole:\n- host firewall\n- SSH policy\n- blocklists\n- private service filtering\n\nPublic interface:\ndefault deny\n\nAllow only:\n- SSH according to admin policy\n- ADMIN WireGuard UDP/51820 if still required\n- established/related\n\nDeny publicly:\n- Web\n- OTA\n- downloads\n- apps\n- backup\n- Worm APIs\n\nPrivate interface:\nenp9s0.4000\n\nDo not mark private as trusted-any.\n\nAllow only explicit Worm service ports.\n\n==================================================\n8. BLOCKLISTS\n==================================================\n\nAudit existing FireHOL blocklist state.\n\nIf blocklists are not currently implemented:\ncreate a documented, controlled blocklist mechanism.\n\nRequirements:\n- trusted sources only\n- automated refresh\n- atomic updates\n- fail-safe behavior\n- no lockout of authorized SSH/admin paths\n- IPv4/IPv6 awareness\n- no secret material\n\nDo not use random/unreviewed lists.\n\n==================================================\n9. LISTENER HARDENING\n==================================================\n\nAudit all sockets.\n\nPay special attention to wildcard listeners:\n\n0.0.0.0\n[::]\n*:9094\n*:5353\n\nClassify:\nPUBLIC_REQUIRED\nPRIVATE_REQUIRED\nWG_ADMIN\nLOOPBACK\nUNEXPECTED\n\nWhere practical, rebind Worm private services to:\n10.90.20.10\n\nDo not bind private Worm services to:\n162.55.6.173\n0.0.0.0\n[::]\n\nIf rebinding is unsafe:\nretain FireHOL protection and document the exception.\n\n==================================================\n10. STALE FIREWALL/NAT CLEANUP\n==================================================\n\nAudit:\n- iptables\n- nftables\n- NAT\n- FORWARD\n\nIdentify stale troubleshooting rules from earlier OPNsense access tests.\n\nRemove ONLY rules proven obsolete.\n\nBefore deletion:\n- document exact rule\n- document why obsolete\n- prepare rollback\n\nworm-robot must remain:\n\nnet.ipv4.ip_forward=0\n\n==================================================\n11. DNS\n==================================================\n\nPrivate Worm DNS target:\n\nworm.coffee.pm -> 10.90.20.10\n\nfor worm-os/private resolution where appropriate.\n\nOPNsense resolver:\n10.60.0.1\n\nRequirements:\n- DNS through protected path\n- no public resolver exposure\n- no DNS leak\n- no fallback to untrusted DNS in production full-tunnel mode\n\n==================================================\n12. IPV6\n==================================================\n\nAudit separately.\n\nUntil full Worm IPv6 tunnel design is complete:\n\nworm-os must not leak IPv6 outside OPNsense.\n\nworm-robot public IPv6 must not expose private services.\n\nOPNsense WAN must not expose:\n22\n80\n443\n\nover IPv6.\n\n==================================================\n13. PUBLIC WEB / OTA\n==================================================\n\nPublic Worm distribution remains disabled.\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC:\n\n80 DENY\n443 DENY\nUDP/443 DENY\n\nPublic deny:\n- WebUSB\n- Web Installer\n- image.zip\n- OTA\n- downloads\n- apps\n\nPrivate service plane:\n10.90.20.10\n\n==================================================\n14. FAIL-CLOSED\n==================================================\n\nWorm private services must never fall back publicly.\n\nIf WireGuard is unavailable:\n\nFAIL CLOSED for:\n- OTA\n- downloads\n- apps\n- backup\n- Worm APIs\n\nNo fallback to:\n162.55.6.173\npublic mirrors\nalternate public URLs\n\n==================================================\n15. REBOOT PERSISTENCE\n==================================================\n\nPrepare and execute controlled persistence validation where safe.\n\nAfter reboot verify:\n\nworm-robot:\n- public IP\n- private IP\n- VLAN 4000\n- MTU 1400\n- private route\n- FireHOL\n- blocklists\n- wg0 ADMIN\n- ip_forward=0\n\nOPNsense:\n- WAN\n- private IP\n- WireGuard\n- peer config\n- NAT\n- firewall\n- DNS\n\nDo not reboot both systems simultaneously.\n\nPreserve recovery path.\n\n==================================================\n16. MONITORING\n==================================================\n\nPrepare private health checks for:\n\n- OPNsense WG handshake\n- peer status\n- private routing\n- NAT\n- DNS\n- FireHOL\n- blocklist refresh\n- private OTA endpoint\n\nDo not log secrets.\n\n==================================================\n17. AUDIT MATRIX\n==================================================\n\nCreate an ACL matrix documenting:\n\nSOURCE:\nInternet\nworm-os 10.60.0.0/24\nADMIN 10.77.0.0/24\nOPNsense private\nworm-robot private\n\nDESTINATIONS:\nOPNsense management\nworm-robot SSH\nOTA\napps\ndownloads\nbackup\ndatabases\nADMIN services\nInternet\n\nFor every relation record:\nALLOW / DENY\nport/protocol\nreason\n\n==================================================\n18. SECURITY / SECRETS\n==================================================\n\nNever store:\n- WireGuard private keys\n- PSKs\n- Hetzner token\n- passwords\n- SSH private keys\n- signing keys\n- backup encryption keys\n\nRun secret scan.\n\n==================================================\n19. OUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nPATCH-NET-007-PRIVATE-NETWORK-HARDENING/\n\nInclude:\n\nREADME.md\nARCHITECTURE.md\nACL-MATRIX.md\nOPNSENSE.md\nWORM-ROBOT.md\nWIREGUARD.md\nFIREHOL.md\nBLOCKLISTS.md\nLISTENERS.md\nDNS.md\nIPV6.md\nFAIL-CLOSED.md\nPERSISTENCE.md\nMONITORING.md\nSECURITY.md\nTESTS.md\nROLLBACK.md\nmodified-files.txt\ntest-results.txt\n\n==================================================\n20. FINAL GATE\n==================================================\n\nDo not mark PASS unless:\n\nPASS frozen topology unchanged\nPASS worm-robot public IP unchanged\nPASS worm-robot private IP 10.90.20.10\nPASS OPNsense public IP 2.28.34.27\nPASS OPNsense private IP 10.90.10.1\nPASS OPNsense WG 10.60.0.1\nPASS worm-os unique /32 policy\nPASS public egress 2.28.34.27\nPASS OPNsense 22/80/443 reachable only through WG/private policy\nPASS OPNsense WAN 22/80/443 denied\nPASS UDP/51821 operational\nPASS FireHOL active\nPASS SSH policy preserved\nPASS blocklist mechanism healthy\nPASS no trusted-any private rule\nPASS stale troubleshooting rules handled\nPASS no broad forwarding\nPASS ip_forward=0 on worm-robot\nPASS public Web disabled\nPASS public OTA/download denied\nPASS private Worm routing\nPASS DNS protected\nPASS IPv6 leak protection\nPASS no public fallback\nPASS reboot persistence or explicitly documented PENDING blocker\nPASS no secrets\n\nFinal output:\n\nPATCH-NET-007 STATUS:\nPASS / PARTIAL / BLOCKED\n\nThen rerun:\n\nWORM PRE-CHECK AUDIT\n\nExpected final gate:\n\nWORM PRE-CHECK: PASS\n\nDo not start another patch automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0788c-2277-70b2-bd08-cdbf0f896396","create_time":1788728844.947061,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07893-2ca1-72e2-8e01-3b6ed8688cf1","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-NET-007-PRIVATE-NETWORK-HARDENING.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nThe frozen Worm network architecture MUST NOT change.\n\nFROZEN TOPOLOGY:\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nOPNsense PUBLIC / worm-os egress:\n2.28.34.27\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nOPNsense WireGuard:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nworm-os:\n10.60.0.x/32\n\nHetzner private backbone:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nADMIN WireGuard:\n10.77.0.0/24\n\nvSwitch:\n85469\n\nVLAN:\n4000\n\n==================================================\nGOAL\n==================================================\n\nComplete and harden all PRIVATE IP networking.\n\nDo NOT redesign addresses.\nDo NOT migrate public IPs.\nDo NOT remove OPNsense.\nDo NOT repurpose worm-robot.\nDo NOT enable broad forwarding.\n\n==================================================\n1. OPNsense MANAGEMENT ON WORM-OS WG\n==================================================\n\nRequired policy:\n\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.60.0.1 TCP/22  ALLOW\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.60.0.1 TCP/80  ALLOW\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.60.0.1 TCP/443 ALLOW\n\nWAN remains:\n\n2.28.34.27:22  DENY\n2.28.34.27:80  DENY\n2.28.34.27:443 DENY\n\nWireGuard:\nUDP/51821 ALLOW\n\nPrefer management services bound to private/WG addresses only.\n\n==================================================\n2. PRIVATE BACKBONE\n==================================================\n\nVerify and harden:\n\nOPNsense:\n10.90.10.1\n\nworm-robot:\n10.90.20.10\n\nNetwork:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nworm-robot VLAN:\nenp9s0.4000\nVLAN 4000\nMTU 1400\n\nExpected route:\n\n10.90.0.0/16 via 10.90.20.1 dev enp9s0.4000 onlink\n\nVerify deterministic source selection:\n10.90.20.10\n\nNo duplicate routes.\nNo stale routes.\n\n==================================================\n3. WORM-OS ADDRESSING\n==================================================\n\nNetwork:\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nOPNsense:\n10.60.0.1\n\nEach worm-os device:\nunique /32\n\nExample:\n10.60.0.10/32\n10.60.0.11/32\n10.60.0.12/32\n\nEach device MUST have:\n- unique keypair\n- unique AllowedIP\n- independent revocation\n\nNo shared private keys.\n\n==================================================\n4. FULL-TUNNEL\n==================================================\n\nworm-os production policy:\n\nAllowedIPs:\n0.0.0.0/0\n\nTraffic path:\n\nworm-os\n ->\nWireGuard\n ->\nOPNsense\n ->\nNAT\n ->\n2.28.34.27\n ->\nInternet\n\nVerify:\n- NAT source 10.60.0.0/24\n- translation WAN address\n- public IPv4 = 2.28.34.27\n\nDo not NAT private traffic unnecessarily between:\n10.60.0.0/24\nand\n10.90.0.0/16\n\n==================================================\n5. PRIVATE WORM SERVICES\n==================================================\n\nRequired path:\n\nworm-os 10.60.0.x\n ->\nOPNsense\n ->\nworm-robot 10.90.20.10\n\nPrivate services may include:\n- OTA metadata\n- OTA payloads\n- image.zip\n- apps\n- downloads\n- backup\n- Worm APIs\n\nDo not allow trusted-any.\n\nUse explicit service ACLs.\n\n==================================================\n6. ADMIN ISOLATION\n==================================================\n\nADMIN:\n10.77.0.0/24\n\nworm-os MUST NOT gain access to:\n- worm-robot SSH\n- ADMIN WireGuard\n- admin dashboard\n- databases\n- management APIs\n\nException:\nOPNsense management on 10.60.0.1 ports 22/80/443 is explicitly allowed\nby frozen policy.\n\n==================================================\n7. FIREHOL\n==================================================\n\nFireHOL remains REQUIRED on worm-robot.\n\nRole:\n- host firewall\n- SSH policy\n- blocklists\n- private service filtering\n\nPublic interface:\ndefault deny\n\nAllow only:\n- SSH according to admin policy\n- ADMIN WireGuard UDP/51820 if still required\n- established/related\n\nDeny publicly:\n- Web\n- OTA\n- downloads\n- apps\n- backup\n- Worm APIs\n\nPrivate interface:\nenp9s0.4000\n\nDo not mark private as trusted-any.\n\nAllow only explicit Worm service ports.\n\n==================================================\n8. BLOCKLISTS\n==================================================\n\nAudit existing FireHOL blocklist state.\n\nIf blocklists are not currently implemented:\ncreate a documented, controlled blocklist mechanism.\n\nRequirements:\n- trusted sources only\n- automated refresh\n- atomic updates\n- fail-safe behavior\n- no lockout of authorized SSH/admin paths\n- IPv4/IPv6 awareness\n- no secret material\n\nDo not use random/unreviewed lists.\n\n==================================================\n9. LISTENER HARDENING\n==================================================\n\nAudit all sockets.\n\nPay special attention to wildcard listeners:\n\n0.0.0.0\n[::]\n*:9094\n*:5353\n\nClassify:\nPUBLIC_REQUIRED\nPRIVATE_REQUIRED\nWG_ADMIN\nLOOPBACK\nUNEXPECTED\n\nWhere practical, rebind Worm private services to:\n10.90.20.10\n\nDo not bind private Worm services to:\n162.55.6.173\n0.0.0.0\n[::]\n\nIf rebinding is unsafe:\nretain FireHOL protection and document the exception.\n\n==================================================\n10. STALE FIREWALL/NAT CLEANUP\n==================================================\n\nAudit:\n- iptables\n- nftables\n- NAT\n- FORWARD\n\nIdentify stale troubleshooting rules from earlier OPNsense access tests.\n\nRemove ONLY rules proven obsolete.\n\nBefore deletion:\n- document exact rule\n- document why obsolete\n- prepare rollback\n\nworm-robot must remain:\n\nnet.ipv4.ip_forward=0\n\n==================================================\n11. DNS\n==================================================\n\nPrivate Worm DNS target:\n\nworm.coffee.pm -> 10.90.20.10\n\nfor worm-os/private resolution where appropriate.\n\nOPNsense resolver:\n10.60.0.1\n\nRequirements:\n- DNS through protected path\n- no public resolver exposure\n- no DNS leak\n- no fallback to untrusted DNS in production full-tunnel mode\n\n==================================================\n12. IPV6\n==================================================\n\nAudit separately.\n\nUntil full Worm IPv6 tunnel design is complete:\n\nworm-os must not leak IPv6 outside OPNsense.\n\nworm-robot public IPv6 must not expose private services.\n\nOPNsense WAN must not expose:\n22\n80\n443\n\nover IPv6.\n\n==================================================\n13. PUBLIC WEB / OTA\n==================================================\n\nPublic Worm distribution remains disabled.\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC:\n\n80 DENY\n443 DENY\nUDP/443 DENY\n\nPublic deny:\n- WebUSB\n- Web Installer\n- image.zip\n- OTA\n- downloads\n- apps\n\nPrivate service plane:\n10.90.20.10\n\n==================================================\n14. FAIL-CLOSED\n==================================================\n\nWorm private services must never fall back publicly.\n\nIf WireGuard is unavailable:\n\nFAIL CLOSED for:\n- OTA\n- downloads\n- apps\n- backup\n- Worm APIs\n\nNo fallback to:\n162.55.6.173\npublic mirrors\nalternate public URLs\n\n==================================================\n15. REBOOT PERSISTENCE\n==================================================\n\nPrepare and execute controlled persistence validation where safe.\n\nAfter reboot verify:\n\nworm-robot:\n- public IP\n- private IP\n- VLAN 4000\n- MTU 1400\n- private route\n- FireHOL\n- blocklists\n- wg0 ADMIN\n- ip_forward=0\n\nOPNsense:\n- WAN\n- private IP\n- WireGuard\n- peer config\n- NAT\n- firewall\n- DNS\n\nDo not reboot both systems simultaneously.\n\nPreserve recovery path.\n\n==================================================\n16. MONITORING\n==================================================\n\nPrepare private health checks for:\n\n- OPNsense WG handshake\n- peer status\n- private routing\n- NAT\n- DNS\n- FireHOL\n- blocklist refresh\n- private OTA endpoint\n\nDo not log secrets.\n\n==================================================\n17. AUDIT MATRIX\n==================================================\n\nCreate an ACL matrix documenting:\n\nSOURCE:\nInternet\nworm-os 10.60.0.0/24\nADMIN 10.77.0.0/24\nOPNsense private\nworm-robot private\n\nDESTINATIONS:\nOPNsense management\nworm-robot SSH\nOTA\napps\ndownloads\nbackup\ndatabases\nADMIN services\nInternet\n\nFor every relation record:\nALLOW / DENY\nport/protocol\nreason\n\n==================================================\n18. SECURITY / SECRETS\n==================================================\n\nNever store:\n- WireGuard private keys\n- PSKs\n- Hetzner token\n- passwords\n- SSH private keys\n- signing keys\n- backup encryption keys\n\nRun secret scan.\n\n==================================================\n19. OUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nPATCH-NET-007-PRIVATE-NETWORK-HARDENING/\n\nInclude:\n\nREADME.md\nARCHITECTURE.md\nACL-MATRIX.md\nOPNSENSE.md\nWORM-ROBOT.md\nWIREGUARD.md\nFIREHOL.md\nBLOCKLISTS.md\nLISTENERS.md\nDNS.md\nIPV6.md\nFAIL-CLOSED.md\nPERSISTENCE.md\nMONITORING.md\nSECURITY.md\nTESTS.md\nROLLBACK.md\nmodified-files.txt\ntest-results.txt\n\n==================================================\n20. FINAL GATE\n==================================================\n\nDo not mark PASS unless:\n\nPASS frozen topology unchanged\nPASS worm-robot public IP unchanged\nPASS worm-robot private IP 10.90.20.10\nPASS OPNsense public IP 2.28.34.27\nPASS OPNsense private IP 10.90.10.1\nPASS OPNsense WG 10.60.0.1\nPASS worm-os unique /32 policy\nPASS public egress 2.28.34.27\nPASS OPNsense 22/80/443 reachable only through WG/private policy\nPASS OPNsense WAN 22/80/443 denied\nPASS UDP/51821 operational\nPASS FireHOL active\nPASS SSH policy preserved\nPASS blocklist mechanism healthy\nPASS no trusted-any private rule\nPASS stale troubleshooting rules handled\nPASS no broad forwarding\nPASS ip_forward=0 on worm-robot\nPASS public Web disabled\nPASS public OTA/download denied\nPASS private Worm routing\nPASS DNS protected\nPASS IPv6 leak protection\nPASS no public fallback\nPASS reboot persistence or explicitly documented PENDING blocker\nPASS no secrets\n\nFinal output:\n\nPATCH-NET-007 STATUS:\nPASS / PARTIAL / BLOCKED\n\nThen rerun:\n\nWORM PRE-CHECK AUDIT\n\nExpected final gate:\n\nWORM PRE-CHECK: PASS\n\nDo not start another patch automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07893-2c86-7791-9c14-76b5631ce08a","create_time":1788729306.2736113,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07893-b122-7192-919e-0c1fe0fc832f","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Perform FINAL WORM INFRASTRUCTURE AUDIT before enabling\nWireGuard as a native/system component of worm-os.\n\nAUDIT ONLY.\n\nDO NOT:\n- modify networking\n- modify OPNsense\n- modify FireHOL\n- reload firewall\n- change NAT\n- change DNS\n- change WireGuard keys\n- recreate peers\n- restart services unless strictly required for a read-only test\n- reboot\n- modify Android/ex-graphene\n- start the system WireGuard implementation\n\n==================================================\nPURPOSE\n==================================================\n\nDetermine whether the current infrastructure is safe and ready for:\n\nWORM-OS SYSTEM WIREGUARD\n\nThe final decision MUST be exactly:\n\nREADY FOR WORM-OS SYSTEM WIREGUARD\n\nor:\n\nNOT READY FOR WORM-OS SYSTEM WIREGUARD\n\n==================================================\nFROZEN ARCHITECTURE\n==================================================\n\nINTERNET\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nOPNsense PUBLIC / worm-os dedicated egress:\n2.28.34.27\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nOPNsense WireGuard:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nworm-os peers:\n10.60.0.x/32\n\nHetzner private backbone:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nADMIN WireGuard:\n10.77.0.0/24\n\nvSwitch:\n85469\n\nVLAN:\n4000\n\nThis topology is FROZEN.\n\n==================================================\n1. VERIFY PATCH STATE\n==================================================\n\nAudit all existing relevant patches:\n\nPATCH-NET-001-OPNSENSE-PRIVATE-IP\nPATCH-NET-002-WORM-ROBOT-PRIVATE-IP\nPATCH-NET-003-WORM-OS-PRIVATE-IP\nPATCH-NET-004-WORM-OS-PUBLIC-EGRESS\nPATCH-NET-004B-OPNSENSE-WORMOS-MANAGEMENT\nPATCH-NET-005-DISABLE-PUBLIC-WEB\nPATCH-NET-006-WORM-PRIVATE-OTA-DOWNLOADS\nPATCH-NET-007-PRIVATE-NETWORK-HARDENING\n\nFor each report:\n\nPASS\nPARTIAL\nPENDING\nFAIL\nNOT IMPLEMENTED\n\nA P0/P1 unresolved networking/security issue blocks system integration.\n\n==================================================\n2. REAL WIREGUARD CLIENT\n==================================================\n\nVerify the existing real Android test peer.\n\nExpected client:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nVerify:\n- peer exists\n- public key matches provisioned peer\n- AllowedIP exactly 10.60.0.10/32\n- latest handshake RECENT while client is connected\n- RX > 0\n- TX > 0\n- UDP/51821 operational\n\nNever print private keys.\n\n==================================================\n3. FULL-TUNNEL INTERNET\n==================================================\n\nWith real Android client connected:\n\nAllowedIPs:\n0.0.0.0/0\n\nVerify actual Internet access.\n\nVerify using at least two independent HTTPS observations that:\n\nPUBLIC_EGRESS_IPV4=2.28.34.27\n\nThe test MUST be performed from the client path.\nDo not infer PASS merely from NAT configuration.\n\nVerify:\n- HTTPS works\n- normal TCP works\n- DNS works\n- network transitions do not immediately break tunnel\n\n==================================================\n4. DNS\n==================================================\n\nExpected protected resolver:\n10.60.0.1\n\nVerify from real worm-os test client:\n- UDP/53 works\n- TCP/53 works where appropriate\n- ordinary hostname resolution works\n- resolver is not exposed publicly on WAN\n- no underlying mobile/Wi-Fi DNS fallback during protected mode\n\nReport:\nDNS_PROTECTED=PASS/FAIL\n\n==================================================\n5. IPV6 LEAK\n==================================================\n\nCritical gate.\n\nVerify whether the Android client has native IPv6 from 5G/Wi-Fi.\n\nWhile full-tunnel WireGuard is enabled:\n- verify IPv6 cannot bypass OPNsense\n- verify external IPv6 is either tunneled according to an explicit design\n  or unavailable by deliberate policy\n\nDo not mark PASS merely because IPv4 works.\n\nReport:\nIPV6_LEAK=NONE/DETECTED/UNTESTED\n\nUNTESTED blocks production system-WireGuard activation.\n\n==================================================\n6. FAIL-CLOSED REQUIREMENT\n==================================================\n\nThis is critical for native worm-os integration.\n\nVerify infrastructure supports:\n\nWireGuard available:\nWorm private services reachable through protected route.\n\nWireGuard unavailable:\nWorm private services NOT reachable through public fallback.\n\nCheck:\n- OTA\n- image.zip\n- update metadata\n- update payloads\n- Worm downloads\n- Apps private distribution\n- backup/private APIs where implemented\n\nVerify no client configuration points to a public fallback.\n\nReport:\nPRIVATE_SERVICES_FAIL_CLOSED=PASS/FAIL/PENDING\n\nFAIL blocks system integration.\nPENDING blocks production activation if the corresponding service is\nalready expected to ship.\n\n==================================================\n7. OPNsense MANAGEMENT POLICY\n==================================================\n\nFrozen policy:\n\nworm-os 10.60.0.0/24\n        ->\nOPNsense 10.60.0.1\n\nRequired:\nTCP/22  ALLOW\nTCP/80  ALLOW\nTCP/443 ALLOW\n\nVerify from real WireGuard client.\n\nWAN must remain:\n\n2.28.34.27:22  CLOSED/FILTERED\n2.28.34.27:80  CLOSED/FILTERED\n2.28.34.27:443 CLOSED/FILTERED\n\nUDP/51821:\nAVAILABLE\n\nVerify IPv6 does not bypass WAN management restrictions.\n\n==================================================\n8. WORM-ROBOT PRIVATE PATH\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\nworm-os\n10.60.0.x\n ->\nOPNsense\n ->\n10.90.20.10\n\nConfirm permitted Worm private service traffic works.\n\nConfirm worm-os cannot reach worm-robot SSH unless explicitly allowed\nby frozen policy.\n\nConfirm worm-os cannot reach:\n10.77.0.0/24 ADMIN\n\n==================================================\n9. WORM-ROBOT\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\nPUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nPRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nVLAN:\n4000\n\nMTU:\n1400\n\nPRIVATE ROUTE:\n10.90.0.0/16 via expected private path\n\nVerify:\nnet.ipv4.ip_forward=0\n\nworm-robot MUST NOT become a router for worm-os.\n\n==================================================\n10. FIREHOL\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n- installed\n- enabled\n- active\n- configuration valid\n- public default deny\n- SSH policy correct\n- ADMIN WireGuard policy correct\n- blocklist mechanism status\n- private interface not trusted-any\n- no broad forwarding\n- IPv4 filtering\n- IPv6 filtering\n\nRecord wildcard listeners separately.\n\nReview especially:\n*:9094\n*:5353\nand any other wildcard socket.\n\nA wildcard listener protected correctly by FireHOL may be WARN,\nbut an unintended publicly reachable service is FAIL.\n\n==================================================\n11. PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION\n==================================================\n\nVerify public Internet CANNOT retrieve:\n\n- Web Installer\n- WebUSB\n- image.zip\n- OTA metadata\n- OTA payloads\n- Worm downloads\n- private Apps content\n\nTest:\n- hostname\n- direct IPv4\n- IPv6\n- alternate Host header where relevant\n- TCP/80\n- TCP/443\n- UDP/443/HTTP3\n\nExpected:\nPUBLIC_WORM_DISTRIBUTION=DENIED\n\n==================================================\n12. PRIVATE OTA / DOWNLOADS\n==================================================\n\nVerify intended private path:\n\nworm-os\n ->\nWireGuard\n ->\nOPNsense\n ->\nworm-robot 10.90.20.10\n\nWhere implemented verify:\n- metadata\n- payload\n- image.zip\n- downloads\n\nVerify OTA signature/integrity verification remains unchanged.\n\nWireGuard transport MUST NOT replace Android OTA cryptographic\nverification.\n\n==================================================\n13. STALE RULES\n==================================================\n\nAudit:\n- iptables\n- nftables\n- PF\n- NAT\n- FORWARD\n\nIdentify stale troubleshooting rules from previous recovery work.\n\nNo unexplained broad forwarding/NAT rule may remain.\n\nDo not delete anything during this audit.\n\n==================================================\n14. REBOOT/PERSISTENCE STATUS\n==================================================\n\nReport persistence status for:\n\nOPNsense:\n- private interface\n- WireGuard\n- peer\n- NAT\n- firewall\n- DNS\n\nworm-robot:\n- VLAN 4000\n- 10.90.20.10\n- route\n- FireHOL\n- ADMIN WG\n- ip_forward=0\n\nIf reboot persistence has already been tested, record PASS.\n\nIf not:\nPENDING REBOOT VALIDATION\n\nDetermine whether this is blocking based on whether the exact persistent\nconfiguration has otherwise been verified.\n\n==================================================\n15. SYSTEM-WIREGUARD PREREQUISITES\n==================================================\n\nAudit ex-graphene/Worm OS source READ-ONLY.\n\nDetermine:\n\n- kernel WireGuard support\n- userspace/platform WireGuard support\n- Android VPN framework capabilities\n- always-on VPN facilities\n- lockdown/fail-closed facilities\n- boot lifecycle hooks\n- ConnectivityService integration points\n- NetworkStack/netd implications\n- Android Keystore options\n- SELinux implications\n- backup exclusion mechanisms\n- OTA integration points\n- Wi-Fi/5G handover support\n\nDO NOT modify source.\n\n==================================================\n16. KEY MANAGEMENT GATE\n==================================================\n\nConfirm proposed system design can satisfy:\n\n- unique keypair per device\n- private key generated on device\n- private key never in source tree\n- private key never in factory image\n- private key never in OTA\n- private key excluded from backup\n- no private key in logs\n- no private key in dumpsys\n- public-key enrollment supported\n- per-device /32 assignment\n- individual peer revocation\n- OPNsense public-key rotation support\n\nAny design requiring one shared private key across Worm OS devices:\nP0 FAIL.\n\n==================================================\n17. SYSTEM PRIVACY POLICY GATE\n==================================================\n\nConfirm intended production policy:\n\nBOOT\n ->\nnetwork available\n ->\nsystem WireGuard\n ->\nhandshake\n ->\nprotected networking available\n ->\nWorm private services enabled\n\nNo race where OTA/apps/downloads contact public endpoints before\nWireGuard is ready.\n\nProduction target:\n\nAllowedIPs:\n0.0.0.0/0\n\nNo ordinary application can disable the Worm system tunnel.\n\nNetwork transitions:\nWi-Fi -> 5G\n5G -> Wi-Fi\nreconnect\nairplane mode\ntemporary loss\n\nmust have a viable recovery design.\n\n==================================================\n18. SECRET SCAN\n==================================================\n\nScan patch and audit documentation for:\n\nPrivateKey\nPresharedKey\npassword\nHetzner token\nSSH private key\nsigning private key\nbackup encryption key\n\nNever print secret values.\n\nPublic WireGuard keys are allowed.\n\n==================================================\n19. FINDING SEVERITY\n==================================================\n\nClassify every finding:\n\nP0 CRITICAL\nP1 HIGH\nP2 MEDIUM\nP3 LOW\nINFO\n\nFor every P0/P1 include:\n- component\n- evidence\n- risk\n- required remediation\n- whether it blocks system WireGuard\n\n==================================================\n20. OUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\n/root/WORM-SYSTEM-WIREGUARD-FINAL-AUDIT/\n\nInclude:\n\n00-EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY.md\n01-FROZEN-ARCHITECTURE.md\n02-PATCH-STATE.md\n03-WIREGUARD-LIVE.md\n04-FULL-TUNNEL.md\n05-DNS.md\n06-IPV6-LEAK.md\n07-FAIL-CLOSED.md\n08-OPNSENSE.md\n09-WORM-ROBOT.md\n10-FIREHOL.md\n11-PUBLIC-DISTRIBUTION.md\n12-PRIVATE-OTA.md\n13-PERSISTENCE.md\n14-ANDROID-PREREQUISITES.md\n15-KEY-MANAGEMENT.md\n16-SELINUX.md\n17-THREAT-MODEL.md\n18-SECRET-SCAN.md\n19-FINDINGS.md\n20-READINESS.md\n\nAlso:\nfindings.json\n\n==================================================\nFINAL GATE\n==================================================\n\nREADY requires at minimum:\n\nPASS frozen topology\nPASS real WireGuard handshake\nPASS RX/TX\nPASS full-tunnel Internet\nPASS public IPv4 = 2.28.34.27\nPASS protected DNS\nPASS no IPv6 leak\nPASS OPNsense WG management policy\nPASS OPNsense WAN management denied\nPASS private routing\nPASS ADMIN isolation\nPASS worm-robot ip_forward=0\nPASS FireHOL\nPASS public WebUSB/Web disabled\nPASS public OTA/download denied\nPASS private service fail-closed\nPASS per-device key-management design\nPASS no shared device private key\nPASS no critical secret leak\nPASS Android integration path identified\nPASS SELinux can remain enforcing\n\nIf any P0/P1 remains unresolved:\n\nNOT READY FOR WORM-OS SYSTEM WIREGUARD\n\nOtherwise:\n\nREADY FOR WORM-OS SYSTEM WIREGUARD\n\nDo not implement WireGuard in Android during this audit.\nDo not remediate automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07893-b107-7692-bf60-95f72743a1fd","create_time":1788729340.1944475,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a0789d-93a6-7900-8d8a-6ca498738866","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Reclassify the final WireGuard audit into two separate gates.\n\nDO NOT modify infrastructure.\nDO NOT modify Android source yet.\n\nGATE A:\nREADY FOR WORM-OS SYSTEM WIREGUARD DEVELOPMENT\n\nGATE B:\nREADY FOR WORM-OS SYSTEM WIREGUARD PRODUCTION\n\nThe previous audit incorrectly treated the absence of the component\nwe intend to develop as a blocker to beginning its development.\n\nReclassify:\n\nP1-ANDROID-SYSTEMWG-001\n\"No completed native/system WireGuard component\"\n\nThis MUST NOT block DEVELOPMENT.\nIt MUST block PRODUCTION until implementation and device tests pass.\n\nP1-FAILCLOSED-001:\nNative fail-closed behavior is part of the system-WireGuard\nimplementation.\n\nIt MUST NOT block DEVELOPMENT.\nIt MUST block PRODUCTION until implemented and tested.\n\nThe following infrastructure/live-client findings remain prerequisites\nthat should be resolved or explicitly validated:\n\nP1-WG-LIVE-001\nP1-FULLTUNNEL-001\nP1-DNS-CLIENT-001\nP1-IPV6-LEAK-001\n\nNow perform a focused live-client validation using the real Android\nWireGuard client.\n\nImportant known correction:\nOPNsense actual public key starts with lowercase \"v\":\n\nvJtONCHbxMj3d7SAsPD5OFkTD7NcAoNgjIfmTcinE04=\n\nAndroid client peer configuration must use exactly that key.\n\nExpected client:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51821\n\nAllowedIPs:\n0.0.0.0/0\n\nDNS:\n10.60.0.1\n\nWhile the Android tunnel is enabled verify:\n\n1. recent WireGuard handshake\n2. RX > 0\n3. TX > 0\n4. Internet connectivity\n5. public IPv4 exactly 2.28.34.27\n6. DNS through 10.60.0.1\n7. IPv6 leak behavior\n8. 10.60.0.1 management reachability according to frozen policy\n9. private routing toward 10.90.0.0/16\n10. ADMIN 10.77.0.0/24 isolation\n\nDo not change keys, NAT, DNS or firewall merely because a test fails.\nIdentify the exact failing layer first.\n\nUpdate:\n/root/WORM-SYSTEM-WIREGUARD-FINAL-AUDIT/20-READINESS.md\n/root/WORM-SYSTEM-WIREGUARD-FINAL-AUDIT/19-FINDINGS.md\n/root/WORM-SYSTEM-WIREGUARD-FINAL-AUDIT/findings.json\n\nFinal output MUST contain TWO decisions:\n\nDEVELOPMENT GATE:\nREADY FOR WORM-OS SYSTEM WIREGUARD DEVELOPMENT\nor\nNOT READY FOR WORM-OS SYSTEM WIREGUARD DEVELOPMENT\n\nPRODUCTION GATE:\nREADY FOR WORM-OS SYSTEM WIREGUARD PRODUCTION\nor\nNOT READY FOR WORM-OS SYSTEM WIREGUARD PRODUCTION\n\nDo not implement the Android component yet."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0789d-935d-71f0-a318-73535221f6ad","create_time":1788729988.0068915,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a078a3-c179-7bf2-8723-4e192db548ab","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Perform WORM-OS SYSTEM WIREGUARD DEVELOPMENT GATE.\n\nAUDIT ONLY.\n\nDO NOT:\n- modify OPNsense\n- modify worm-robot\n- modify FireHOL\n- change NAT\n- change DNS\n- change WireGuard keys\n- recreate peers\n- reboot\n- modify ex-graphene/Worm OS source yet\n- start implementation\n\n==================================================\nGOAL\n==================================================\n\nDecide only whether we can START DEVELOPMENT of native/system WireGuard\ninside worm-os.\n\nThis is NOT the production-release gate.\n\nFinal decision must be exactly:\n\nREADY FOR WORM-OS SYSTEM WIREGUARD DEVELOPMENT\n\nor:\n\nNOT READY FOR WORM-OS SYSTEM WIREGUARD DEVELOPMENT\n\n==================================================\nFROZEN TOPOLOGY\n==================================================\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nOPNsense PUBLIC / worm-os egress:\n2.28.34.27\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nOPNsense WireGuard:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nworm-os peers:\n10.60.0.x/32\n\nHetzner private backbone:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nADMIN WireGuard:\n10.77.0.0/24\n\nvSwitch:\n85469\n\nVLAN:\n4000\n\nTopology is frozen.\n\n==================================================\n1. SERVER-SIDE READINESS\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\n- OPNsense server running\n- WAN = 2.28.34.27\n- PRIVATE = 10.90.10.1\n- WG = 10.60.0.1/24\n- UDP/51821 listening\n- real Android peer exists\n- peer AllowedIP = 10.60.0.10/32\n- worm-robot PRIVATE = 10.90.20.10\n- worm-robot FireHOL active\n- worm-robot net.ipv4.ip_forward = 0\n- ADMIN network 10.77.0.0/24 unchanged\n\nNever print private keys.\n\n==================================================\n2. REAL CLIENT WIREGUARD VALIDATION\n==================================================\n\nKnown Android client:\n\nAddress:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51821\n\nOPNsense actual public key:\nvJtONCHbxMj3d7SAsPD5OFkTD7NcAoNgjIfmTcinE04=\n\nAllowedIPs:\n0.0.0.0/0\n\nDNS:\n10.60.0.1\n\nWith Android tunnel enabled, verify:\n\n- latest handshake RECENT\n- RX > 0\n- TX > 0\n\nIf no recent handshake:\nDEVELOPMENT GATE = FAIL\n\n==================================================\n3. FULL-TUNNEL VALIDATION\n==================================================\n\nWith real Android client connected verify:\n\n- Internet works\n- public IPv4 = 2.28.34.27\n- HTTPS works\n\nUse real client-path evidence.\n\nDo not infer PASS from NAT configuration alone.\n\nIf public egress is not proven:\nDEVELOPMENT GATE = FAIL\n\n==================================================\n4. DNS VALIDATION\n==================================================\n\nVerify from real Android client:\n\n- DNS through 10.60.0.1 works\n- hostname resolution works\n- no public WAN DNS exposure is required\n\nFor DEVELOPMENT:\na validated working protected DNS path is required.\n\nIf DNS is broken:\nDEVELOPMENT GATE = FAIL\n\n==================================================\n5. IPV6 DEVELOPMENT POLICY\n==================================================\n\nFor DEVELOPMENT, one of these is acceptable:\n\nA. IPv6 is properly tunneled and tested\n\nOR\n\nB. IPv6 is explicitly blocked for worm-os protected mode and there is\nevidence that direct IPv6 cannot bypass the VPN during test.\n\nIf IPv6 behavior is completely unknown:\nDEVELOPMENT GATE = FAIL\n\nProduction-grade exhaustive leak validation belongs to the production gate,\nbut the development design must already have a safe explicit policy.\n\n==================================================\n6. PRIVATE ROUTING\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\nworm-os 10.60.0.10\n ->\nOPNsense\n ->\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nAt minimum verify one intended private destination is reachable.\n\nVerify:\n- no dependency on worm-robot forwarding\n- worm-robot ip_forward remains 0\n\n==================================================\n7. ADMIN ISOLATION\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\n10.77.0.0/24 ADMIN remains isolated from normal worm-os peer.\n\nException allowed by frozen policy:\n\nworm-os -> OPNsense 10.60.0.1:\nTCP 22\nTCP 80\nTCP 443\n\nNo broad access to worm-robot SSH/admin must be introduced.\n\n==================================================\n8. PUBLIC EXPOSURE\n==================================================\n\nVerify OPNsense WAN:\n\n2.28.34.27:22  DENY\n2.28.34.27:80  DENY\n2.28.34.27:443 DENY\n\nUDP/51821 available.\n\nVerify worm-robot public web/OTA policy remains denied.\n\n==================================================\n9. ANDROID SOURCE PREREQUISITES\n==================================================\n\nAudit ex-graphene/Worm OS source READ-ONLY.\n\nDetermine whether development can proceed using existing platform pieces.\n\nAudit:\n\n- kernel WireGuard support\n- VPN framework\n- always-on VPN support\n- lockdown support\n- ConnectivityService\n- NetworkStack/netd\n- boot lifecycle\n- Wi-Fi/mobile handover hooks\n- Keystore\n- SELinux\n- backup exclusion\n- OTA integration points\n- existing Worm system services\n\nDo NOT require a completed system-WireGuard component.\nIts absence is the reason for this development phase.\n\n==================================================\n10. KEY MANAGEMENT DESIGN\n==================================================\n\nDevelopment is allowed only if the proposed design supports:\n\n- unique keypair per device\n- private key generated on device\n- no shared production private key\n- no private key in source\n- no private key in factory image\n- no private key in OTA\n- backup exclusion\n- per-device /32 address\n- public-key enrollment\n- independent peer revocation\n\nIf design requires a shared key:\nP0 FAIL.\n\n==================================================\n11. FAIL-CLOSED DESIGN\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT require native fail-closed implementation to already exist.\n\nInstead confirm a viable design exists for:\n\n- OTA\n- downloads\n- Apps\n- backup\n- private Worm APIs\n\nProduction requirement:\nno public fallback if WireGuard is unavailable.\n\nFor DEVELOPMENT:\ndesign identified = PASS\nno viable design = FAIL\n\n==================================================\n12. SELINUX / PLATFORM SECURITY\n==================================================\n\nConfirm implementation can proceed while preserving:\n\n- SELinux enforcing\n- AVB / Verified Boot\n- Android sandbox\n- Keystore\n- GrapheneOS security hardening\n\nAny proposal requiring:\nSELinux permissive\nroot shell scripts\ndisabled neverallows\nshared plaintext secrets\n\nis a DEVELOPMENT blocker.\n\n==================================================\n13. SECRET SCAN\n==================================================\n\nScan relevant audit/patch docs.\n\nNever print:\n- WireGuard private keys\n- PSKs\n- passwords\n- Hetzner tokens\n- SSH private keys\n- signing keys\n\n==================================================\n14. DEVELOPMENT BLOCKERS\n==================================================\n\nOnly these categories should block DEVELOPMENT:\n\nP0:\n- shared/private key design flaw\n- requirement to weaken Android core security\n- topology fundamentally incompatible\n\nP1:\n- no real WG handshake\n- full-tunnel egress not working\n- protected DNS not working\n- unsafe/unknown IPv6 behavior\n- no viable Android integration path\n- unresolved server networking failure\n\nThe following DO NOT block development by themselves:\n\n- native Worm system-WG not implemented yet\n- native fail-closed not implemented yet\n- hardware/device production tests not completed yet\n- production OTA integration not completed yet\n\nThose belong to PRODUCTION GATE.\n\n==================================================\n15. OUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\n/root/WORM-SYSTEM-WIREGUARD-DEVELOPMENT-GATE/\n\nFiles:\n\n00-SUMMARY.md\n01-INFRASTRUCTURE.md\n02-LIVE-WIREGUARD.md\n03-FULL-TUNNEL.md\n04-DNS.md\n05-IPV6.md\n06-PRIVATE-ROUTING.md\n07-ANDROID-PREREQUISITES.md\n08-KEY-MANAGEMENT.md\n09-FAIL-CLOSED-DESIGN.md\n10-SELINUX.md\n11-FINDINGS.md\n12-GATE.md\nfindings.json\n\n==================================================\nFINAL RESULT\n==================================================\n\nPrint exactly one:\n\nREADY FOR WORM-OS SYSTEM WIREGUARD DEVELOPMENT\n\nor\n\nNOT READY FOR WORM-OS SYSTEM WIREGUARD DEVELOPMENT\n\nIf NOT READY:\nlist only the exact P0/P1 blockers.\n\nDo not implement anything after the audit."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a078a3-c15e-7e81-9693-d9f877601739","create_time":1788730392.95305,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a078ab-b5b3-7bb0-a1fd-f5604cf24181","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Perform WORM WIREGUARD COMPLETE RECOVERY AND LIVE VALIDATION.\n\nGOAL:\nMake the existing Android test client successfully establish WireGuard\nwith OPNsense and validate full-tunnel networking.\n\nThis task MAY remediate WireGuard configuration after diagnosis.\n\nDO NOT change the frozen network architecture.\n\n==================================================\nFROZEN NETWORK\n==================================================\n\nOPNsense WAN:\n2.28.34.27\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nOPNsense WireGuard:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nWireGuard UDP:\n51821\n\nAndroid test client:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nAndroid public key:\njxZ2fEyoynXMrhNPZitfKY1kqMZBZ/6bYLbyZb6XCXM=\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nADMIN:\n10.77.0.0/24\n\nExpected worm-os public egress:\n2.28.34.27\n\n==================================================\nPHASE 1 — COMPLETE READ-ONLY DIAGNOSIS\n==================================================\n\nUse private SSH:\nworm-robot -> OPNsense 10.90.10.1\n\nInspect LIVE WireGuard state.\n\nDetermine:\n\n- actual interface name\n- tunnel address\n- listen port\n- live public key\n- configured persistent public/private key relationship\n- Android peer\n- Android AllowedIP\n- number of WireGuard interfaces\n- number of UDP/51821 listeners\n- OPNsense WireGuard service state\n- PF rules for UDP/51821\n- interface assignment\n- NAT state\n- DNS state\n\nNEVER print private keys.\n\nVerify server private key derives to the live public key.\n\nReport only public-key result.\n\n==================================================\nPHASE 2 — CONFIGURATION CONSISTENCY\n==================================================\n\nCompare THREE states:\n\nA. OPNsense persistent config\nB. OPNsense generated/runtime WireGuard config\nC. live wg show state\n\nThey MUST agree on:\n\ninterface:\nwg0\n\naddress:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nlisten:\n51821\n\nserver keypair:\nsame identity\n\nAndroid peer:\njxZ2fEyoynXMrhNPZitfKY1kqMZBZ/6bYLbyZb6XCXM=\n\nAndroid AllowedIP:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nFind:\n- stale keys\n- duplicate instances\n- duplicate peers\n- stale generated config\n- config/runtime mismatch\n- service reload failure\n\n==================================================\nPHASE 3 — REPAIR SERVER IF NECESSARY\n==================================================\n\nIf persistent/runtime/live configuration differs:\n\nBACK UP OPNsense configuration first.\n\nThen make the MINIMUM correction required.\n\nDo NOT regenerate the OPNsense keypair unless the existing keypair is\nactually invalid/inconsistent and cannot safely be repaired.\n\nIf regeneration becomes absolutely necessary:\nSTOP before doing it and report why.\n\nDo not regenerate Android keys.\n\nAfter any correction:\napply/reload WireGuard safely.\n\nVerify:\n\nwg0 = 10.60.0.1/24\nUDP = 51821\npeer = correct Android public key\nAllowedIP = 10.60.0.10/32\n\n==================================================\nPHASE 4 — PRODUCE EXACT ANDROID CONFIG\n==================================================\n\nAfter determining the authoritative LIVE OPNsense public key, output\nan Android configuration checklist.\n\nDo NOT include Android PrivateKey.\n\nOutput:\n\nANDROID_ADDRESS=10.60.0.10/32\nANDROID_DNS=10.60.0.1\nOPNSENSE_PUBLIC_KEY=<AUTHORITATIVE LIVE KEY>\nENDPOINT=2.28.34.27:51821\nALLOWED_IPS=0.0.0.0/0\nKEEPALIVE=25\n\nAlso verify that the Android public key expected by OPNsense remains:\n\njxZ2fEyoynXMrhNPZitfKY1kqMZBZ/6bYLbyZb6XCXM=\n\n==================================================\nPHASE 5 — LIVE HANDSHAKE TEST\n==================================================\n\nStart simultaneous monitoring:\n\n- tcpdump WAN UDP/51821\n- wg show wg0\n- relevant PF counters/logs\n\nThen print clearly:\n\n========================================\nENABLE ANDROID WIREGUARD NOW\n========================================\n\nWait/monitor for 90 seconds.\n\nDo not change configuration during this window.\n\nRecord:\n\nWAN UDP packets\nPF pass/block\nlatest handshake\nRX\nTX\n\n==================================================\nPHASE 6 — IF PACKETS ARRIVE BUT NO HANDSHAKE\n==================================================\n\nIf WAN packets > 0 but handshake = NEVER:\n\nDo NOT touch NAT or DNS.\n\nInvestigate only WireGuard authentication/runtime:\n\n- server key identity\n- client public key\n- correct UDP socket\n- correct instance\n- peer lookup\n- persistent/runtime mismatch\n- WireGuard logs\n- PF delivery to local socket\n\nIdentify exact cause.\n\nApply a correction ONLY if evidence proves it.\n\nThen repeat the 90-second live test.\n\n==================================================\nPHASE 7 — HANDSHAKE PASS\n==================================================\n\nOnce handshake becomes RECENT:\n\nrequire:\n\nRX > 0\nTX > 0\n\nThen proceed downstream.\n\nDo not continue to NAT/DNS tests until handshake passes.\n\n==================================================\nPHASE 8 — FIREWALL / FULL-TUNNEL\n==================================================\n\nVerify OPNsense permits worm-os Internet egress.\n\nSource:\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nOutbound NAT:\nWAN address\n\nExpected:\n\n10.60.0.10\n ->\nwg0\n ->\nOPNsense\n ->\nWAN NAT\n ->\n2.28.34.27\n ->\nInternet\n\nPreserve explicit security denies.\n\n==================================================\nPHASE 9 — DNS\n==================================================\n\nVerify resolver:\n\n10.60.0.1:53\n\nfrom worm-os WireGuard network.\n\nVerify:\n- UDP DNS\n- TCP DNS where applicable\n- resolver response\n- no WAN resolver exposure\n\nIf DNS fails after handshake:\nrepair DNS/firewall only after identifying exact cause.\n\n==================================================\nPHASE 10 — INTERNET TEST\n==================================================\n\nWith Android WireGuard ENABLED:\n\nverify Internet connectivity.\n\nExpected public IPv4:\n\n2.28.34.27\n\nUse real client-path evidence where available.\n\nDo not infer PASS solely from NAT configuration.\n\n==================================================\nPHASE 11 — PRIVATE NETWORK\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\nAndroid\n10.60.0.10\n ->\nOPNsense\n ->\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nTest intended private connectivity.\n\nPreserve:\n\nworm-robot ip_forward=0\n\nOPNsense remains the router.\n\n==================================================\nPHASE 12 — MANAGEMENT POLICY\n==================================================\n\nFrozen policy requires from worm-os:\n\n10.60.0.1:22  ALLOW\n10.60.0.1:80  ALLOW\n10.60.0.1:443 ALLOW\n\nWAN remains:\n\n2.28.34.27:22  DENY\n2.28.34.27:80  DENY\n2.28.34.27:443 DENY\n\nDo not accidentally expose management publicly.\n\n==================================================\nPHASE 13 — IPV6\n==================================================\n\nVerify Android IPv6 cannot bypass the protected path.\n\nUntil dedicated tunneled IPv6 is implemented:\nuse the existing explicit no-leak policy.\n\nDo not break the working IPv4 tunnel while investigating IPv6.\n\n==================================================\nPHASE 14 — REGRESSION\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\nworm-robot:\n162.55.6.173 unchanged\n10.90.20.10 unchanged\nFireHOL active\nADMIN WG unchanged\nip_forward=0\n\nOPNsense:\n2.28.34.27 unchanged\n10.90.10.1 unchanged\n10.60.0.1 unchanged\nWAN management closed\n\nNo frozen topology changes.\n\n==================================================\nPHASE 15 — PERSISTENCE\n==================================================\n\nOnce working, verify the configuration is persistent.\n\nDo not leave a fix applied only through:\n\nwg set\n\nor another runtime-only command.\n\nThe authoritative OPNsense configuration must contain the working\nWireGuard state.\n\nDo not reboot merely to prove this unless necessary.\n\n==================================================\nPHASE 16 — SECRET SAFETY\n==================================================\n\nNever print/store:\n\n- OPNsense WireGuard private key\n- Android WireGuard private key\n- PSK\n- Hetzner API token\n- passwords\n- SSH private keys\n\nPublic keys are allowed.\n\nRun secret scan.\n\n==================================================\nOUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\n/root/WORM-WIREGUARD-RECOVERY/\n\nInclude:\n\n00-SUMMARY.md\n01-DIAGNOSIS.md\n02-OPNSENSE-WG.md\n03-ANDROID-CONFIG.md\n04-HANDSHAKE.md\n05-FIREWALL.md\n06-NAT.md\n07-DNS.md\n08-INTERNET.md\n09-PRIVATE-ROUTING.md\n10-IPV6.md\n11-PERSISTENCE.md\n12-REGRESSION.md\n13-FINDINGS.md\n14-FINAL.md\nROLLBACK.md\nfindings.json\n\n==================================================\nFINAL GATE\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT claim success unless:\n\nPASS server key consistency\nPASS Android peer\nPASS AllowedIP 10.60.0.10/32\nPASS UDP/51821\nPASS WAN packets reach OPNsense\nPASS PF permits WireGuard\nPASS handshake RECENT\nPASS RX > 0\nPASS TX > 0\nPASS DNS\nPASS Internet through tunnel\nPASS public IPv4 = 2.28.34.27\nPASS private routing\nPASS OPNsense WG management policy\nPASS WAN management denied\nPASS IPv6 no-leak policy\nPASS persistent configuration\nPASS frozen topology\nPASS no secrets\n\nFinal output must be:\n\nWORM WIREGUARD: PASS\n\nor:\n\nWORM WIREGUARD: FAIL\n\nIf FAIL:\nstate the ONE CURRENT FAILING STAGE and exact evidence.\n\nDo not start system-WireGuard Android development automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a078ab-b598-7153-bc4c-18858deaf5b8","create_time":1788730914.2276084,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a078b3-ceb3-7ee3-bd57-afd92f162b7a","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-DIAG-WIREGUARD-OPNSENSE-MINIMAL.\n\nTEMPORARY DIAGNOSTIC PATCH ONLY.\n\nFROZEN ADDRESSES MUST NOT CHANGE:\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC:  162.55.6.173\nworm-robot PRIVATE: 10.90.20.10\n\nOPNsense WAN:       2.28.34.27\nOPNsense PRIVATE:   10.90.10.1\nOPNsense WG:        10.60.0.1/24\n\nworm-os test peer:  10.60.0.10/32\n\nADMIN WG:           10.77.0.0/24\n\nWireGuard port:     UDP/51821\n\n==================================================\n1. WORM-ROBOT: LEAVE NETWORKING ALONE\n==================================================\n\nDO NOT change:\n- 162.55.6.173\n- 10.90.20.10\n- VLAN 4000\n- MTU 1400\n- private route\n- ADMIN WireGuard\n- SSH configuration\n- default route\n\nnet.ipv4.ip_forward MUST remain 0.\n\nFireHOL:\nDO NOT disable the entire public firewall.\n\nTemporarily remove/disable ONLY rules that could interfere with\ntraffic on the PRIVATE interface enp9s0.4000 if evidence shows they\nblock the diagnostic private path.\n\nPreserve PUBLIC protection:\n- SSH policy\n- block/default-deny policy\n- no public Web/OTA/download exposure\n\nBefore any FireHOL change:\nbackup configuration and record current effective state.\n\n==================================================\n2. OPNsense BACKUP\n==================================================\n\nBack up the complete persistent OPNsense configuration before changes.\n\nRecord:\n- wg0\n- peers\n- PF rules\n- NAT\n- DNS\n- interface assignments\n\nNever print private keys.\n\n==================================================\n3. MINIMAL WIREGUARD SERVER\n==================================================\n\nEnsure exactly ONE worm-os WireGuard instance exists:\n\nwg0\n10.60.0.1/24\nUDP 51821\n\nEnsure exactly ONE Android test peer:\n\nPublicKey:\njxZ2fEyoynXMrhNPZitfKY1kqMZBZ/6bYLbyZb6XCXM=\n\nAllowedIP:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nDo not regenerate either side's keys.\n\nDetermine authoritative OPNsense public key from LIVE wg0.\n\nNever print private key.\n\n==================================================\n4. TEMPORARY OPNsense DIAGNOSTIC PF POLICY\n==================================================\n\nKeep WAN default deny.\n\nWAN temporary explicit rule:\n\nALLOW UDP\nsource: any\ndestination: WAN address\nport: 51821\n\nDo NOT open publicly:\nTCP/22\nTCP/80\nTCP/443\n\nFor the worm-os WG interface, temporarily use a SIMPLE diagnostic IPv4\npolicy sufficient for:\n\n- client -> OPNsense WG address\n- client -> DNS\n- client -> Internet through NAT\n- client -> intended 10.90.0.0/16 private test path\n\nDo not create public management exposure.\n\nKeep 10.77.0.0/24 ADMIN isolated.\n\nDocument every temporary rule.\n\n==================================================\n5. NAT\n==================================================\n\nUse one clear outbound NAT rule:\n\nsource:\n10.60.0.0/24\n\ninterface:\nWAN\n\ntranslation:\nWAN address\n\nDo not add duplicate NAT rules.\n\nDo not NAT private traffic between:\n10.60.0.0/24\nand\n10.90.0.0/16\n\n==================================================\n6. DNS\n==================================================\n\nEnsure OPNsense resolver can answer:\n\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.60.0.1 TCP/UDP 53\n\nDo NOT expose DNS on WAN.\n\n==================================================\n7. LIVE HANDSHAKE TEST FIRST\n==================================================\n\nBefore testing DNS/NAT/private services:\n\nmonitor:\n- WAN UDP/51821\n- wg show wg0\n- PF pass/block\n\nPrint:\n\nENABLE ANDROID WIREGUARD NOW\n\nThen monitor for 90 seconds.\n\nPASS requires:\nWAN packets > 0\nHANDSHAKE RECENT\nRX > 0\nTX > 0\n\nIf handshake remains NEVER:\nSTOP.\n\nDo NOT blame FireHOL, NAT, DNS or worm-robot.\nReport the exact WireGuard authentication/runtime problem.\n\n==================================================\n8. AFTER HANDSHAKE ONLY\n==================================================\n\nOnly after handshake PASS test:\n\n1. 10.60.0.1 reachability\n2. DNS 10.60.0.1\n3. Internet\n4. public IPv4 = 2.28.34.27\n5. private route toward 10.90.0.0/16\n\n==================================================\n9. PUBLIC SECURITY REGRESSION\n==================================================\n\nMust remain:\n\nOPNsense:\n2.28.34.27:22  CLOSED/FILTERED\n2.28.34.27:80  CLOSED/FILTERED\n2.28.34.27:443 CLOSED/FILTERED\n\nworm-robot:\npublic Web/OTA/download remains denied.\n\nDo not expose additional services for diagnosis.\n\n==================================================\n10. ROLLBACK\n==================================================\n\nCreate exact rollback for every temporary OPNsense/FireHOL change.\n\nDo not leave diagnostic broad rules in production.\n\n==================================================\nOUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nPATCH-DIAG-WIREGUARD-OPNSENSE-MINIMAL/\n\nInclude:\n\nREADME.md\nBEFORE.md\nOPNSENSE.md\nWIREGUARD.md\nPF.md\nNAT.md\nDNS.md\nFIREHOL.md\nTESTS.md\nROLLBACK.md\ntest-results.txt\nmodified-files.txt\n\nFinal output:\n\nDIAGNOSTIC WG HANDSHAKE:\nPASS / FAIL\n\nWAN PACKETS:\n<count>\n\nHANDSHAKE:\nRECENT / NEVER\n\nRX:\n<value>\n\nTX:\n<value>\n\nINTERNET:\nPASS / NOT TESTED / FAIL\n\nPUBLIC IPV4:\n<value or NOT TESTED>\n\nPRIVATE ROUTING:\nPASS / NOT TESTED / FAIL\n\nFIREHOL:\nUNCHANGED / PRIVATE-RULES-TEMPORARILY-RELAXED\n\nFROZEN IP TOPOLOGY:\nUNCHANGED / FAIL\n\nDo not start another patch."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a078b3-ce98-7a13-9544-44e5bcd2c9ef","create_time":1788731444.9155874,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a078b7-6d1d-7881-89c1-7dcd4ab29cf6","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-NET-008-DEDICATED-OPNSENSE-PUBLIC-IP.\n\nGOAL:\nGive OPNsense/Worm-OS WireGuard its own dedicated NEW public IPv4,\nwhile leaving worm-robot and the complete private network unchanged.\n\nDO NOT invent an IPv4 address.\n\nFIRST inspect Hetzner resources and determine whether an additional\npublic IPv4/Floating IP/Primary IP suitable for this OPNsense VPS is\nalready allocated or can be correctly attached.\n\nCURRENT:\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nOPNsense CURRENT PUBLIC:\n2.28.34.27\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nOPNsense WG:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nworm-os:\n10.60.0.x/32\n\nPRIVATE NETWORK:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\n==================================================\nABSOLUTE REQUIREMENTS\n==================================================\n\nWORM-ROBOT MUST REMAIN UNCHANGED:\n\nPUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nPRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nFireHOL:\nKEEP ENABLED\n\nPreserve:\n- SSH policy\n- blocklists\n- ADMIN WireGuard\n- public default deny\n- ip_forward=0\n\nDo not route worm-os Internet through worm-robot.\n\n==================================================\nPRIVATE NETWORK MUST REMAIN UNCHANGED\n==================================================\n\nPreserve:\n\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nVLAN:\n4000\n\nMTU:\n1400\n\nDo not modify private addressing or routing unless required only to\nrepair an existing fault.\n\n==================================================\nNEW OPNsense PUBLIC IP\n==================================================\n\nDiscover/allocate the correct Hetzner-supported dedicated IPv4 for\nOPNsense.\n\nCall it:\n\nNEW_OPNSENSE_PUBLIC_IPV4\n\nDo not proceed with cutover until Hetzner confirms the address is\nassigned/routed correctly to OPNsense.\n\nThe new address becomes:\n\nworm-os dedicated public egress IP\nWireGuard public endpoint\n\nWireGuard endpoint:\n\nNEW_OPNSENSE_PUBLIC_IPV4:51821\n\nExpected worm-os Internet egress:\n\nNEW_OPNSENSE_PUBLIC_IPV4\n\n==================================================\nSAFE MIGRATION\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT immediately remove 2.28.34.27.\n\nUse staged migration:\n\n1. provision NEW_OPNSENSE_PUBLIC_IPV4\n2. verify OPNsense receives it\n3. verify routing/gateway\n4. allow UDP/51821 on NEW address\n5. keep TCP 22/80/443 publicly denied\n6. test WireGuard using NEW address\n7. verify handshake\n8. verify DNS\n9. verify NAT\n10. verify Internet egress equals NEW address\n11. verify private routing\n12. only then decide whether 2.28.34.27 can be retired\n\nNo destructive cutover before validation.\n\n==================================================\nOPNsense PUBLIC POLICY\n==================================================\n\nNEW_OPNSENSE_PUBLIC_IPV4:\n\nALLOW:\nUDP/51821 WireGuard\n\nDENY PUBLIC:\nTCP/22\nTCP/80\nTCP/443\nDNS\nWebGUI\nSSH\nall other unsolicited inbound traffic\n\nManagement remains through protected/private paths.\n\n==================================================\nWIREGUARD\n==================================================\n\nPreserve:\n\nOPNsense WG:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nAndroid test peer:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nDo NOT regenerate keys.\n\nOnly change endpoint after NEW public IP is confirmed operational.\n\n==================================================\nNAT\n==================================================\n\nAfter successful migration:\n\n10.60.0.0/24\n ->\nOPNsense WAN\n ->\nNEW_OPNSENSE_PUBLIC_IPV4\n\nExpected public IP from worm-os:\n\nNEW_OPNSENSE_PUBLIC_IPV4\n\nDo not NAT private traffic unnecessarily between:\n\n10.60.0.0/24\nand\n10.90.0.0/16\n\n==================================================\nTESTS\n==================================================\n\nPASS worm-robot 162.55.6.173 unchanged\nPASS FireHOL active\nPASS worm-robot 10.90.20.10 unchanged\nPASS OPNsense 10.90.10.1 unchanged\nPASS private 10.90.0.0/16 unchanged\nPASS OPNsense WG 10.60.0.1 unchanged\nPASS NEW public IPv4 actually assigned by Hetzner\nPASS NEW IPv4 routed to OPNsense\nPASS UDP/51821 reaches NEW IPv4\nPASS real WireGuard handshake\nPASS RX/TX\nPASS DNS\nPASS Internet through WG\nPASS public egress = NEW_OPNSENSE_PUBLIC_IPV4\nPASS NEW IP TCP/22 denied\nPASS NEW IP TCP/80 denied\nPASS NEW IP TCP/443 denied\nPASS no secrets\n\nIf no suitable additional IPv4 is currently available:\n\nSTOP.\n\nPrint:\nNEW OPNsense PUBLIC IPv4 REQUIRED\n\nDo not fabricate an address.\nDo not break the current 2.28.34.27 configuration.\n\n==================================================\nOUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nPATCH-NET-008-DEDICATED-OPNSENSE-PUBLIC-IP/\n\nInclude:\nREADME.md\nHETZNER.md\nMIGRATION.md\nOPNSENSE.md\nWIREGUARD.md\nNAT.md\nFIREWALL.md\nTESTS.md\nROLLBACK.md\nmodified-files.txt\ntest-results.txt\n\nFinal output:\n\nPATCH-NET-008 STATUS:\nPASS / PENDING-IP / BLOCKED\n\nWORM-ROBOT PUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nWORM-ROBOT PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nFIREHOL:\nACTIVE\n\nOPNSENSE PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nOPNSENSE NEW PUBLIC:\n<actual address or REQUIRED>\n\nOPNSENSE WG:\n10.60.0.1\n\nWIREGUARD ENDPOINT:\n<NEW-IP>:51821\n\nFROZEN PRIVATE NETWORK:\nUNCHANGED\n\nDo not start another patch automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a078b3-ce98-7a13-9544-44e5bcd2c9ef","create_time":1788731682.0779321,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a078bb-7585-71d2-8ef9-51889ab41667","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-NET-BASE-PRIVATE-WORM.\n\nPURPOSE:\nBuild and certify the minimum stable private networking foundation\nbefore doing any additional Worm OS networking work.\n\nDO NOT:\n- change public IPs\n- add another public IP\n- modify OTA\n- modify WebUSB\n- modify Apps\n- modify Android/ex-graphene\n- redesign addressing\n- remove FireHOL\n- enable worm-robot forwarding\n- regenerate WireGuard keys\n\n==================================================\nFROZEN PRIVATE FOUNDATION\n==================================================\n\nWireGuard client / future worm-os:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nWireGuard network:\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nOPNsense WireGuard:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nOPNsense private:\n10.90.10.1\n\nHetzner private network:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nworm-robot private:\n10.90.20.10\n\nworm-robot private interface:\nenp9s0.4000\n\nVLAN:\n4000\n\nMTU:\n1400\n\n==================================================\nTARGET CONNECTIVITY\n==================================================\n\nMust work:\n\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.60.0.1\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.10.1\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.20.10\n\nSSH must work:\n\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.20.10 TCP/22\n\nThis SSH path is PRIVATE ONLY.\n\nDo not require worm-os/client to use:\n\n162.55.6.173:22\n\nfor this private SSH path.\n\n==================================================\n1. WIREGUARD\n==================================================\n\nAudit existing OPNsense WireGuard.\n\nExpected:\n\nwg0:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nlisten:\nUDP/51821\n\nreal test peer:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nDo not regenerate keys.\n\nVerify:\n- peer present\n- correct /32\n- handshake\n- RX/TX\n\nNever print private keys.\n\n==================================================\n2. OPNsense PRIVATE ROUTING\n==================================================\n\nEnsure OPNsense routes:\n\n10.60.0.0/24\n<->\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nDo not NAT this private-to-private traffic unless technically required\nand explicitly justified.\n\nPreferred:\nROUTED PRIVATE TRAFFIC.\n\nOPNsense remains the router.\n\n==================================================\n3. WORM-ROBOT PRIVATE NETWORK\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\nenp9s0.4000 UP\nVLAN 4000\nMTU 1400\n10.90.20.10/24\n\nExpected private route:\n10.90.0.0/16 via existing Hetzner private path.\n\nVerify return path for:\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nIf an explicit route via OPNsense/Hetzner private gateway is required,\nimplement the minimum persistent route.\n\nDo not change the public default route.\n\n==================================================\n4. FIREHOL\n==================================================\n\nFireHOL MUST remain active.\n\nPUBLIC policy remains unchanged.\n\nAdd/verify a PRIVATE rule permitting:\n\nSOURCE:\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nDESTINATION:\nworm-robot private address 10.90.20.10\n\nSERVICE:\nSSH TCP/22\n\nThis rule must apply to the PRIVATE path only.\n\nDo not create:\n10.60.0.0/24 -> any ACCEPT\n\nDo not make enp9s0.4000 trusted-any.\n\nKeep blocklist/public protection.\n\n==================================================\n5. SSH\n==================================================\n\nVerify sshd is able to accept connections on:\n\n10.90.20.10:22\n\nPrefer explicit private/admin bindings where compatible with the\nexisting recovery design.\n\nDo not disable the existing public recovery/admin SSH during this patch.\n\nDo not weaken:\n- authentication\n- root policy\n- key requirements\n- crypto policy\n\nThis patch changes NETWORK REACHABILITY, not SSH authentication policy.\n\n==================================================\n6. OPNsense FIREWALL\n==================================================\n\nAllow from:\n\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nto:\n\n10.90.20.10 TCP/22\n\nAllow required private routing traffic for testing.\n\nKeep ADMIN network:\n10.77.0.0/24\n\nseparate unless explicitly required.\n\nDo not open worm-robot SSH through OPNsense WAN.\n\n==================================================\n7. NO PUBLIC DEPENDENCY\n==================================================\n\nPrivate SSH test must succeed without connecting to:\n\n162.55.6.173\n\nExpected path:\n\nclient 10.60.0.10\n  ->\nWireGuard\n  ->\nOPNsense 10.60.0.1\n  ->\nOPNsense private routing\n  ->\nworm-robot 10.90.20.10:22\n\n==================================================\n8. LIVE TESTS\n==================================================\n\nWith real WireGuard client connected:\n\nTEST A:\nreach 10.60.0.1\n\nTEST B:\nreach 10.90.10.1\n\nTEST C:\nreach 10.90.20.10\n\nTEST D:\nTCP connect 10.90.20.10:22\n\nTEST E:\nreal SSH authentication to:\n\nssh <authorized-admin-user>@10.90.20.10\n\nDo not alter authentication credentials for the test.\n\nTEST F:\nverify WireGuard handshake remains RECENT.\n\nTEST G:\nverify RX/TX increase.\n\n==================================================\n9. RETURN PATH\n==================================================\n\nExplicitly verify return traffic:\n\nworm-robot 10.90.20.10\n ->\nOPNsense\n ->\n10.60.0.10\n\nDo not accept a one-way connectivity test as PASS.\n\nUse routing/firewall counters where useful.\n\n==================================================\n10. SECURITY TESTS\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\n- worm-robot ip_forward = 0\n- FireHOL active\n- no broad PRIVATE allow\n- no new public SSH exposure\n- OPNsense WAN management unchanged\n- no private key leak\n- no public-IP dependency for private SSH\n\n==================================================\n11. PERSISTENCE\n==================================================\n\nEnsure all required configuration is persistent:\n\nOPNsense:\n- WG instance\n- peer\n- private route\n- PF rules\n\nworm-robot:\n- VLAN\n- private IP\n- private route\n- FireHOL rule\n- SSH private reachability\n\nDo not leave essential configuration as runtime-only commands.\n\n==================================================\n12. OUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nPATCH-NET-BASE-PRIVATE-WORM/\n\nInclude:\n\nREADME.md\nARCHITECTURE.md\nWIREGUARD.md\nOPNSENSE.md\nWORM-ROBOT.md\nFIREHOL.md\nSSH.md\nROUTING.md\nACL-MATRIX.md\nTESTS.md\nPERSISTENCE.md\nSECURITY.md\nROLLBACK.md\nmodified-files.txt\ntest-results.txt\n\n==================================================\nPASS GATE\n==================================================\n\nDo not mark PASS unless a REAL WireGuard client verifies:\n\nPASS 10.60.0.10 -> 10.60.0.1\nPASS 10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.10.1\nPASS 10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.20.10\nPASS 10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.20.10:22\nPASS real private SSH connection\nPASS WireGuard handshake RECENT\nPASS WireGuard RX/TX\nPASS bidirectional return path\nPASS FireHOL active\nPASS worm-robot ip_forward=0\nPASS no broad private allow\nPASS no new public exposure\nPASS persistent configuration\nPASS frozen private addressing\nPASS no secrets\n\nFinal output must be exactly:\n\nPRIVATE WORM FOUNDATION: PASS\n\nor:\n\nPRIVATE WORM FOUNDATION: FAIL\n\nIf FAIL:\nstate the exact failing hop:\n\nCLIENT -> WG\nWG -> OPNsense\nOPNsense -> PRIVATE\nPRIVATE -> WORM-ROBOT\nFIREHOL\nSSH\nRETURN PATH\n\nDo not start OTA/Web/Android work automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a078b3-ce98-7a13-9544-44e5bcd2c9ef","create_time":1788731946.3732257,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a078c8-2b47-7d13-9380-096921e6e982","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-WG-RESET-TEST-IDENTITY.\n\nPURPOSE:\nRepair ONLY the failing CLIENT -> WG hop.\n\nThe private Worm foundation already has the required server-side\nrouting/firewall rules.\n\nDO NOT modify:\n- 10.60.0.0/24\n- OPNsense 10.60.0.1\n- OPNsense private 10.90.10.1\n- worm-robot 10.90.20.10\n- VLAN 4000\n- private routing\n- FireHOL private rules\n- worm-robot SSH\n- worm-robot public IP\n- worm-robot ip_forward\n- NAT/DNS unless handshake succeeds first\n\n==================================================\n1. BACKUP\n==================================================\n\nBackup persistent OPNsense configuration.\n\nRecord current:\n- wg0\n- UDP/51821\n- current server public key\n- current Android peer\n- PF rules\n- private routing\n\nNever print private keys.\n\n==================================================\n2. REMOVE ONLY THE BROKEN TEST WG IDENTITY\n==================================================\n\nRemove the current Android test peer from the worm-os WG instance.\n\nDo NOT remove:\n- wg0 address\n- UDP/51821\n- PF rules\n- private routing\n\nGenerate a NEW OPNsense WireGuard keypair only if necessary to create\na clean known-good identity.\n\nIf regenerated:\n- store private key only in OPNsense persistent configuration\n- never print it\n- output ONLY the new public key\n\n==================================================\n3. CLEAN ANDROID TEST PEER\n==================================================\n\nWe will create a fresh Android WireGuard tunnel manually.\n\nReserve:\n\nCLIENT IP:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nGenerate a NEW keypair ON THE ANDROID DEVICE.\n\nDo not generate or store the Android private key on worm-robot,\nOPNsense, Codex workspace or Git.\n\nSTOP after server preparation and print:\n\nOPNSENSE_WG_PUBLIC_KEY=<public key>\nENDPOINT=2.28.34.27:51821\nCLIENT_ADDRESS=10.60.0.10/32\nDNS=10.60.0.1\nALLOWED_IPS=10.60.0.0/24,10.90.0.0/16\nKEEPALIVE=25\n\nIMPORTANT:\nUse SPLIT TUNNEL for this recovery test.\n\nDo NOT use 0.0.0.0/0 yet.\n\nThen print:\n\nWAITING FOR NEW ANDROID PUBLIC KEY\n\nDo not continue until the new Android public key is supplied.\n\n==================================================\n4. AFTER ANDROID PUBLIC KEY IS SUPPLIED\n==================================================\n\nCreate exactly one peer:\n\nPublicKey:\n<NEW ANDROID PUBLIC KEY>\n\nAllowedIP:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nPersist configuration.\n\nVerify:\n- exactly one test peer\n- exactly one AllowedIP\n- wg0 10.60.0.1/24\n- UDP/51821\n\n==================================================\n5. LIVE TEST\n==================================================\n\nStart:\n\ntcpdump WAN UDP/51821\nwg show wg0\n\nPrint:\n\nENABLE NEW ANDROID WIREGUARD TUNNEL NOW\n\nMonitor 90 seconds.\n\nPASS requires:\n\nWAN packets > 0\nlatest handshake RECENT\nRX > 0\nTX > 0\n\n==================================================\n6. ONLY AFTER HANDSHAKE\n==================================================\n\nTest in this exact order:\n\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.60.0.1\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.10.1\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.20.10\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.20.10:22\n\nThen real SSH:\n\nssh <authorized-user>@10.90.20.10\n\nDo not test full-tunnel Internet yet.\n\n==================================================\n7. PASS GATE\n==================================================\n\nPASS server identity consistent\nPASS new Android peer\nPASS UDP/51821\nPASS handshake RECENT\nPASS RX/TX\nPASS OPNsense WG reachable\nPASS OPNsense private reachable\nPASS worm-robot private reachable\nPASS private SSH reachable\nPASS FireHOL remains active\nPASS worm-robot ip_forward=0\nPASS frozen private network unchanged\nPASS persistence\nPASS no secrets\n\nFinal:\n\nPRIVATE WORM FOUNDATION: PASS\n\nor:\n\nPRIVATE WORM FOUNDATION: FAIL\n\nDo not modify OTA/Web/Android system integration."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a078c7-1d4b-7603-ad6b-ab6cf2605cc8","create_time":1788732779.3357832,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a078cf-6f3b-7502-aa3d-11021e5f55ca","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"IMPORTANT NEW LIVE EVIDENCE:\n\nThe Android WireGuard tunnel is currently working for Internet access\nafter changing Android DNS from 10.60.0.1 to 1.1.1.1.\n\nDO NOT reset or regenerate WireGuard keys.\nDO NOT recreate wg0.\nDO NOT recreate the Android peer.\n\nRe-evaluate the previous CLIENT -> WG failure.\n\nWhile Android WireGuard remains ENABLED:\n\n1. Run:\n   wg show wg0\n\nVerify:\n- latest handshake\n- RX\n- TX\n\n2. Verify the Android peer:\n   10.60.0.10/32\n\n3. Verify outbound NAT counters for 10.60.0.0/24.\n\n4. Determine actual client public IPv4.\nExpected:\n2.28.34.27\n\n5. Diagnose ONLY OPNsense DNS for the worm-os WG network.\n\nExpected resolver:\n10.60.0.1\n\nCheck:\n- Unbound listening interfaces\n- 10.60.0.1 UDP/53\n- 10.60.0.1 TCP/53\n- PF rules from 10.60.0.0/24\n- Unbound ACL for 10.60.0.0/24\n- resolver logs\n- DNSSEC/upstream resolution\n\nDo not expose DNS on WAN.\n\n6. If WireGuard handshake is RECENT and RX/TX > 0:\nreclassify CLIENT -> WG as PASS.\n\n7. If public IPv4 is 2.28.34.27:\nreclassify FULL TUNNEL as PASS.\n\n8. Keep 1.1.1.1 only as TEMPORARY diagnostic DNS.\n\nRepair 10.60.0.1 DNS only after identifying the exact failure.\n\nTarget final state:\n\nAndroid:\nAddress = 10.60.0.10/32\nDNS = 10.60.0.1\n\nWireGuard:\nEndpoint = 2.28.34.27:51821\nAllowedIPs = 0.0.0.0/0\n\nExpected:\nHANDSHAKE=RECENT\nRX>0\nTX>0\nPUBLIC_IPV4=2.28.34.27\nDNS_10.60.0.1=PASS\n\nDo not alter frozen private addressing.\nDo not modify FireHOL.\nDo not modify worm-robot networking."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a078cf-6efb-76d2-8316-b62caa101f69","create_time":1788733255.4832513,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a078d5-6139-7423-858f-8bc8b58306fd","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"TEMPORARILY STOP FULL-TUNNEL DEBUGGING.\n\nWe are establishing the PRIVATE WORM FOUNDATION first.\n\nAndroid test client configuration is now:\n\nAddress:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nAllowedIPs:\n10.60.0.0/24,10.90.0.0/16\n\nDNS:\n1.1.1.1\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51821\n\nDo NOT modify:\n- WireGuard keys\n- public IPs\n- private addressing\n- NAT\n- DNS\n- FireHOL public policy\n\nGoal:\nmake split-tunnel private connectivity work first.\n\nWhile Android WireGuard is ENABLED, verify:\n\n1. wg show wg0\n\nRequired:\nHANDSHAKE=RECENT\nRX>0\nTX>0\n\nIf handshake is not RECENT:\nSTOP.\nDiagnose WireGuard only.\n\nIf handshake passes, test routing in this exact order:\n\nA:\nAndroid -> 10.60.0.1\nExpected PASS.\n\nB:\nAndroid -> 10.90.10.1\nExpected PASS.\n\nC:\nAndroid -> 10.90.20.10\nExpected PASS.\n\nD:\nAndroid -> 10.90.20.10 TCP/22\nExpected PASS.\n\nE:\nReal SSH:\nAndroid -> 10.90.20.10:22\nExpected PASS with existing authorized credentials.\n\nTrace failures using:\nOPNsense PF logs/counters\nrouting tables\npacket captures on wg0/private interface\nworm-robot FireHOL counters\n\nDo not create broad allow rules.\n\nRequired private policy:\n\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.90.20.10 TCP/22 ALLOW\n\nReturn traffic:\n10.90.20.10 -> 10.60.0.0/24\nmust have a valid route through OPNsense/private infrastructure.\n\nworm-robot:\nnet.ipv4.ip_forward=0\n\nOPNsense remains the router.\n\nDo not touch full-tunnel NAT until:\n\nPASS handshake\nPASS 10.60.0.1\nPASS 10.90.10.1\nPASS 10.90.20.10\nPASS 10.90.20.10:22\nPASS real private SSH\n\nFinal output:\n\nPRIVATE_WG_HANDSHAKE=\nOPNSENSE_WG=\nOPNSENSE_PRIVATE=\nWORM_ROBOT_PRIVATE=\nWORM_ROBOT_PRIVATE_SSH=\nRETURN_PATH=\nPRIVATE_WORM_FOUNDATION=\n\nDo not start another patch."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a078d5-60fa-7542-bdd5-ace16a98dae5","create_time":1788733645.1131108,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a078d8-52d0-7f00-a587-77cb93574485","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"TEMPORARILY STOP FULL-TUNNEL DEBUGGING.\n\nWe are establishing the PRIVATE WORM FOUNDATION first.\n\nAndroid test client configuration is now:\n\nAddress:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nAllowedIPs:\n10.60.0.0/24,10.90.0.0/16\n\nDNS:\n1.1.1.1\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51821\n\nDo NOT modify:\n- WireGuard keys\n- public IPs\n- private addressing\n- NAT\n- DNS\n- FireHOL public policy\n\nGoal:\nmake split-tunnel private connectivity work first.\n\nWhile Android WireGuard is ENABLED, verify:\n\n1. wg show wg0\n\nRequired:\nHANDSHAKE=RECENT\nRX>0\nTX>0\n\nIf handshake is not RECENT:\nSTOP.\nDiagnose WireGuard only.\n\nIf handshake passes, test routing in this exact order:\n\nA:\nAndroid -> 10.60.0.1\nExpected PASS.\n\nB:\nAndroid -> 10.90.10.1\nExpected PASS.\n\nC:\nAndroid -> 10.90.20.10\nExpected PASS.\n\nD:\nAndroid -> 10.90.20.10 TCP/22\nExpected PASS.\n\nE:\nReal SSH:\nAndroid -> 10.90.20.10:22\nExpected PASS with existing authorized credentials.\n\nTrace failures using:\nOPNsense PF logs/counters\nrouting tables\npacket captures on wg0/private interface\nworm-robot FireHOL counters\n\nDo not create broad allow rules.\n\nRequired private policy:\n\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.90.20.10 TCP/22 ALLOW\n\nReturn traffic:\n10.90.20.10 -> 10.60.0.0/24\nmust have a valid route through OPNsense/private infrastructure.\n\nworm-robot:\nnet.ipv4.ip_forward=0\n\nOPNsense remains the router.\n\nDo not touch full-tunnel NAT until:\n\nPASS handshake\nPASS 10.60.0.1\nPASS 10.90.10.1\nPASS 10.90.20.10\nPASS 10.90.20.10:22\nPASS real private SSH\n\nFinal output:\n\nPRIVATE_WG_HANDSHAKE=\nOPNSENSE_WG=\nOPNSENSE_PRIVATE=\nWORM_ROBOT_PRIVATE=\nWORM_ROBOT_PRIVATE_SSH=\nRETURN_PATH=\nPRIVATE_WORM_FOUNDATION=\n\nDo not start another patch."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a078d8-52b5-7550-b0f6-b02f3a6c15c2","create_time":1788733838.0329626,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a078d9-135e-7881-8bca-84586f537b45","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Repair ONLY the missing Android peer on the existing OPNsense wg0.\n\nDO NOT change:\n- wg0 server keypair\n- 10.60.0.1/24\n- UDP/51821\n- PF\n- NAT\n- DNS\n- FireHOL\n- routing\n- public IPs\n- private IPs\n\nCurrent problem:\nwg0 is UP but has ZERO peers.\n\nProvision persistently the existing Android peer:\n\nName:\nworm-os-test-01\n\nPublicKey:\njxZ2fEyoynXMrhNPZitfKY1kqMZBZ/6bYLbyZb6XCXM=\n\nAllowedIP:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nIMPORTANT:\nThis must be written to the authoritative persistent OPNsense\nconfiguration, not only applied with `wg set`.\n\nNever print any private key.\n\nAfter applying/reloading safely verify:\n\nwg show wg0\nwg show wg0 peers\nwg show wg0 allowed-ips\n\nExpected:\n\nPEER_PRESENT=YES\nANDROID_PUBLIC_KEY=\njxZ2fEyoynXMrhNPZitfKY1kqMZBZ/6bYLbyZb6XCXM=\n\nALLOWED_IP=\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nThen verify the authoritative OPNsense configuration contains the peer\nand that it will survive service restart/reboot.\n\nSTOP after peer provisioning and print:\n\nPEER RESTORED - ENABLE ANDROID WIREGUARD NOW\n\nThen monitor for 90 seconds:\n\n- wg show wg0\n- WAN UDP/51821\n- handshake\n- RX/TX\n\nAndroid is currently using SPLIT TUNNEL:\n\nAddress:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nAllowedIPs:\n10.60.0.0/24,10.90.0.0/16\n\nDNS:\n1.1.1.1\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51821\n\nDo NOT change Android configuration during this test.\n\nIf handshake becomes RECENT:\n\ntest sequentially:\n\n10.60.0.1\n10.90.10.1\n10.90.20.10\n10.90.20.10:22\n\nThen test real private SSH.\n\nFinal report:\n\nPEER_PERSISTENT=\nHANDSHAKE=\nRX=\nTX=\nOPNSENSE_WG=\nOPNSENSE_PRIVATE=\nWORM_ROBOT_PRIVATE=\nWORM_ROBOT_SSH=\nPRIVATE_WORM_FOUNDATION=\n\nDo not start full-tunnel work."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a078d9-1344-7c62-a6a1-55e72d5a92d8","create_time":1788733887.3266647,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a078dd-4800-7550-ae75-7ca0fdf5f511","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"ll"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a078dd-47e8-7e20-bfb7-72cf2715cba3","create_time":1788734162.9444172,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a078dd-740a-7ab1-ac32-fa1ebcf631f8","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Repair ONLY the missing Android peer on the existing OPNsense wg0.\n\nDO NOT change:\n- wg0 server keypair\n- 10.60.0.1/24\n- UDP/51821\n- PF\n- NAT\n- DNS\n- FireHOL\n- routing\n- public IPs\n- private IPs\n\nCurrent problem:\nwg0 is UP but has ZERO peers.\n\nProvision persistently the existing Android peer:\n\nName:\nworm-os-test-01\n\nPublicKey:\njxZ2fEyoynXMrhNPZitfKY1kqMZBZ/6bYLbyZb6XCXM=\n\nAllowedIP:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nIMPORTANT:\nThis must be written to the authoritative persistent OPNsense\nconfiguration, not only applied with `wg set`.\n\nNever print any private key.\n\nAfter applying/reloading safely verify:\n\nwg show wg0\nwg show wg0 peers\nwg show wg0 allowed-ips\n\nExpected:\n\nPEER_PRESENT=YES\nANDROID_PUBLIC_KEY=\njxZ2fEyoynXMrhNPZitfKY1kqMZBZ/6bYLbyZb6XCXM=\n\nALLOWED_IP=\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nThen verify the authoritative OPNsense configuration contains the peer\nand that it will survive service restart/reboot.\n\nSTOP after peer provisioning and print:\n\nPEER RESTORED - ENABLE ANDROID WIREGUARD NOW\n\nThen monitor for 90 seconds:\n\n- wg show wg0\n- WAN UDP/51821\n- handshake\n- RX/TX\n\nAndroid is currently using SPLIT TUNNEL:\n\nAddress:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nAllowedIPs:\n10.60.0.0/24,10.90.0.0/16\n\nDNS:\n1.1.1.1\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51821\n\nDo NOT change Android configuration during this test.\n\nIf handshake becomes RECENT:\n\ntest sequentially:\n\n10.60.0.1\n10.90.10.1\n10.90.20.10\n10.90.20.10:22\n\nThen test real private SSH.\n\nFinal report:\n\nPEER_PERSISTENT=\nHANDSHAKE=\nRX=\nTX=\nOPNSENSE_WG=\nOPNSENSE_PRIVATE=\nWORM_ROBOT_PRIVATE=\nWORM_ROBOT_SSH=\nPRIVATE_WORM_FOUNDATION=\n\nDo not start full-tunnel work."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a078dd-73ef-71d3-bb43-8308259677f2","create_time":1788734174.218259,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07a1d-82e8-7f43-8803-1d7c70d2db08","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Perform WIREGUARD KEY IDENTITY FINAL CHECK.\n\nDO NOT change any configuration.\nDO NOT regenerate keys.\nDO NOT modify PF/NAT/DNS/FireHOL/routes.\n\nKnown facts:\n\nOPNsense wg0:\n10.60.0.1/24\nUDP/51821\npeer present\nAllowedIP 10.60.0.10/32\n\nAndroid packets reach OPNsense WAN from:\n78.211.55.136\n\nBut:\nHANDSHAKE=NEVER\nRX=0\nTX=0\n\n==================================================\nSERVER IDENTITY\n==================================================\n\nOn OPNsense obtain:\n\nSERVER_LIVE_PUBLIC=\"$(wg show wg0 public-key)\"\n\nIndependently derive the public key from the private key currently\nactually loaded/configured for wg0.\n\nNEVER print the private key.\n\nCompare byte-for-byte.\n\nReport:\n\nSERVER_LIVE_PUBLIC=<public key>\nSERVER_PRIVATE_DERIVES_TO=<public key>\nSERVER_KEYPAIR_MATCH=YES/NO\n\nAlso compare the persistent OPNsense WireGuard instance identity with\nthe live identity.\n\nReport:\n\nPERSISTENT_SERVER_PUBLIC=<public key>\nPERSISTENT_LIVE_MATCH=YES/NO\n\n==================================================\nANDROID PEER IDENTITY\n==================================================\n\nOPNsense currently expects Android public key:\n\njxZ2fEyoynXMrhNPZitfKY1kqMZBZ/6bYLbyZb6XCXM=\n\nConfirm byte-for-byte:\n\nOPNSENSE_ANDROID_PEER=<public key>\nEXPECTED_ANDROID_PUBLIC=\njxZ2fEyoynXMrhNPZitfKY1kqMZBZ/6bYLbyZb6XCXM=\n\nANDROID_PEER_MATCH=YES/NO\n\n==================================================\nSERVER PUBLIC KEY FOR ANDROID\n==================================================\n\nOutput ONE authoritative value that must be entered on Android:\n\nANDROID_MUST_USE_SERVER_PUBLIC_KEY=<SERVER_LIVE_PUBLIC>\n\nDo not rely on previous documentation.\nUse the current live wg0 identity.\n\n==================================================\nOTHER AUTHENTICATION CHECKS\n==================================================\n\nVerify whether a PresharedKey is configured on either the OPNsense\npeer or expected by persistent configuration.\n\nDo not print PSK.\n\nReport only:\n\nPSK_CONFIGURED=YES/NO\n\nVerify:\n- exactly one peer for 10.60.0.10/32\n- no duplicate public key\n- no duplicate AllowedIP\n- no stale wg instance\n- exactly one UDP/51821 WireGuard listener\n\n==================================================\nRESULT\n==================================================\n\nPrint:\n\nSERVER_KEYPAIR_MATCH=\nPERSISTENT_LIVE_MATCH=\nANDROID_PEER_MATCH=\nPSK_CONFIGURED=\nPEER_COUNT=\nWG_INSTANCE_COUNT=\nANDROID_MUST_USE_SERVER_PUBLIC_KEY=\n\nDIAGNOSIS=\n\nDo not remediate."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07a1d-8298-7012-9018-e5657ff727e9","create_time":1788755149.5444264,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07a2b-edb4-7d61-9b6b-be9cf5b3079a","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Replace ONLY the Android test peer public key on the existing\nOPNsense worm-os WireGuard instance.\n\nNEW ANDROID PUBLIC KEY:\nsMg3tbte/Jjzpc7GNT4oC+Y2wxrPOFMuC0dtxDDT91E=\n\nAllowedIP:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nKeep unchanged:\n- wg0\n- 10.60.0.1/24\n- UDP/51821\n- OPNsense server keypair\n- PF\n- NAT\n- DNS\n- routing\n- FireHOL\n- all public/private IPs\n\nRemove the OLD Android peer:\njxZ2fEyoynXMrhNPZitfKY1kqMZBZ/6bYLbyZb6XCXM=\n\nCreate exactly ONE persistent peer using the NEW public key.\n\nVerify persistent + live state:\n\nPEER_COUNT=1\nPEER_PUBLIC_KEY=sMg3tbte/Jjzpc7GNT4oC+Y2wxrPOFMuC0dtxDDT91E=\nALLOWED_IP=10.60.0.10/32\n\nNever print private keys.\n\nThen start simultaneous monitoring:\n- wg show wg0\n- WAN UDP/51821\n- handshake\n- RX/TX\n\nPrint clearly:\n\nENABLE WORM-TEST NOW\n\nMonitor for 90 seconds after activation.\n\nIf handshake becomes RECENT:\ntest in order:\n\n10.60.0.1\n10.90.10.1\n10.90.20.10\n10.90.20.10:22\n\nThen test real private SSH.\n\nReport:\n\nNEW_PEER_PERSISTENT=\nWAN_PACKETS=\nHANDSHAKE=\nRX=\nTX=\nOPNSENSE_WG=\nOPNSENSE_PRIVATE=\nWORM_ROBOT_PRIVATE=\nWORM_ROBOT_SSH=\nPRIVATE_WORM_FOUNDATION=\n\nDo NOT start full-tunnel work.\nDo NOT change anything else."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07a2b-ed9a-7161-b4bf-a0b6d857f727","create_time":1788756094.3880289,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07a32-050b-7622-b41a-c2b309ff7caf","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Perform OPNsense WireGuard LOCAL-DELIVERY diagnosis.\n\nDO NOT modify:\n- Android\n- WireGuard keys\n- peer\n- AllowedIPs\n- NAT\n- DNS\n- FireHOL\n- private routing\n- IP addresses\n\nKnown evidence:\n\nNEW Android identity tested.\nPeer persistent = PASS.\nWAN receives UDP/51821 packets.\nHandshake = NEVER.\nRX/TX = 0.\n\nTherefore diagnose OPNsense local UDP delivery/runtime.\n\n==================================================\n1. VERIFY ACTUAL WAN ADDRESS\n==================================================\n\nDetermine the LIVE IPv4 configuration of vtnet0.\n\nReport:\n\nVTNET0_IPV4=\nWAN_CONFIGURED_IPV4=\nDEFAULT_GATEWAY=\n\nVerify that 2.28.34.27 is actually a LOCAL address owned by OPNsense\nand not merely routed/NATed externally.\n\n==================================================\n2. UDP SOCKET\n==================================================\n\nIdentify the actual kernel socket listening on UDP/51821.\n\nVerify:\n- process/kernel owner\n- IPv4 bind\n- IPv6 bind\n- local address\n- port\n- association with wg0\n\nReport:\n\nUDP51821_BOUND=\nUDP51821_LOCAL_ADDRESS=\nUDP51821_OWNER=\n\n==================================================\n3. PF RULE PATH\n==================================================\n\nInspect the effective PF rules, not only config.xml.\n\nFind the exact rule matching incoming:\n\nsource Android public IP\ndestination 2.28.34.27\nUDP/51821\n\nDetermine:\nPASS / BLOCK\n\nCheck rule counters.\n\nAlso inspect:\nreply-to\nroute-to\nrdr\nNAT\nfloating rules\ninterface-group rules\n\nLook specifically for an incorrect reply-to or routing decision.\n\n==================================================\n4. SIMULTANEOUS CAPTURE\n==================================================\n\nPrint:\n\nENABLE WORM-TEST NOW\n\nThen capture simultaneously for 90 seconds:\n\nvtnet0 UDP/51821\npflog0 matching UDP/51821\nwg0 traffic\nwg show wg0\n\nRecord timestamps and packet counts.\n\nDetermine:\n\nWAN_RECEIVED=\nPF_BLOCKED=\nPF_PASSED=\nWG0_RECEIVED=\nHANDSHAKE=\n\n==================================================\n5. RESPONSE CHECK\n==================================================\n\nFor every incoming WireGuard initiation determine whether OPNsense\nsends any UDP response back to the Android public source.\n\nReport:\n\nWG_INITIATIONS_IN=\nWG_RESPONSES_OUT=\n\nIf:\nINITIATIONS > 0\nRESPONSES = 0\n\ndetermine whether the packet reached the WireGuard socket.\n\nIf responses leave OPNsense but Android receives none:\nclassify as RETURN_PATH.\n\n==================================================\n6. WIREGUARD IMPLEMENTATION\n==================================================\n\nAudit OPNsense WireGuard implementation/runtime:\n\n- kernel module status\n- interface creation\n- service/plugin status\n- generated configuration\n- runtime configuration\n- system logs\n- WireGuard-specific errors\n\nVerify wg0 is not merely present but operational.\n\nDo not regenerate keys.\n\n==================================================\n7. CONTROL TEST\n==================================================\n\nIf technically safe, perform a LOCAL diagnostic against the WireGuard\nlistener to establish whether UDP/51821 is handled by the expected\nWireGuard interface.\n\nDo not create a second production peer or expose another port.\n\n==================================================\n8. REPORT EXACT LAYER\n==================================================\n\nReturn exactly:\n\nWAN_LOCAL_IP=\nUDP_SOCKET=\nPF_51821=\nREPLY_TO=\nWG_INITIATIONS_IN=\nWG_RESPONSES_OUT=\nWG0_HANDSHAKE=\nWG_RUNTIME=\nFAILING_LAYER=\n\nPossible FAILING_LAYER values:\n\nPF_BLOCK\nLOCAL_DELIVERY\nWIREGUARD_RUNTIME\nWIREGUARD_AUTH\nRETURN_PATH\nUNKNOWN\n\nDo not remediate yet."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07a32-04ef-7ac0-9dc0-f2ecc644bc56","create_time":1788756493.5790443,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07a35-a0bf-7660-b5e6-609670a8a95a","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Perform WIREGUARD AUTHENTICATION CONTROL TEST.\n\nKnown state:\n\nOPNsense:\nWAN 2.28.34.27\nWG 10.60.0.1/24\nUDP 51821\nPF PASS\n\nAndroid NEW public key:\nsMg3tbte/Jjzpc7GNT4oC+Y2wxrPOFMuC0dtxDDT91E=\n\nAndroid AllowedIP on OPNsense:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nOPNsense authoritative public key:\nvJtONCHbxMj3d7SAsPD5OFkTD7NcAoNgjIfmTcinE04=\n\nObserved:\nWG_INITIATIONS_IN=13\nWG_RESPONSES_OUT=0\nHANDSHAKE=NEVER\n\nDO NOT modify the production wg0 configuration yet.\nDO NOT modify PF/NAT/DNS/FireHOL/private routing.\n\nGoal:\nindependently determine whether the authentication failure is caused\nby the Android configuration or the OPNsense WireGuard implementation.\n\n1. Reconfirm LIVE server public key and current Android peer.\n\n2. Verify the NEW Android public key is exactly:\n\nsMg3tbte/Jjzpc7GNT4oC+Y2wxrPOFMuC0dtxDDT91E=\n\n3. Confirm PSK is absent on both expected server peer configuration\nand generated runtime configuration.\n\nNever print private keys.\n\n4. Create an ISOLATED temporary WireGuard control test on OPNsense\nusing a DIFFERENT interface and DIFFERENT UDP port.\n\nDo not alter wg0.\n\nExample conceptual test:\nwg-test\n10.61.0.1/30\ntemporary UDP port\n\nGenerate temporary test server/client identities securely.\n\nNever print private keys in reports/logs.\n\n5. From a controlled client available on worm-robot or another safe\nhost, attempt a WireGuard handshake to the temporary OPNsense test\ninterface.\n\nThe purpose is ONLY to prove whether OPNsense WireGuard can\ncryptographically complete a handshake.\n\nDo not route production traffic through this interface.\n\n6. If CONTROL HANDSHAKE succeeds:\n\nOPNsense WireGuard implementation = GOOD\n\nThen classify the remaining problem as:\nANDROID_CLIENT_AUTH_CONFIGURATION\n\n7. If CONTROL HANDSHAKE also fails despite correct temporary keys:\n\nclassify:\nOPNSENSE_WIREGUARD_RUNTIME\n\nand investigate kernel/module/plugin implementation.\n\n8. Remove the temporary test interface, peer, keys, port and firewall\nrule after the control test.\n\nVerify production wg0 is unchanged.\n\n9. Report:\n\nPRODUCTION_WG0_UNCHANGED=\nCONTROL_WG_HANDSHAKE=\nCONTROL_RX=\nCONTROL_TX=\nOPNSENSE_WG_IMPLEMENTATION=\nANDROID_PRODUCTION_HANDSHAKE=\nAUTH_FAILURE_SIDE=\nTEMP_TEST_REMOVED=\n\nDo not change production keys automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07a35-a089-7ab3-83c0-412716e162a6","create_time":1788756730.0476522,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07a3a-af56-76e0-a4e7-d8b6197915fb","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Replace ONLY the Android test peer public key on the existing OPNsense wg0.\n\nNEW ANDROID PUBLIC KEY:\np7popPkCV9gcdMyI0PgejMnwKFIftjw8WTudloKgjAQ=\n\nAllowedIP:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nKeep unchanged:\n- wg0\n- 10.60.0.1/24\n- UDP/51821\n- OPNsense server keypair\n- PF\n- NAT\n- DNS\n- routing\n- FireHOL\n- all public/private IPs\n\nRemove the previous Android peer:\nsMg3tbte/Jjzpc7GNT4oC+Y2wxrPOFMuC0dtxDDT91E=\n\nCreate exactly ONE persistent peer using the NEW public key.\n\nVerify:\nPEER_COUNT=1\nPEER_PUBLIC_KEY=p7popPkCV9gcdMyI0PgejMnwKFIftjw8WTudloKgjAQ=\nALLOWED_IP=10.60.0.10/32\n\nNever print private keys.\n\nThen start live monitoring:\n- wg show wg0\n- WAN UDP/51821\n- handshake\n- RX/TX\n\nPrint:\n\nENABLE WORM-CLEAN NOW\n\nMonitor for 90 seconds.\n\nIf handshake becomes RECENT, immediately test:\n\n10.60.0.1\n10.90.10.1\n10.90.20.10\n10.90.20.10:22\n\nThen real private SSH.\n\nReport:\n\nPEER_PERSISTENT=\nWAN_PACKETS=\nHANDSHAKE=\nRX=\nTX=\nOPNSENSE_WG=\nOPNSENSE_PRIVATE=\nWORM_ROBOT_PRIVATE=\nWORM_ROBOT_SSH=\nPRIVATE_WORM_FOUNDATION=\n\nDo NOT change anything else.\nDo NOT start full-tunnel work."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07a3a-af3b-7f42-89c4-b389c2f30f69","create_time":1788757061.462341,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07a3d-7f60-7152-8060-f7fd0613e384","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Perform WIREGUARD EXTERNAL CONTROL TEST.\n\nDo NOT modify production wg0.\nDo NOT modify FireHOL/private routing/NAT/DNS.\n\nCreate a TEMPORARY independent WireGuard instance on OPNsense:\n\ninterface:\nwg-ext-test\n\nnetwork:\n10.61.0.1/30\n\nlisten:\nUDP/51822\n\nGenerate a temporary SERVER keypair securely.\nNever print the private key.\n\nCreate ONE peer after I provide a fresh Android public key.\n\nAdd a TEMPORARY WAN PF rule:\n\nALLOW UDP/51822\ndestination OPNsense WAN only\n\nDo not open TCP 22/80/443.\n\nOutput only:\n\nEXT_TEST_SERVER_PUBLIC_KEY=<public key>\nEXT_TEST_ENDPOINT=2.28.34.27:51822\nEXT_TEST_CLIENT_ADDRESS=10.61.0.2/32\n\nThen STOP and print:\n\nWAITING FOR EXT-TEST ANDROID PUBLIC KEY\n\nThe Android test profile will use:\n\nAddress:\n10.61.0.2/32\n\nPeer PublicKey:\n<EXT_TEST_SERVER_PUBLIC_KEY>\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51822\n\nAllowedIPs:\n10.61.0.0/30\n\nPersistentKeepalive:\n25\n\nDNS:\nnone required\n\nAfter receiving Android public key, configure:\n\npeer public key:\n<ANDROID EXT TEST PUBLIC KEY>\n\nAllowedIP:\n10.61.0.2/32\n\nThen start simultaneous capture:\n\nWAN UDP/51822\nwg show wg-ext-test\n\nPrint:\n\nENABLE EXT-TEST NOW\n\nPASS requires:\n\nWAN_PACKETS > 0\nHANDSHAKE = RECENT\nRX > 0\nTX > 0\n10.61.0.1 reachable from Android\n\nInterpretation:\n\nIf EXTERNAL_CONTROL_HANDSHAKE=PASS:\nAndroid WireGuard implementation is GOOD.\nProduction wg0/51821 configuration is the problem.\n\nIf WAN packets arrive but EXTERNAL_CONTROL_HANDSHAKE=NEVER:\nthe common factor is Android/external-WAN authentication path and must\nbe investigated before touching private routing.\n\nAfter test:\nremove wg-ext-test\nremove UDP/51822 PF rule\nremove temporary keys/config\n\nVerify:\nproduction wg0 unchanged\nUDP/51822 closed again\n\nReport:\n\nEXTERNAL_WAN_PACKETS=\nEXTERNAL_CONTROL_HANDSHAKE=\nEXTERNAL_RX=\nEXTERNAL_TX=\nANDROID_WIREGUARD_IMPLEMENTATION=\nPRODUCTION_WG0=\nDIAGNOSIS=\nTEMPORARY_TEST_REMOVED=\n\nDo not remediate production wg0 automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07a3d-7f45-72e3-917f-c14c7379a206","create_time":1788757245.7928443,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07a41-840b-7dd2-b8bb-78d5ec44311b","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Continue the existing WIREGUARD EXTERNAL CONTROL TEST.\n\nANDROID EXT-TEST PUBLIC KEY:\n\nzSfM+Ti9I2V9Z+aCZgPzgoSCD/w3UQx7NVp10wRRMA0=\n\nConfigure exactly ONE temporary peer on:\n\nwg-ext-test\n\nPeer PublicKey:\nzSfM+Ti9I2V9Z+aCZgPzgoSCD/w3UQx7NVp10wRRMA0=\n\nAllowedIP:\n10.61.0.2/32\n\nKeep unchanged:\n\nwg-ext-test server:\n10.61.0.1/30\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51822\n\nProduction wg0:\nDO NOT TOUCH\n\nProduction/private networking:\nDO NOT TOUCH\n\nVerify the temporary peer is live.\n\nThen start simultaneous monitoring:\n\n- WAN UDP/51822\n- wg show wg-ext-test\n- RX/TX\n- outbound UDP responses\n\nPrint clearly:\n\nENABLE EXT-TEST NOW\n\nThen monitor for 90 seconds.\n\nPASS requires:\n\nWAN_PACKETS > 0\nHANDSHAKE = RECENT\nRX > 0\nTX > 0\n\nIf handshake succeeds, verify from Android:\n\n10.61.0.1 reachable\n\nDo not test NAT, DNS or Internet.\nThis is strictly an external WireGuard authentication control test.\n\nReport:\n\nEXT_PEER_PRESENT=\nWAN_PACKETS=\nHANDSHAKE=\nRX=\nTX=\nWG_RESPONSES_OUT=\n10_61_0_1_REACHABLE=\nEXTERNAL_CONTROL_TEST=\nDIAGNOSIS=\n\nInterpretation:\n\nIf HANDSHAKE=RECENT:\nANDROID_WIREGUARD=PASS\nOPNSENSE_EXTERNAL_WIREGUARD=PASS\n\nThen the fault is specific to production wg0/51821 configuration.\n\nIf packets arrive but HANDSHAKE=NEVER:\nthe failure is common to Android -> external OPNsense WireGuard and\nmust be investigated at that layer.\n\nAfter collecting the result, DO NOT remove the temporary test yet.\nWait for explicit instruction so the result can be reviewed first.\n\nNever print private keys."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07a41-83f1-79a3-9f43-2deab2c63d6b","create_time":1788757509.1311953,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07a44-46ae-7622-8354-b5e6b720fe0e","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-WG-REBUILD-PRODUCTION-FROM-KNOWN-GOOD.\n\nKNOWN-GOOD EVIDENCE:\n\nTemporary external WireGuard:\nUDP/51822\nHANDSHAKE=RECENT\nRX/TX>0\nAndroid external WireGuard authentication=PASS\nOPNsense external WireGuard=PASS\n\nTherefore:\nDo NOT modify worm-robot.\nDo NOT modify FireHOL.\nDo NOT modify Hetzner private networking.\nDo NOT modify 10.90.0.0/16.\nDo NOT debug Android further.\n\nThe failure is isolated to production wg0 / UDP 51821.\n\n==================================================\nGOAL\n==================================================\n\nRebuild the production worm-os WireGuard instance using the\nKNOWN-GOOD temporary wg-ext-test configuration pattern.\n\nPreserve production addressing:\n\nOPNsense WG:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nAndroid:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nProduction port:\nUDP/51821\n\nAndroid current public key:\np7popPkCV9gcdMyI0PgejMnwKFIftjw8WTudloKgjAQ=\n\n==================================================\n1. BACKUP FIRST\n==================================================\n\nBackup complete OPNsense config.\n\nExport/document WITHOUT secrets:\n\n- production wg0 settings\n- production peer\n- interface assignment\n- PF rules\n- generated runtime configuration\n\nKeep wg-ext-test available as known-good reference during rebuild.\n\nNever print private keys.\n\n==================================================\n2. COMPARE KNOWN-GOOD VS PRODUCTION\n==================================================\n\nCompare wg-ext-test and production wg0 field-by-field:\n\n- instance configuration\n- interface assignment\n- enabled state\n- listen port\n- key handling\n- peer structure\n- generated config\n- PF association\n- interface groups\n- normalization/options\n- gateway/reply-to behavior\n- OPNsense WireGuard plugin fields\n\nIdentify every difference other than:\naddress\nport\nkeys\npeer AllowedIP\n\nDocument differences.\n\n==================================================\n3. REBUILD PRODUCTION INSTANCE\n==================================================\n\nRecreate the production Worm WireGuard instance CLEANLY using the\nsame configuration structure that made wg-ext-test work.\n\nProduction target:\n\ninterface:\nwg0\n\naddress:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nlisten:\n51821\n\npeer:\np7popPkCV9gcdMyI0PgejMnwKFIftjw8WTudloKgjAQ=\n\nAllowedIP:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nPrefer preserving the existing production server keypair if it can be\ncleanly attached to the rebuilt instance.\n\nIf the existing server identity itself prevents a clean rebuild:\nSTOP before regenerating it and report.\n\nDo not silently change server public key.\n\n==================================================\n4. PERSISTENCE\n==================================================\n\nMake the rebuilt instance authoritative in OPNsense persistent config.\n\nNo runtime-only wg set fix.\n\nApply through normal OPNsense configuration/service generation.\n\nVerify:\n\nexactly one production instance\nexactly one production peer\nexactly one 10.60.0.10/32 AllowedIP\nUDP/51821\n10.60.0.1/24\n\n==================================================\n5. LIVE TEST\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT remove wg-ext-test yet.\n\nStart monitoring production:\n\nWAN UDP/51821\nwg show wg0\nPF\n\nPrint:\n\nENABLE WORM-CLEAN PRODUCTION NOW\n\nAndroid worm-clean configuration remains:\n\nAddress:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nPeer server public key:\nCURRENT PRODUCTION SERVER PUBLIC KEY\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51821\n\nAllowedIPs:\n10.60.0.0/24,10.90.0.0/16\n\nDNS:\n1.1.1.1\n\nKeepalive:\n25\n\nMonitor 90 seconds.\n\nRequired:\n\nWAN_PACKETS>0\nHANDSHAKE=RECENT\nRX>0\nTX>0\n\n==================================================\n6. PRIVATE FOUNDATION TEST\n==================================================\n\nONLY after production handshake PASS test:\n\n10.60.0.1\n10.90.10.1\n10.90.20.10\n10.90.20.10:22\n\nThen verify real private SSH where credentials already permit it.\n\nTrace any failure without broad allow rules.\n\n==================================================\n7. CLEANUP TEMP TEST\n==================================================\n\nONLY after production wg0 handshake PASS:\n\nremove:\nwg-ext-test\nUDP/51822 PF rule\ntemporary peer\ntemporary keys/config\n\nVerify:\nUDP/51822 CLOSED again.\n\nIf production still fails:\nKEEP the known-good temporary test intact for comparison.\nDo not destroy evidence.\n\n==================================================\n8. REGRESSION\n==================================================\n\nVerify unchanged:\n\nworm-robot:\n162.55.6.173\n10.90.20.10\nFireHOL active\nip_forward=0\n\nOPNsense:\n2.28.34.27\n10.90.10.1\n\nPrivate:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nWAN TCP:\n22 DENY\n80 DENY\n443 DENY\n\n==================================================\nFINAL\n==================================================\n\nReport:\n\nPRODUCTION_REBUILT=\nPRODUCTION_SERVER_PUBLIC_KEY=\nPRODUCTION_PEER=\nPRODUCTION_WAN_PACKETS=\nPRODUCTION_HANDSHAKE=\nPRODUCTION_RX=\nPRODUCTION_TX=\n\nOPNSENSE_WG_PRIVATE=\nOPNSENSE_PRIVATE=\nWORM_ROBOT_PRIVATE=\nWORM_ROBOT_PRIVATE_SSH=\n\nTEMP_TEST_REMOVED=\nFROZEN_PRIVATE_NETWORK=\nFIREHOL=\n\nPRIVATE_WORM_FOUNDATION=\nPASS / FAIL\n\nDo not start full-tunnel/NAT/DNS/OTA/system-WireGuard work."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07a44-4693-7c71-9cfe-289237de3844","create_time":1788757690.03052,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07a49-fa1c-72e3-8609-f575a3c0a67c","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Create WORM-PRIVATE-IP-BACKLOG-AUDIT.\n\nAUDIT ONLY.\nDO NOT remediate anything.\nDO NOT change WireGuard.\nDO NOT change routes.\nDO NOT change PF.\nDO NOT change NAT/DNS.\nDO NOT change public/private IPs.\nDO NOT reboot.\n\nFireHOL may currently be TEMPORARILY STOPPED for diagnosis.\nRecord its state accurately; do not start/stop it during this audit.\n\n==================================================\nFROZEN PRIVATE NETWORK\n==================================================\n\nworm-os WG:\n10.60.0.0/24\n\ntest device:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nOPNsense WG:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nHetzner PRIVATE:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nworm-robot VLAN:\n4000\n\nMTU:\n1400\n\nADMIN WG:\n10.77.0.0/24\n\n==================================================\nBACKLOG 01 — WIREGUARD FOUNDATION\n==================================================\n\nRecord:\n\n- production WG interface\n- production UDP port\n- server public key consistency\n- peer count\n- peer /32\n- handshake\n- RX/TX\n- persistence\n\nStatus:\nPASS / FAIL / PENDING\n\nUse the known-good UDP/51822 test as evidence where appropriate.\n\nDo not print private keys.\n\n==================================================\nBACKLOG 02 — OPNsense WG ADDRESS\n==================================================\n\nRequired:\n\nworm-os 10.60.0.10\n ->\n10.60.0.1\n\nTest:\nICMP where permitted\nTCP 22\nTCP 80\nTCP 443\n\nRecord actual results.\n\n==================================================\nBACKLOG 03 — OPNsense PRIVATE ADDRESS\n==================================================\n\nRequired:\n\nworm-os 10.60.0.10\n ->\nOPNsense 10.90.10.1\n\nVerify bidirectional routing.\n\nNo public-IP dependency.\n\n==================================================\nBACKLOG 04 — WORM-ROBOT PRIVATE\n==================================================\n\nRequired:\n\nworm-os 10.60.0.10\n ->\nOPNsense\n ->\nworm-robot 10.90.20.10\n\nTest reachability.\n\nVerify traffic actually uses the private path.\n\n==================================================\nBACKLOG 05 — PRIVATE SSH\n==================================================\n\nRequired:\n\n10.60.0.10\n ->\n10.90.20.10:22\n\nTest:\n- TCP/22\n- real SSH authentication where existing credentials permit\n\nDo not change SSH authentication.\n\n==================================================\nBACKLOG 06 — RETURN PATH\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\n10.90.20.10\n ->\nOPNsense/private network\n ->\n10.60.0.10\n\nCheck routing and captures/counters.\n\nNo one-way PASS accepted.\n\n==================================================\nBACKLOG 07 — WORM-ROBOT NETWORK\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\nPRIVATE IP:\n10.90.20.10\n\ninterface:\nenp9s0.4000\n\nVLAN:\n4000\n\nMTU:\n1400\n\nprivate route:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nsource selection:\n10.90.20.10\n\nip_forward:\n0\n\n==================================================\nBACKLOG 08 — OPNsense PRIVATE ROUTING\n==================================================\n\nVerify routing between:\n\n10.60.0.0/24\nand\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nPrefer routed private traffic.\n\nIdentify:\n- missing route\n- duplicate route\n- stale route\n- unnecessary private NAT\n\nDo not modify.\n\n==================================================\nBACKLOG 09 — PF\n==================================================\n\nAudit effective OPNsense PF policy for:\n\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.60.0.1\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.90.10.1\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.90.20.10\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.90.20.10:22\n\nIdentify exact pass/block rules.\n\nFlag broad rules.\n\n==================================================\nBACKLOG 10 — FIREHOL\n==================================================\n\nRecord:\n\nFIREHOL_STATE=\nACTIVE / INACTIVE\n\nAudit existing persistent configuration even if service is stopped.\n\nDetermine exactly which rules are required when FireHOL is restored:\n\nPUBLIC:\n- SSH according to admin policy\n- blocklists\n- default deny\n\nPRIVATE:\nallow only explicitly required Worm services.\n\nRequired private SSH:\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.90.20.10 TCP/22\n\nDo not use trusted-any.\n\n==================================================\nBACKLOG 11 — ADMIN ISOLATION\n==================================================\n\nVerify normal worm-os peers do not obtain broad access to:\n\n10.77.0.0/24\n\nDocument explicit exceptions only if frozen policy requires them.\n\n==================================================\nBACKLOG 12 — LISTENERS\n==================================================\n\nInventory worm-robot listeners.\n\nClassify:\n\nPUBLIC_REQUIRED\nPRIVATE_REQUIRED\nADMIN\nLOOPBACK\nUNEXPECTED\n\nFlag:\n0.0.0.0\n[::]\n*:9094\n*:5353\n\nDo not change bindings.\n\n==================================================\nBACKLOG 13 — PUBLIC SEPARATION\n==================================================\n\nVerify private networking does NOT depend on:\n\nworm-robot public 162.55.6.173\n\nPrivate SSH/services must target:\n\n10.90.20.10\n\nRecord any configuration still using public robot IP for internal Worm\ntraffic.\n\n==================================================\nBACKLOG 14 — PERSISTENCE\n==================================================\n\nAudit persistence of:\n\n- OPNsense WG\n- OPNsense peer\n- PF rules\n- OPNsense private interface\n- worm-robot VLAN 4000\n- 10.90.20.10\n- private routes\n- FireHOL configuration\n\nFlag runtime-only state.\n\n==================================================\nBACKLOG 15 — CLEANUP\n==================================================\n\nInventory temporary diagnostic state:\n\n- UDP/51821\n- UDP/51822\n- wg-ext-test\n- temporary PF rules\n- stale peers\n- stale WireGuard instances\n- temporary routes\n- temporary files\n\nDO NOT REMOVE THEM.\n\nClassify each:\nKEEP\nREMOVE_AFTER_VALIDATION\nUNKNOWN\n\n==================================================\nBACKLOG 16 — SECURITY\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\nworm-robot ip_forward=0\nno broad forwarding\nno trusted-any private rule\nno accidental public management exposure\nno private keys in reports\nno PSKs exposed\nno API tokens exposed\n\n==================================================\nOUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\n/root/WORM-PRIVATE-IP-BACKLOG/\n\nFiles:\n\n00-SUMMARY.md\n01-WIREGUARD.md\n02-OPNSENSE-WG.md\n03-OPNSENSE-PRIVATE.md\n04-WORM-ROBOT-PRIVATE.md\n05-PRIVATE-SSH.md\n06-RETURN-PATH.md\n07-ROUTING.md\n08-PF.md\n09-FIREHOL.md\n10-ADMIN-ISOLATION.md\n11-LISTENERS.md\n12-PUBLIC-SEPARATION.md\n13-PERSISTENCE.md\n14-CLEANUP.md\n15-SECURITY.md\n16-BACKLOG.md\nfindings.json\n\n==================================================\nFINAL BACKLOG\n==================================================\n\nPrint a compact table:\n\nID | COMPONENT | STATUS | BLOCKER | NEXT ACTION\n\nOrder unresolved work by:\n\nP0\nP1\nP2\nP3\n\nThen print:\n\nPRIVATE-IP FOUNDATION:\nPASS / PARTIAL / FAIL\n\nFIREHOL CURRENT STATE:\nACTIVE / INACTIVE\n\nSAFE TO RESTORE FIREHOL:\nYES / NO\n\nREADY FOR WORM-OS SYSTEM WG DEVELOPMENT:\nYES / NO\n\nDo not remediate automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07a49-fa01-73c2-85e5-9d74e9254370","create_time":1788758063.6447,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07a4f-6eba-74e2-b3a7-d622c7fc462f","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-NET-REMOVE-OPNSENSE-WIREGUARD-KEEP-PRIVATE.\n\nGOAL:\n\nRemove ALL worm-os WireGuard configuration from OPNsense.\n\nKEEP the complete Hetzner Private IP infrastructure intact.\n\nThis is a simplification/reset patch.\n\n==================================================\nMUST PRESERVE\n==================================================\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nOPNsense PUBLIC:\n2.28.34.27\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nHetzner PRIVATE NETWORK:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nworm-robot:\nenp9s0.4000\nVLAN 4000\nMTU 1400\n\nPreserve all required private routing.\n\nworm-robot:\nnet.ipv4.ip_forward=0\n\nDo NOT redesign any Private IP.\n\n==================================================\nREMOVE OPNsense WIREGUARD\n==================================================\n\nBack up /conf/config.xml FIRST.\n\nInventory current WireGuard state before removal.\n\nThen remove the worm-os WireGuard configuration from OPNsense:\n\n- production wg0\n- 10.60.0.1/24\n- 10.60.0.0/24 network references\n- all worm-os peers\n- 10.60.0.10/32 peer\n- UDP/51821 WireGuard listener/config\n- UDP/51821 WAN PF rule\n- temporary wg-ext-test\n- 10.61.0.0/30\n- UDP/51822\n- UDP/51822 temporary WAN PF rule\n- temporary WireGuard peers\n- temporary WireGuard test keys/configuration\n\nRemove stale generated/runtime WireGuard state only after persistent\nconfiguration has been corrected.\n\nDo NOT print private keys before deletion.\n\n==================================================\nREMOVE WIREGUARD-DEPENDENT RULES\n==================================================\n\nAudit OPNsense for rules created specifically for:\n\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nRemove ONLY WireGuard/worm-os-specific:\n\n- PF rules\n- NAT rules\n- DNS ACLs\n- routes\n- interface assignments\n- aliases\n- management rules\n- temporary diagnostic rules\n\nDo NOT remove anything required by:\n\n10.90.0.0/16\n\n==================================================\nPRIVATE OPNsense\n==================================================\n\nPreserve:\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nVerify from worm-robot:\n\n10.90.20.10 -> 10.90.10.1\n\nRequired:\n- routing PASS\n- private HTTPS PASS\n- private SSH PASS if currently part of management policy\n\nNo public IP should be required for private management.\n\n==================================================\nWORM-ROBOT PRIVATE\n==================================================\n\nPreserve:\n\n10.90.20.10\n\nenp9s0.4000\nVLAN 4000\nMTU 1400\n\nVerify:\n- interface UP\n- private route healthy\n- source selection = 10.90.20.10\n- ip_forward=0\n\n==================================================\nFIREHOL\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT redesign FireHOL in this patch.\n\nIf FireHOL is currently stopped for diagnosis:\nrecord:\n\nFIREHOL_STATE=INACTIVE\n\nDo not automatically enable it until obsolete 10.60.0.0/24 rules have\nbeen audited.\n\nRemove/document only obsolete FireHOL rules whose sole purpose was:\n\n10.60.0.0/24 -> worm-robot\n\nDo not alter public SSH policy or blocklist configuration.\n\n==================================================\nOPNsense PUBLIC SECURITY\n==================================================\n\nAfter WireGuard removal verify:\n\n2.28.34.27 UDP/51821 = CLOSED\n2.28.34.27 UDP/51822 = CLOSED\n\nKeep:\n\n2.28.34.27 TCP/22  = CLOSED/FILTERED\n2.28.34.27 TCP/80  = CLOSED/FILTERED\n2.28.34.27 TCP/443 = CLOSED/FILTERED\n\nNo WireGuard service should remain exposed.\n\n==================================================\nCLEANUP\n==================================================\n\nSearch for stale references to:\n\nwg0\nwg-ext-test\n51821\n51822\n10.60.0.\n10.61.0.\nworm-os-test\nworm-clean\next-test\n\nClassify every occurrence.\n\nRemove live configuration references.\n\nHistorical patch/report documentation may remain as historical evidence;\ndo not rewrite history.\n\n==================================================\nDO NOT REMOVE\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT remove:\n\n10.90.0.0/16\n10.90.10.1\n10.90.20.10\nVLAN 4000\nHetzner private network\nprivate OPNsense management\nworm-robot public IP\nOPNsense public IP\nSSH keys\nFireHOL blocklists\nbackup configuration\n\n==================================================\nTESTS\n==================================================\n\nRequired:\n\nPASS OPNsense 10.90.10.1 exists\nPASS worm-robot 10.90.20.10 exists\nPASS 10.90.20.10 -> 10.90.10.1\nPASS private HTTPS OPNsense\nPASS private SSH OPNsense if configured\nPASS VLAN 4000\nPASS MTU 1400\nPASS private routing\nPASS worm-robot ip_forward=0\n\nRequired WireGuard absence:\n\nPASS no production wg0\nPASS no wg-ext-test\nPASS no 10.60.0.1\nPASS no 10.61.0.1\nPASS no WireGuard peers\nPASS no UDP/51821 listener\nPASS no UDP/51822 listener\nPASS no WAN PF allow 51821\nPASS no WAN PF allow 51822\nPASS no WireGuard NAT rule\nPASS no live 10.60.0.0/24 policy\n\n==================================================\nOUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nPATCH-NET-REMOVE-OPNSENSE-WIREGUARD-KEEP-PRIVATE/\n\nInclude:\n\nREADME.md\nBEFORE.md\nAFTER.md\nPRIVATE-NETWORK.md\nOPNSENSE.md\nWORM-ROBOT.md\nFIREHOL.md\nCLEANUP.md\nTESTS.md\nROLLBACK.md\nmodified-files.txt\ntest-results.txt\n\n==================================================\nFINAL GATE\n==================================================\n\nPrint:\n\nOPNSENSE WIREGUARD:\nREMOVED / FAIL\n\nOPNSENSE PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1 PASS / FAIL\n\nWORM-ROBOT PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10 PASS / FAIL\n\nPRIVATE NETWORK:\n10.90.0.0/16 PASS / FAIL\n\nVLAN 4000:\nPASS / FAIL\n\nFIREHOL:\nACTIVE / INACTIVE\n\nWORM-ROBOT IP_FORWARD:\n0 / FAIL\n\nUDP 51821:\nCLOSED / FAIL\n\nUDP 51822:\nCLOSED / FAIL\n\nPRIVATE-IP FOUNDATION:\nPASS / FAIL\n\nDo not configure a replacement VPN.\nDo not start worm-os system WireGuard.\nDo not modify OTA/Web."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07a4f-6e9f-7622-ba42-e8a9d63f48a4","create_time":1788758421.1783824,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07a55-e3ee-7682-b6e3-5d1bb459be4a","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-NET-ROBOT-BEHIND-OPNSENSE.\n\nGOAL:\nMove worm-robot normal Internet traffic behind OPNsense, while keeping\nthe existing worm-robot public IPv4 only as a restricted recovery path.\n\nDO NOT remove the public IPv4 during this patch.\nDO NOT break SSH recovery.\nDO NOT disable FireHOL permanently.\nDO NOT change the frozen private addressing.\n\n==================================================\nFROZEN ADDRESSES\n==================================================\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nOPNsense PUBLIC:\n2.28.34.27\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nHetzner private network:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nvSwitch:\n85469\n\nVLAN:\n4000\n\nworm-robot private interface:\nenp9s0.4000\n\nMTU:\n1400\n\n==================================================\nTARGET ARCHITECTURE\n==================================================\n\n                     INTERNET\n                        |\n                 OPNsense PUBLIC\n                   2.28.34.27\n                        |\n                 OPNsense FW/NAT\n                   10.90.10.1\n                        |\n             Hetzner Private Network\n                  10.90.0.0/16\n                        |\n                    vSwitch\n                    VLAN 4000\n                        |\n                  worm-robot\n                  10.90.20.10\n\nRECOVERY ONLY:\n\nInternet\n   |\n162.55.6.173\n   |\nFireHOL\n   |\nSSH recovery only\n\n==================================================\n1. AUDIT FIRST\n==================================================\n\nRecord current worm-robot:\n\n- public interface\n- public IPv4\n- private interface\n- private IPv4\n- default route\n- private routes\n- policy rules\n- DNS\n- FireHOL\n- nftables/iptables\n- listening sockets\n- wg/admin state if still present\n- current SSH path\n- active SSH sessions\n\nRecord current OPNsense:\n\n- WAN\n- private interface\n- routes\n- outbound NAT\n- PF rules\n- DNS\n- gateways\n\nDo not change anything until current state is documented.\n\n==================================================\n2. SAFETY / RECOVERY\n==================================================\n\nPreserve:\n\nworm-robot public IPv4:\n162.55.6.173\n\nPreserve public default route initially.\n\nPreserve current SSH recovery path.\n\nFireHOL must protect the public IP.\n\nBefore changing routing:\n- create backup of /etc/network/interfaces\n- backup interfaces.d\n- backup FireHOL config\n- backup route/rule state\n- prepare rollback script\n- prepare automatic rollback timer if safe\n\nDo not rely on a single remote SSH session.\n\n==================================================\n3. OPNsense AS ROBOT GATEWAY\n==================================================\n\nConfigure OPNsense to route/NAT worm-robot private traffic.\n\nSource:\n10.90.20.10/32\n\nOutbound interface:\nOPNsense WAN\n\nTranslation:\nWAN address 2.28.34.27\n\nTarget:\n\nworm-robot\n10.90.20.10\n   ->\nOPNsense\n   ->\nNAT\n   ->\n2.28.34.27\n   ->\nInternet\n\nDo not NAT private-to-private traffic unnecessarily.\n\n==================================================\n4. OPNsense FIREWALL\n==================================================\n\nAllow from worm-robot private:\n\n10.90.20.10\n ->\nInternet\n\nonly as required.\n\nDo not create a broad trusted private network if avoidable.\n\nInbound from Internet to worm-robot private remains DENY unless a later\ndedicated service-publishing patch explicitly adds rules.\n\nDo not publish:\n- Web\n- OTA\n- downloads\n- Apps\n- Git\n- backup\n- databases\n- management\n\n==================================================\n5. WORM-ROBOT ROUTING\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT immediately replace the public default route.\n\nFirst create a staged/policy-routing test so selected traffic from\n10.90.20.10 exits through OPNsense.\n\nUse the correct Hetzner private routing model already proven for\n10.90.0.0/16.\n\nDetermine the correct next-hop toward OPNsense/private network from\nactual live configuration.\n\nDo not invent a gateway.\n\nTest selected destination(s) first.\n\n==================================================\n6. POLICY ROUTING PHASE\n==================================================\n\nCreate a dedicated routing table for OPNsense egress.\n\nExample logical design only:\n\ntable worm-opnsense\n\nsource:\n10.90.20.10\n\ndefault:\nvia private OPNsense/Hetzner path\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDerive the actual route from the live Hetzner network.\nDo not blindly set 10.90.10.1 as an L2 gateway if the network is routed\nthrough Hetzner's virtual gateway.\n\nVerify:\nip route get <public-test-ip> from 10.90.20.10\n\nExpected:\nprivate path toward OPNsense.\n\n==================================================\n7. TEST EGRESS BEFORE CUTOVER\n==================================================\n\nUsing traffic explicitly sourced from 10.90.20.10, verify:\n\n- DNS\n- HTTPS\n- public IPv4 observation\n- return traffic\n\nExpected public IPv4:\n2.28.34.27\n\nUse at least two independent HTTPS IP-check endpoints.\n\nDo not mark PASS based only on route/NAT config.\n\n==================================================\n8. DNS\n==================================================\n\nVerify worm-robot DNS still works when using OPNsense egress.\n\nDo not create a DNS dependency that breaks recovery.\n\nDocument whether DNS remains:\n- local resolver\n- OPNsense\n- external resolver\n\nDo not expose resolver publicly.\n\n==================================================\n9. FIREHOL ROLE\n==================================================\n\nFireHOL remains on worm-robot.\n\nPUBLIC interface:\n162.55.6.173\n\nTarget:\ndefault deny inbound\n\nAllow only:\n- SSH recovery according to current admin policy\n- established/related\n- explicitly required admin service if documented\n\nKeep blocklists.\n\nDeny public:\n- Web\n- OTA\n- downloads\n- Apps\n- backup\n- internal APIs\n\nPRIVATE interface:\n10.90.20.10\n\nDo not mark trusted-any.\n\n==================================================\n10. SSH RECOVERY\n==================================================\n\nPublic SSH on:\n162.55.6.173:22\n\nremains available during migration.\n\nDo not change SSH authentication policy.\n\nPrivate SSH on:\n10.90.20.10:22\n\nmay remain available according to private/admin policy.\n\nDo not expose private SSH through OPNsense WAN.\n\n==================================================\n11. NO ROUTER ROLE FOR WORM-ROBOT\n==================================================\n\nworm-robot must remain:\n\nnet.ipv4.ip_forward=0\n\nDo not turn worm-robot into a transit router.\n\nOPNsense is the gateway/router.\n\n==================================================\n12. CONTROLLED CUTOVER\n==================================================\n\nOnly after policy-routing test PASS:\n\nswitch normal outbound worm-robot traffic to OPNsense.\n\nPreserve a host-specific/public recovery route so:\n\nSSH responses for sessions arriving on 162.55.6.173\n\ncontinue to leave through the public interface and original Hetzner\ngateway.\n\nUse source-based policy routing if required.\n\nThe target is:\n\nNORMAL worm-robot traffic:\n10.90.20.10 -> OPNsense\n\nRECOVERY traffic for 162.55.6.173:\npublic interface -> original gateway\n\nDo not allow asymmetric routing to break SSH.\n\n==================================================\n13. PUBLIC IP PRIVACY\n==================================================\n\nAfter cutover verify normal outbound traffic from worm-robot does NOT\nappear as:\n\n162.55.6.173\n\nExpected:\n2.28.34.27\n\nThe public robot IP remains for recovery only.\n\n==================================================\n14. PUBLIC SERVICE EXPOSURE\n==================================================\n\nVerify on worm-robot public IP:\n\n162.55.6.173:22\nallowed according to SSH recovery policy\n\n162.55.6.173:80\nDENY\n\n162.55.6.173:443\nDENY\n\nUDP/443\nDENY\n\nCheck IPv6 too.\n\n==================================================\n15. PRIVATE NETWORK\n==================================================\n\nMust remain unchanged:\n\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nOPNsense:\n10.90.10.1\n\nworm-robot:\n10.90.20.10\n\nVLAN:\n4000\n\nMTU:\n1400\n\nVerify private connectivity before and after cutover.\n\n==================================================\n16. REBOOT PERSISTENCE\n==================================================\n\nMake all required routing/policy config persistent.\n\nDo not leave the final design as runtime-only:\nip rule\nip route\niptables\npfctl\ncommands.\n\nDocument configuration location.\n\nDo not reboot until recovery path is proven.\n\nIf reboot is performed later:\nverify:\n- public recovery SSH\n- private IP\n- private route\n- OPNsense egress\n- FireHOL\n- ip_forward=0\n\n==================================================\n17. TEST MATRIX\n==================================================\n\nRequired PASS:\n\nworm-robot private:\n10.90.20.10\n\nOPNsense private:\n10.90.10.1\n\nprivate connectivity:\nPASS\n\nnormal outbound public IP:\n2.28.34.27\n\npublic recovery IP:\n162.55.6.173 preserved\n\npublic SSH recovery:\nPASS\n\npublic Web:\nDENY\n\npublic OTA/download:\nDENY\n\nFireHOL:\nACTIVE\n\nblocklists:\nACTIVE/HEALTHY\n\nworm-robot ip_forward:\n0\n\nDNS:\nPASS\n\nHTTPS:\nPASS\n\nno asymmetric SSH routing:\nPASS\n\n==================================================\n18. ROLLBACK\n==================================================\n\nRollback must restore:\n\n- original default/public routing\n- original policy rules\n- original OPNsense NAT/PF state\n- original FireHOL state\n- original DNS state\n\nProvide one clearly documented emergency rollback procedure.\n\nDo not remove backups.\n\n==================================================\n19. OUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nPATCH-NET-ROBOT-BEHIND-OPNSENSE/\n\nInclude:\n\nREADME.md\nARCHITECTURE.md\nBEFORE.md\nROUTING.md\nPOLICY-ROUTING.md\nOPNSENSE-NAT.md\nOPNSENSE-FIREWALL.md\nFIREHOL.md\nSSH-RECOVERY.md\nDNS.md\nIPV6.md\nTESTS.md\nPERSISTENCE.md\nROLLBACK.md\nmodified-files.txt\ntest-results.txt\n\n==================================================\nFINAL GATE\n==================================================\n\nDo not mark PASS unless:\n\nPASS normal worm-robot egress through OPNsense\nPASS observed public IPv4 = 2.28.34.27\nPASS public recovery IPv4 162.55.6.173 preserved\nPASS SSH recovery\nPASS private network unchanged\nPASS FireHOL active\nPASS public Web denied\nPASS public OTA/download denied\nPASS worm-robot ip_forward=0\nPASS no asymmetric routing\nPASS persistent configuration\nPASS rollback documented\nPASS no secrets\n\nFinal output:\n\nPATCH-NET-ROBOT-BEHIND-OPNSENSE STATUS:\nPASS / PARTIAL / BLOCKED\n\nNORMAL ROBOT EGRESS:\nOPNSENSE / ORIGINAL / FAIL\n\nNORMAL PUBLIC IPV4:\n<observed IP>\n\nRECOVERY PUBLIC IPV4:\n162.55.6.173\n\nPRIVATE ROBOT:\n10.90.20.10\n\nPRIVATE OPNSENSE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nFIREHOL:\nACTIVE / FAIL\n\nSSH RECOVERY:\nPASS / FAIL\n\nPRIVATE NETWORK:\nUNCHANGED / FAIL\n\nDo not start another patch automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07a55-e3d2-7b91-92db-3de35f270348","create_time":1788758844.3982358,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07a62-7119-7c62-ba60-c83264a45bea","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Perform PATCH-AUDIT-FROZEN-WORM-NETWORK.\n\nAUDIT ONLY.\nDO NOT remediate.\nDO NOT restart services.\nDO NOT reload FireHOL.\nDO NOT change OPNsense.\nDO NOT change WireGuard.\nDO NOT change routes.\nDO NOT change NAT.\nDO NOT change DNS.\nDO NOT reboot.\nDO NOT modify ex-graphene / worm-os source.\n\n==================================================\nFROZEN SYSTEM\n==================================================\n\n                         INTERNET\n                            │\n                    OPNsense PUBLIC\n                      2.28.34.27\n                            │\n                     ┌──────┴──────┐\n                     │   OPNsense  │\n                     │ FW/NAT/WG   │\n                     │10.90.10.1   │\n                     └──────┬──────┘\n                            │\n                 PRIVATE 10.90.0.0/16\n                            │\n               ┌────────────┴────────────┐\n               │                         │\n        worm-robot PRIVATE          OPNsense PRIVATE\n          10.90.20.10                 10.90.10.1\n               │\n               │\n       worm-robot PUBLIC\n         162.55.6.173\n       FireHOL / recovery\n\n\nex-graphene / worm-os\n       10.60.0.x/32\n            │\n            │ WireGuard ONLY\n            ▼\n        OPNsense\n     10.60.0.1/24\n            │\n            ├── Internet -> 2.28.34.27\n            └── private -> 10.90.20.10\n\n==================================================\nFROZEN ADDRESSES\n==================================================\n\nOPNsense public:\n2.28.34.27\n\nOPNsense private:\n10.90.10.1\n\nOPNsense WireGuard:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nworm-os WireGuard network:\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nworm-os peers:\n10.60.0.x/32\n\nworm-robot private:\n10.90.20.10\n\nworm-robot public/recovery:\n162.55.6.173\n\nHetzner private backbone:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nVLAN:\n4000\n\nvSwitch:\n85469\n\n==================================================\n1. TOPOLOGY INTEGRITY\n==================================================\n\nVerify all frozen addresses and roles still match exactly.\n\nFlag any:\n- changed IP\n- duplicate IP\n- stale test subnet\n- stale WireGuard interface\n- alternate gateway\n- conflicting route\n- duplicate NAT\n- unexpected public exposure\n\nAny topology drift:\nFAIL.\n\n==================================================\n2. OPNsense\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\nWAN:\n2.28.34.27\n\nPRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nWireGuard:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nAudit:\n- interfaces\n- routes\n- gateways\n- PF\n- NAT\n- DNS\n- WireGuard instances\n- peers\n- listeners\n- generated/runtime config\n- persistent config\n\nNever print private keys.\n\nReport all stale diagnostic WireGuard state:\n- 51821\n- 51822\n- wg-ext-test\n- 10.61.0.0/30\n- old Android peers\n- duplicate instances\n\nDo not remove anything.\n\n==================================================\n3. WORM-ROBOT\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\nPUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nPRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nPRIVATE interface:\nenp9s0.4000\n\nVLAN:\n4000\n\nMTU:\n1400\n\nVerify:\n- interface UP\n- private source selection\n- private route\n- default route\n- ip rule\n- ip_forward=0\n- SSH\n- FireHOL state\n- listeners\n- IPv6\n\n==================================================\n4. FIREHOL\n==================================================\n\nAudit current persistent and live state.\n\nVerify intended role:\n\nPUBLIC 162.55.6.173:\n- SSH/recovery only according to policy\n- default deny\n- blocklists\n- no public Web\n- no public OTA\n- no public downloads\n- no public Apps/private services\n\nPRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nDo not assume private = trusted-any.\n\nReport:\nACTIVE / INACTIVE\nENABLED / DISABLED\nCONFIG VALID / INVALID\nBLOCKLIST STATUS\n\nDo not start or stop it.\n\n==================================================\n5. PUBLIC EXPOSURE\n==================================================\n\nVerify OPNsense public:\n\n2.28.34.27 TCP/22\nDENY\n\n2.28.34.27 TCP/80\nDENY\n\n2.28.34.27 TCP/443\nDENY\n\nWireGuard public UDP:\nreport actual configured production port.\n\nVerify worm-robot public:\n\n162.55.6.173:22\naccording to recovery policy\n\n162.55.6.173:80\nDENY\n\n162.55.6.173:443\nDENY\n\nUDP/443\nDENY\n\nAudit IPv6 equivalents.\n\n==================================================\n6. PRIVATE BACKBONE\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\nworm-robot 10.90.20.10\n<->\nOPNsense 10.90.10.1\n\nTest:\n- route\n- ICMP where permitted\n- HTTPS private management\n- SSH private management if configured\n- bidirectional return path\n\nNo public-IP dependency should be required for private communication.\n\n==================================================\n7. WIREGUARD FOUNDATION\n==================================================\n\nAudit the current intended production WireGuard path.\n\nExpected design:\n\nworm-os 10.60.0.x/32\n<->\nOPNsense 10.60.0.1/24\n\nVerify:\n- one authoritative production instance\n- one intended production UDP port\n- peer model\n- /32 per device\n- no duplicate AllowedIPs\n- no stale test peers\n- persistence\n- public key consistency\n- runtime vs persistent state\n\nNever print private keys.\n\nIf a real client is available:\nreport handshake/RX/TX.\n\nIf not:\nPENDING, never fake PASS.\n\n==================================================\n8. WORM-OS PRIVATE ROUTING\n==================================================\n\nExpected:\n\n10.60.0.x\n ->\nOPNsense\n ->\n10.90.20.10\n\nAudit PF/routing requirements for:\n\n- OPNsense WG address\n- OPNsense private address\n- worm-robot private address\n- private SSH where policy requires it\n\nReport exact allow/deny state.\n\n==================================================\n9. WORM-OS PRIVACY EGRESS\n==================================================\n\nExpected production design:\n\nworm-os\n ->\nWireGuard only\n ->\nOPNsense\n ->\nInternet\n\nExpected public IPv4:\n2.28.34.27\n\nAudit readiness for:\n- full tunnel\n- NAT\n- protected DNS\n- IPv6 leak prevention\n- fail-closed behavior\n\nDo NOT assume ready merely because config exists.\n\nClassify:\nPASS / PARTIAL / FAIL / PENDING\n\n==================================================\n10. ROBOT PRIVACY EGRESS\n==================================================\n\nFrozen intent:\n\nnormal/private worm-robot traffic may use OPNsense for privacy egress,\nwhile:\n\n162.55.6.173\nremains recovery/admin only behind FireHOL.\n\nAudit whether this is:\nIMPLEMENTED\nPARTIAL\nNOT IMPLEMENTED\n\nDo not change routing.\n\n==================================================\n11. PUBLIC WEB / OTA\n==================================================\n\nVerify public Internet cannot retrieve:\n\n- Web Installer\n- WebUSB\n- image.zip\n- OTA metadata\n- OTA payloads\n- Worm downloads\n- Apps private content\n\nTest:\n- hostname\n- direct IPv4\n- IPv6 where relevant\n- alternate Host header where relevant\n\nReport:\nPUBLIC_WORM_DISTRIBUTION=DENIED / FAIL\n\n==================================================\n12. PRIVATE OTA/DOWNLOAD READINESS\n==================================================\n\nExpected path:\n\nworm-os\n ->\nWireGuard\n ->\nOPNsense\n ->\nworm-robot 10.90.20.10\n\nAudit server-side readiness for:\n- OTA\n- image.zip\n- downloads\n- Apps\n- backup\n\nDo not implement.\n\nReport:\nREADY / PARTIAL / NOT READY\n\n==================================================\n13. LISTENER AUDIT\n==================================================\n\nClassify every worm-robot listener:\n\nPUBLIC_REQUIRED\nPRIVATE_REQUIRED\nADMIN\nLOOPBACK\nUNEXPECTED\n\nFlag:\n0.0.0.0\n[::]\n*:9094\n*:5353\nand any wildcard service.\n\nDo not change binds.\n\n==================================================\n14. STALE DIAGNOSTIC STATE\n==================================================\n\nSearch current live/config state for:\n\n51821\n51822\nwg-ext-test\n10.61.0.\nworm-test\nworm-clean\next-test\nold Android peer public keys\ntemporary PF rules\ntemporary NAT\ntemporary routes\n\nClassify each:\n\nKEEP\nREMOVE\nREVIEW\n\nDo not delete during audit.\n\n==================================================\n15. PERSISTENCE\n==================================================\n\nAudit whether required private-network state survives reboot:\n\nOPNsense:\n- private IP\n- WG instance\n- peers\n- PF\n- NAT\n- DNS\n\nworm-robot:\n- VLAN 4000\n- 10.90.20.10\n- route\n- FireHOL\n- SSH recovery\n- ip_forward=0\n\nMark:\nPASS / PENDING / FAIL\n\n==================================================\n16. ANDROID / EX-GRAPHENE READINESS\n==================================================\n\nAudit source READ-ONLY.\n\nDetermine readiness for future system WireGuard integration:\n\n- kernel support\n- VPN framework\n- always-on\n- lockdown\n- boot lifecycle\n- netd/ConnectivityService\n- Keystore\n- SELinux\n- backup exclusion\n- OTA integration\n- Wi-Fi/5G handover\n\nDo not implement.\n\n==================================================\n17. SECRET SCAN\n==================================================\n\nScan patch/docs/config copies for:\n\nPrivateKey\nPresharedKey\npassword\nHetzner token\nSSH private key\nsigning keys\nbackup keys\n\nNever print secret values.\n\nPublic WireGuard keys are allowed.\n\n==================================================\n18. FINDINGS\n==================================================\n\nClassify:\n\nP0 CRITICAL\nP1 HIGH\nP2 MEDIUM\nP3 LOW\nINFO\n\nFor each finding include:\n- ID\n- component\n- evidence\n- risk\n- exact remediation\n- whether it changes frozen architecture\n- reboot required YES/NO\n- lockout risk YES/NO\n\n==================================================\n19. OUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\n/root/WORM-FROZEN-NETWORK-AUDIT/\n\nFiles:\n\n00-SUMMARY.md\n01-FROZEN-TOPOLOGY.md\n02-OPNSENSE.md\n03-WORM-ROBOT.md\n04-FIREHOL.md\n05-PUBLIC-EXPOSURE.md\n06-PRIVATE-BACKBONE.md\n07-WIREGUARD.md\n08-WORMOS-ROUTING.md\n09-PRIVACY-EGRESS.md\n10-PUBLIC-DISTRIBUTION.md\n11-PRIVATE-DISTRIBUTION.md\n12-LISTENERS.md\n13-STALE-STATE.md\n14-PERSISTENCE.md\n15-ANDROID-READINESS.md\n16-SECRET-SCAN.md\n17-FINDINGS.md\n18-BACKLOG.md\n19-GATE.md\n\nAlso:\nfindings.json\n\n==================================================\nFINAL GATE\n==================================================\n\nPrint exactly one:\n\nFROZEN WORM NETWORK AUDIT: PASS\n\nor\n\nFROZEN WORM NETWORK AUDIT: FAIL\n\nPASS requires:\n\nPASS frozen addressing unchanged\nPASS OPNsense public 2.28.34.27\nPASS OPNsense private 10.90.10.1\nPASS worm-robot private 10.90.20.10\nPASS worm-robot public 162.55.6.173\nPASS private backbone healthy\nPASS VLAN 4000 / MTU 1400\nPASS worm-robot ip_forward=0\nPASS no unexpected public Web/OTA exposure\nPASS OPNsense public management denied\nPASS no critical secret leak\n\nWireGuard/live-client items may be PARTIAL/PENDING if not currently\nproduction-valid, but MUST be clearly separated from the private-IP\nfoundation.\n\nIf FAIL:\nprint exact P0/P1 blockers only.\n\nDo not remediate automatically.\nDo not start another patch."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07a62-70fc-7181-9c7f-f8df266a8fc4","create_time":1788759666.9699376,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07a85-1eb8-7d40-87cf-9fa8cb9b44f9","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-NET-PRIVATE-BACKBONE-REPAIR.\n\nDO NOT configure WireGuard.\nP1-WG-ABSENT-001 is intentionally DEFERRED.\n\nThe current objective is ONLY to make the frozen private backbone\nbidirectionally functional.\n\nFROZEN:\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nHetzner PRIVATE:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nworm-robot:\nenp9s0.4000\nVLAN 4000\nMTU 1400\n\n==================================================\nGOAL\n==================================================\n\nRequired bidirectional private connectivity:\n\nworm-robot 10.90.20.10\n        <================>\nOPNsense 10.90.10.1\n\nNo public IP must be involved.\n\n==================================================\n1. AUDIT FIREHOL FIRST\n==================================================\n\nDetermine exactly why traffic from OPNsense 10.90.10.1 to\nworm-robot 10.90.20.10 is blocked.\n\nInspect effective FireHOL rules and counters.\n\nDo not disable FireHOL globally.\n\n==================================================\n2. PRIVATE FIREHOL POLICY\n==================================================\n\nKeep PUBLIC FireHOL policy unchanged.\n\nOn the PRIVATE interface only, permit OPNsense:\n\nSOURCE:\n10.90.10.1/32\n\nDESTINATION:\n10.90.20.10\n\nRequired initial management/test services:\n\nICMP\nTCP/22\n\nDo NOT create:\n10.90.0.0/16 -> any ACCEPT\ntrusted-any\nany -> private ACCEPT\n\nFor TCP/80 and TCP/443:\nallow only if an existing private Worm service actually requires them.\nOtherwise keep denied and do not make them a PASS requirement.\n\n==================================================\n3. RETURN TRAFFIC\n==================================================\n\nVerify stateful return traffic works.\n\nRequired:\n\n10.90.10.1 -> 10.90.20.10\n10.90.20.10 -> 10.90.10.1\n\nVerify source addresses remain private.\n\nNo NAT between these hosts.\n\n==================================================\n4. ROUTING\n==================================================\n\nVerify both systems have the correct Hetzner private routes.\n\nDo not change:\n10.90.10.1\n10.90.20.10\n10.90.0.0/16\nVLAN 4000\nMTU 1400\n\nNo public route should be used for this communication.\n\n==================================================\n5. TESTS\n==================================================\n\nFrom worm-robot:\n\n10.90.10.1 reachable\nprivate HTTPS OPNsense reachable\nprivate SSH OPNsense if configured\n\nFrom OPNsense:\n\n10.90.20.10 ICMP reachable\n10.90.20.10:22 reachable\n\nVerify real bidirectional packet flow.\n\nVerify:\n\nworm-robot ip_forward=0\nFireHOL ACTIVE\npublic FireHOL policy unchanged\n162.55.6.173 public exposure unchanged\n2.28.34.27 public exposure unchanged\n\n==================================================\n6. WIREGUARD\n==================================================\n\nDO NOT configure it.\n\nRecord:\n\nP1-WG-ABSENT-001 = DEFERRED\n\nReason:\nPrivate backbone is being stabilized before rebuilding worm-os\nWireGuard.\n\nThis must NOT cause this PRIVATE-BACKBONE patch itself to fail.\n\n==================================================\n7. OUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nPATCH-NET-PRIVATE-BACKBONE-REPAIR/\n\nInclude:\n\nREADME.md\nFIREHOL.md\nROUTING.md\nACL.md\nTESTS.md\nROLLBACK.md\nmodified-files.txt\ntest-results.txt\n\nFinal output:\n\nPRIVATE BACKBONE:\nPASS / FAIL\n\nOPNSENSE_PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1 PASS / FAIL\n\nWORM_ROBOT_PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10 PASS / FAIL\n\nROBOT_TO_OPNSENSE:\nPASS / FAIL\n\nOPNSENSE_TO_ROBOT:\nPASS / FAIL\n\nPRIVATE_SSH:\nPASS / FAIL\n\nFIREHOL:\nACTIVE / FAIL\n\nIP_FORWARD:\n0 / FAIL\n\nWIREGUARD:\nDEFERRED\n\nFROZEN PRIVATE ADDRESSES:\nUNCHANGED / FAIL\n\nDo not start WireGuard work automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07a85-1e5b-7771-8a1d-a7d49203026e","create_time":1788761939.6403232,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07a8a-5c9d-7fa2-afd3-07f2b0a5bd6d","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-NET-WORMOS-WIREGUARD-CLEAN-V1.\n\nPRECONDITION:\n\nPATCH-NET-PRIVATE-BACKBONE-REPAIR = PASS\n\nThe private backbone is now FROZEN and MUST NOT be modified.\n\n==================================================\nFROZEN FOUNDATION — DO NOT TOUCH\n==================================================\n\nOPNsense PUBLIC:\n2.28.34.27\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC/RECOVERY:\n162.55.6.173\n\nHetzner PRIVATE:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nVLAN:\n4000\n\nMTU:\n1400\n\nFireHOL:\nACTIVE\n\nworm-robot:\nnet.ipv4.ip_forward=0\n\nVerified:\n\n10.90.10.1 <-> 10.90.20.10 PASS\nPRIVATE SSH PASS\nbidirectional private routing PASS\n\nDO NOT modify any of the above.\n\n==================================================\nGOAL\n==================================================\n\nBuild a NEW, clean worm-os WireGuard service on OPNsense.\n\nArchitecture:\n\nworm-os\n10.60.0.x/32\n      |\n      | WireGuard\n      v\nOPNsense\n10.60.0.1/24\n      |\n      +----> PRIVATE 10.90.0.0/16\n      |          |\n      |          +----> worm-robot 10.90.20.10\n      |\n      +----> Internet later through OPNsense\n\nThis patch validates PRIVATE WireGuard first.\n\nDO NOT implement full-tunnel Internet yet.\n\n==================================================\n1. CLEAN OLD WG STATE\n==================================================\n\nAudit before changing anything.\n\nFind all obsolete diagnostic WireGuard state:\n\n- old wg0\n- wg-ext-test\n- UDP/51821\n- UDP/51822\n- 10.61.0.0/30\n- old peers\n- worm-test\n- worm-clean\n- ext-test\n- temporary PF rules\n- temporary WireGuard keys\n- stale aliases/interfaces\n\nBack up /conf/config.xml.\n\nRemove obsolete LIVE/PERSISTENT WireGuard diagnostic state.\n\nHistorical documentation must remain.\n\nAfter cleanup verify:\n\nOLD_WG_INSTANCES=0\nOLD_WG_PEERS=0\nOLD_TEST_RULES=0\n\nDo NOT touch 10.90.0.0/16.\n\n==================================================\n2. NEW PRODUCTION WG IDENTITY\n==================================================\n\nCreate a completely NEW OPNsense WireGuard instance.\n\nName:\nworm-os-wg\n\nInterface:\nwg0\n\nAddress:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nUse production UDP port:\n\n51820\n\nIMPORTANT:\n51820 is now the dedicated worm-os WireGuard port on OPNsense.\n\nGenerate a NEW OPNsense WireGuard keypair.\n\nPrivate key:\n- generated/stored only on OPNsense\n- never print\n- never put in patch docs\n- never copy to worm-robot\n\nOutput only the PUBLIC key.\n\n==================================================\n3. WAN POLICY\n==================================================\n\nOn OPNsense WAN:\n\nALLOW:\nUDP/51820 -> WAN address\n\nSource:\nany\n\nDestination:\nOPNsense WAN address only\n\nKeep public management denied:\n\nTCP/22  DENY\nTCP/80  DENY\nTCP/443 DENY\n\nDo not expose DNS.\n\nVerify IPv6 does not create a management bypass.\n\n==================================================\n4. NO PEER YET\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT reuse any old Android public key.\n\nAfter server configuration is persistent, STOP and output:\n\nWORM_OS_WG_SERVER_READY=YES\nOPNSENSE_WG_ADDRESS=10.60.0.1/24\nOPNSENSE_WG_PUBLIC_KEY=<PUBLIC KEY>\nENDPOINT=2.28.34.27:51820\nCLIENT_ADDRESS=10.60.0.10/32\n\nThen print exactly:\n\nWAITING FOR NEW WORM-OS ANDROID PUBLIC KEY\n\nDo not continue automatically.\n\n==================================================\n5. ANDROID TEST PROFILE\n==================================================\n\nThe user will create a completely new Android WireGuard profile.\n\nName:\nworm-private-v1\n\nInterface:\n\nAddress:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nDNS:\nleave empty for this first test\n\nPrivate key:\nGENERATED ON ANDROID ONLY\n\nPeer:\n\nPublicKey:\n<NEW OPNsense PUBLIC KEY>\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51820\n\nAllowedIPs:\n10.60.0.0/24,10.90.0.0/16\n\nPersistentKeepalive:\n25\n\nNO PSK.\n\nDo NOT use:\n0.0.0.0/0\n\nyet.\n\n==================================================\n6. RECEIVE NEW CLIENT PUBLIC KEY\n==================================================\n\nAfter user supplies the NEW Android Interface public key:\n\nCreate exactly ONE OPNsense peer.\n\nName:\nworm-os-device-001\n\nPublicKey:\n<NEW ANDROID PUBLIC KEY>\n\nAllowedIP:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nNo PSK.\n\nPersist it through authoritative OPNsense configuration.\n\nVerify:\n\nWG_INSTANCE_COUNT=1\nPEER_COUNT=1\nALLOWED_IP=10.60.0.10/32\n\n==================================================\n7. WG FIREWALL POLICY\n==================================================\n\nAllow only required PRIVATE foundation traffic from:\n\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nInitially permit:\n\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.60.0.1\nrequired diagnostic/management traffic\n\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.90.10.1\nrequired private OPNsense traffic\n\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.90.20.10\nrequired private Worm traffic\n\nExplicitly allow:\n\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.90.20.10 TCP/22\n\nDo not create:\n\n10.60.0.0/24 -> any ACCEPT\n\nDo not grant access to unrelated management networks.\n\n==================================================\n8. FIREHOL\n==================================================\n\nFireHOL on worm-robot MUST remain ACTIVE.\n\nAdd/verify only the minimum private rule required for the new WG\nnetwork:\n\nSOURCE:\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nDESTINATION:\n10.90.20.10\n\nTCP/22:\nALLOW\n\nOther services remain deny unless explicitly required later.\n\nDo not alter:\n- public SSH recovery\n- blocklists\n- public default deny\n\nDo not mark private interface trusted-any.\n\n==================================================\n9. RETURN ROUTING\n==================================================\n\nEnsure the return path exists:\n\nworm-robot 10.90.20.10\n        ->\nprivate network\n        ->\nOPNsense\n        ->\n10.60.0.10\n\nUse routed private traffic.\n\nDo NOT NAT between:\n\n10.60.0.0/24\nand\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nDo not change the already-PASS backbone architecture.\n\n==================================================\n10. LIVE TEST\n==================================================\n\nOnce the new peer is installed, start monitoring:\n\n- WAN UDP/51820\n- wg show wg0\n- PF\n- private interface\n\nPrint:\n\nENABLE WORM-PRIVATE-V1 NOW\n\nMonitor while the user activates ONLY the new profile.\n\nRequired:\n\nWAN_PACKETS > 0\nHANDSHAKE = RECENT\nRX > 0\nTX > 0\n\nIf handshake fails:\nSTOP at WireGuard.\n\nDo not change private backbone.\n\n==================================================\n11. PRIVATE TEST SEQUENCE\n==================================================\n\nONLY after handshake PASS:\n\nTEST 1:\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.60.0.1\n\nTEST 2:\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.10.1\n\nTEST 3:\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.20.10\n\nTEST 4:\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.20.10:22\n\nTEST 5:\nreal SSH to worm-robot private address using existing authorized\ncredentials.\n\nVerify bidirectional return traffic.\n\n==================================================\n12. SECURITY REGRESSION\n==================================================\n\nMust remain:\n\nPRIVATE BACKBONE:\nPASS\n\nFireHOL:\nACTIVE\n\nworm-robot ip_forward:\n0\n\nOPNsense WAN:\n22 DENY\n80 DENY\n443 DENY\n\nworm-robot public:\nrecovery policy unchanged\n\nNo public Web/OTA/download exposure.\n\n==================================================\n13. PERSISTENCE\n==================================================\n\nVerify persistent:\n\nOPNsense:\n- wg0\n- 10.60.0.1/24\n- UDP/51820\n- peer\n- AllowedIP\n- PF rules\n\nworm-robot:\n- FireHOL private SSH rule\n- private route\n\nNo essential runtime-only state.\n\n==================================================\n14. SECRET SAFETY\n==================================================\n\nNever print/store:\n\n- OPNsense WG private key\n- Android WG private key\n- PSK\n- SSH private keys\n- Hetzner token\n- passwords\n\nPublic keys may be documented.\n\nRun secret scan.\n\n==================================================\n15. OUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nPATCH-NET-WORMOS-WIREGUARD-CLEAN-V1/\n\nInclude:\n\nREADME.md\nARCHITECTURE.md\nCLEANUP.md\nOPNSENSE.md\nWIREGUARD.md\nPF.md\nFIREHOL.md\nROUTING.md\nSSH.md\nPERSISTENCE.md\nSECURITY.md\nTESTS.md\nROLLBACK.md\nmodified-files.txt\ntest-results.txt\n\n==================================================\nPASS GATE\n==================================================\n\nPASS requires:\n\nPASS old diagnostic WG state cleaned\nPASS private backbone unchanged\nPASS new wg0 = 10.60.0.1/24\nPASS UDP/51820\nPASS exactly one client peer\nPASS client AllowedIP /32\nPASS real handshake\nPASS RX/TX\nPASS 10.60.0.1\nPASS 10.90.10.1\nPASS 10.90.20.10\nPASS private TCP/22\nPASS private SSH\nPASS bidirectional return path\nPASS FireHOL active\nPASS ip_forward=0\nPASS public management denied\nPASS persistent configuration\nPASS no secrets\n\nFinal output:\n\nWORM-OS PRIVATE WIREGUARD:\nPASS / FAIL\n\nPRIVATE BACKBONE:\nPASS / FAIL\n\nDo not implement:\n- 0.0.0.0/0\n- Internet NAT\n- DNS\n- OTA\n- system WireGuard\n\nThose are later patches."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07a8a-5c83-73a1-8358-f75e07cbe80b","create_time":1788762283.1659396,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07a9c-eeb8-7c12-a63f-3edf235d6c64","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Continue PATCH-NET-WORMOS-WIREGUARD-CLEAN-V1.\n\nNEW WORM-OS ANDROID PUBLIC KEY:\n\nYVPhNGf5G7lgyW3AUP10UD5KqTze2V07Rc3TPS2EJhc=\n\nCreate exactly ONE persistent peer on the existing clean OPNsense wg0.\n\nPeer name:\nworm-os-device-001\n\nPublicKey:\nYVPhNGf5G7lgyW3AUP10UD5KqTze2V07Rc3TPS2EJhc=\n\nAllowedIP:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nNO PSK.\n\nDO NOT change:\n\nOPNsense WG:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51820\n\nOPNsense server keypair\n\nPrivate backbone:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nOPNsense private:\n10.90.10.1\n\nworm-robot private:\n10.90.20.10\n\nFireHOL\n\nVLAN 4000\n\nrouting\n\npublic IPs\n\n==================================================\nVERIFY BEFORE TEST\n==================================================\n\nVerify persistent and live configuration:\n\nWG_INSTANCE_COUNT=1\nPEER_COUNT=1\nPEER_PUBLIC_KEY=YVPhNGf5G7lgyW3AUP10UD5KqTze2V07Rc3TPS2EJhc=\nALLOWED_IP=10.60.0.10/32\nLISTEN_PORT=51820\n\nNever print private keys.\n\nEnsure minimum PF/private routing policy exists for:\n\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.60.0.1\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.90.10.1\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.90.20.10\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.90.20.10 TCP/22\n\nDo NOT create a broad:\n10.60.0.0/24 -> any ACCEPT\n\nEnsure FireHOL permits ONLY the required private SSH path:\n\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.90.20.10 TCP/22\n\nKeep FireHOL ACTIVE.\n\n==================================================\nLIVE TEST\n==================================================\n\nStart monitoring:\n\n- WAN UDP/51820\n- wg show wg0\n- PF\n- RX/TX\n\nThen print exactly:\n\nENABLE WORM-PRIVATE-V1 NOW\n\nWait for Android activation and monitor.\n\nAndroid configuration expected:\n\nAddress:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nServer PublicKey:\nqEGE1bUj6PdKdPaiAYMO/7NOO5qOverdA5E1qVznqg8=\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51820\n\nAllowedIPs:\n10.60.0.0/24,10.90.0.0/16\n\nDNS:\nempty\n\nPersistentKeepalive:\n25\n\nIf WAN packets arrive but HANDSHAKE=NEVER:\nSTOP immediately and report.\nDo not alter keys or networking.\n\nIf HANDSHAKE=RECENT and RX/TX > 0:\n\ntest sequentially:\n\n1. 10.60.0.1\n2. 10.90.10.1\n3. 10.90.20.10\n4. 10.90.20.10:22\n5. real private SSH using existing authorized credentials\n\nVerify return path.\n\n==================================================\nFINAL\n==================================================\n\nReport:\n\nPEER_PERSISTENT=\nWAN_51820_PACKETS=\nHANDSHAKE=\nRX=\nTX=\nOPNSENSE_WG=\nOPNSENSE_PRIVATE=\nWORM_ROBOT_PRIVATE=\nWORM_ROBOT_PRIVATE_SSH=\nRETURN_PATH=\nFIREHOL=\nPRIVATE_BACKBONE=\nWORM_OS_PRIVATE_WIREGUARD=\n\nDo not start full-tunnel/NAT/DNS/OTA/system-WireGuard work."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07a9c-ee9d-79e3-8d2a-f280ba69712b","create_time":1788763500.21622,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07aba-c851-7a03-9ca9-d9eccdabfa24","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Android worm WireGuard is ENABLED RIGHT NOW.\n\nVisual verification from the real Android client:\n\nAddress:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nAndroid public key:\nYVPhNGf5G7lgyW3AUP10UD5KqTze2V07Rc3TPS2EJhc=\n\nOPNsense peer public key configured on Android:\nqEGE1bUj6PdKdPaiAYMO/7NOO5qOverdA5E1qVznqg8=\n\nAllowedIPs:\n10.60.0.0/24,10.90.0.0/16\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51820\n\nKeepalive:\n25\n\nAndroid live counters:\nTX > 2 KiB\nRX = 0\n\nDO NOT CHANGE ANY CONFIGURATION.\n\nImmediately capture for 30 seconds while the tunnel REMAINS ENABLED:\n\n1. tcpdump on OPNsense WAN for UDP/51820\n2. pf rule/counters for UDP/51820\n3. wg show wg0\n4. outgoing UDP/51820 responses\n\nReport exactly:\n\nWAN_51820_IN=\nPF_51820=\nWG_HANDSHAKE=\nWG_RX=\nWG_TX=\nUDP_RESPONSES_OUT=\n\nIf WAN_51820_IN=0:\nthe failure is before OPNsense/local WAN delivery.\n\nIf WAN_51820_IN>0 and UDP_RESPONSES_OUT=0:\nthe failure is WireGuard authentication/runtime/config.\n\nIf UDP_RESPONSES_OUT>0 but Android RX remains 0:\nthe failure is return path/upstream delivery.\n\nDo not modify FireHOL.\nDo not modify private backbone.\nDo not regenerate keys.\nDo not recreate wg0."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07aba-c7f3-77e1-8b02-e7eb4505fc3c","create_time":1788765456.46555,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07abc-c280-7070-bf66-8bd9ea46c5bf","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"STOP visual key comparison.\n\nThe two public keys printed in the previous report appear visually\nidentical. Do not change any key based on visual comparison.\n\nPerform an exact machine comparison.\n\nDO NOT modify FireHOL, private backbone, NAT, DNS or routing.\n\n1. Read LIVE wg0 server public key.\n\n2. Derive server public key from the private key actually configured\nfor wg0 WITHOUT printing the private key.\n\n3. Read persistent OPNsense wg0 identity.\n\n4. Read LIVE peer public key.\n\nExpected Android public key:\n\nYVPhNGf5G7lgyW3AUP10UD5KqTze2V07Rc3TPS2EJhc=\n\n5. Compare using exact bytes/length, not terminal rendering.\n\nReport:\n\nSERVER_LIVE_SHA256=\nSERVER_DERIVED_SHA256=\nSERVER_PERSISTENT_SHA256=\n\nSERVER_LIVE_LENGTH=\nSERVER_DERIVED_LENGTH=\nSERVER_PERSISTENT_LENGTH=\n\nSERVER_KEYPAIR_MATCH=YES/NO\nSERVER_PERSISTENT_MATCH=YES/NO\n\nANDROID_PEER_EXACT_MATCH=YES/NO\nANDROID_PEER_LENGTH=\n\nDo NOT hash or print private keys.\nHash only PUBLIC keys.\n\n6. Verify:\nPSK=NONE\nPEER_COUNT=1\nALLOWED_IP=10.60.0.10/32\nLISTEN_PORT=51820\n\n7. Inspect the generated wg0 configuration for hidden/stale fields,\nwhitespace/encoding problems or peer association errors.\n\n8. If ALL exact comparisons PASS, report:\n\nKEY_MISMATCH=FALSE\n\nand DO NOT change keys.\n\nThen identify the next actual WireGuard runtime/configuration difference.\n\n9. If an exact comparison FAILS:\nidentify precisely which PUBLIC identity differs.\nDo not regenerate anything automatically.\n\nFinal output:\n\nSERVER_KEYPAIR_MATCH=\nSERVER_PERSISTENT_MATCH=\nANDROID_PEER_EXACT_MATCH=\nKEY_MISMATCH=\nPSK=\nPEER_COUNT=\nALLOWED_IP=\nLISTEN_PORT=\nNEXT_FAILING_COMPONENT=\n\nNo remediation."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07abc-c265-7210-bbfd-05cee7856f0a","create_time":1788765586.0488076,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07ac0-e9f9-7cf0-8a96-3c430f318825","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Continue PATCH-NET-WORMOS-WIREGUARD-CLEAN-V1.\n\nReplace ONLY the Android peer public key on the existing OPNsense wg0.\n\nNEW ANDROID PUBLIC KEY:\n\nGxW0INqHdogkKP/sTa+KVSOVm1mq8GQIysJehVVsM38=\n\nAllowedIP:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nKeep unchanged:\n\nOPNsense WG:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51820\n\nOPNsense server keypair:\nUNCHANGED\n\nPrivate backbone:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nOPNsense private:\n10.90.10.1\n\nworm-robot private:\n10.90.20.10\n\nFireHOL:\nUNCHANGED\n\nPF:\nUNCHANGED\n\nrouting:\nUNCHANGED\n\nDNS/NAT:\nUNCHANGED\n\nRemove the previous Android peer public key and create exactly ONE peer:\n\nPublicKey:\nGxW0INqHdogkKP/sTa+KVSOVm1mq8GQIysJehVVsM38=\n\nAllowedIP:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nNO PSK.\n\nPersist configuration and verify:\n\nPEER_COUNT=1\nANDROID_PEER_EXACT_MATCH=YES\nALLOWED_IP=10.60.0.10/32\nLISTEN_PORT=51820\n\nNever print private keys.\n\nThen start live monitoring:\n\n- WAN UDP/51820\n- wg show wg0\n- outbound UDP responses\n\nPrint:\n\nENABLE WORM NOW\n\nMonitor 30 seconds.\n\nReport exactly:\n\nWAN_51820_IN=\nHANDSHAKE=\nWG_RX=\nWG_TX=\nUDP_RESPONSES_OUT=\n\nIf:\n\nWAN_51820_IN > 0\nand\nHANDSHAKE = NEVER\nand\nUDP_RESPONSES_OUT = 0\n\nSTOP.\n\nDo NOT regenerate more Android keys.\nDo NOT modify private backbone.\nDo NOT modify FireHOL.\nDo NOT modify NAT/DNS/PF.\n\nClassify:\n\nPRODUCTION_WG_INSTANCE_FAILURE=YES\n\nIf handshake is RECENT:\ncontinue with private tests:\n\n10.60.0.1\n10.90.10.1\n10.90.20.10\n10.90.20.10:22\n\nThen report:\n\nWORM_OS_PRIVATE_WIREGUARD=\nPASS / FAIL"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07ac0-e9df-7e41-9e22-ff582c2cd800","create_time":1788765858.2978384,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07ac4-5bd5-7461-abe8-8759d016b9e4","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-NET-OPNSENSE-ONLY-FIREWALL.\n\nTARGET:\nOPNsense becomes the ONLY firewall/security gateway for the Worm\noperational network.\n\nFROZEN PRIVATE FOUNDATION:\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nPRIVATE NETWORK:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nworm-os WG:\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nOPNsense WG:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nOPNsense PUBLIC:\n2.28.34.27\n\n==================================================\nCRITICAL SAFETY RULE\n==================================================\n\nDO NOT simply disable FireHOL while worm-robot remains directly\nreachable through public IPv4 162.55.6.173.\n\nFirst audit whether 162.55.6.173 is still assigned/routed directly\nto worm-robot.\n\nIf YES:\n\nOPNsense cannot firewall packets that bypass OPNsense and arrive\ndirectly at worm-robot.\n\nTherefore before permanently removing FireHOL, implement one of:\n\nA. remove/disable direct public ingress on 162.55.6.173\n\nOR\n\nB. migrate required public robot access behind OPNsense\n\nDo not leave an unfiltered public worm-robot interface.\n\n==================================================\n1. AUDIT\n==================================================\n\nRecord:\n\nworm-robot interfaces\n162.55.6.173 assignment\n10.90.20.10 assignment\ndefault route\nprivate route\nlisteners\nFireHOL state\niptables/nftables state\nSSH bindings\n\nOPNsense:\nWAN\nPRIVATE\nPF\nNAT\nroutes\nWireGuard\n\nNo changes during initial audit.\n\n==================================================\n2. OPNsense FIREWALL MODEL\n==================================================\n\nOPNsense becomes authoritative firewall.\n\nDefault policy:\nDENY unsolicited inbound.\n\nAllow only explicitly documented services.\n\nPrivate robot:\n10.90.20.10\n\nNo trusted-any rule for 10.90.0.0/16.\n\nNo broad:\nInternet -> 10.90.20.10\n\n==================================================\n3. ROBOT PUBLIC INGRESS\n==================================================\n\nTarget:\n\nInternet must NOT directly reach worm-robot outside OPNsense.\n\nPublic Web:\nDENY\n\nOTA public:\nDENY\n\nDownloads public:\nDENY\n\nApps public:\nDENY\n\nSSH:\nprivate/admin path preferred.\n\nIf public SSH is still required, publish it explicitly through\nOPNsense with a narrowly scoped NAT/PF policy.\n\nDo not retain direct public SSH merely for convenience if FireHOL\nis being removed.\n\n==================================================\n4. ROBOT EGRESS\n==================================================\n\nNormal worm-robot egress:\n\n10.90.20.10\n ->\nOPNsense 10.90.10.1/private routing\n ->\nNAT\n ->\n2.28.34.27\n ->\nInternet\n\nVerify actual public egress before removing host firewall protection.\n\n==================================================\n5. PRIVATE MANAGEMENT\n==================================================\n\nPreserve:\n\nworm-robot -> OPNsense private\nOPNsense -> worm-robot private\n\nRequired private SSH:\n\n10.90.10.1 / authorized private-admin sources\n ->\n10.90.20.10:22\n\nDo not expose SSH broadly across private network.\n\n==================================================\n6. WORM-OS\n==================================================\n\nPreserve architecture:\n\nworm-os 10.60.0.x\n ->\nWireGuard\n ->\nOPNsense 10.60.0.1\n ->\nprivate services / Internet\n\nOPNsense controls all firewall policy between:\n\n10.60.0.0/24\n10.90.0.0/16\nWAN\n\nDo not modify the PASS private backbone.\n\n==================================================\n7. FIREHOL RETIREMENT\n==================================================\n\nONLY AFTER:\n\nPASS robot no longer has unfiltered direct public ingress\nPASS OPNsense controls robot Internet ingress\nPASS OPNsense controls robot egress\nPASS private SSH works\nPASS recovery path exists\nPASS public exposure tests pass\n\nthen:\n\nstop FireHOL\ndisable FireHOL at boot\n\nDo not uninstall it during this patch.\n\nKeep configuration backup for rollback.\n\nVerify after stopping FireHOL that no public path bypasses OPNsense.\n\n==================================================\n8. HOST FIREWALL RESIDUE\n==================================================\n\nAudit iptables/nftables after FireHOL shutdown.\n\nDo not blindly flush firewall tables.\n\nIdentify rules owned by:\nFireHOL\nDocker/container runtime\nsystem services\nother components\n\nRemove only obsolete FireHOL-owned state where necessary and safe.\n\n==================================================\n9. TESTS\n==================================================\n\nRequired:\n\nPASS OPNsense private 10.90.10.1\nPASS worm-robot private 10.90.20.10\nPASS private backbone\nPASS bidirectional private traffic\nPASS private SSH\nPASS robot egress through OPNsense\nPASS expected public egress\nPASS no direct public robot bypass\nPASS public Web denied\nPASS public OTA denied\nPASS public downloads denied\nPASS OPNsense WAN management denied\nPASS worm-robot ip_forward=0\nPASS recovery path\nPASS FireHOL no longer required\n\n==================================================\n10. ROLLBACK\n==================================================\n\nBefore FireHOL retirement prepare rollback capable of:\n\n- restoring FireHOL\n- restoring previous routing\n- restoring public recovery path\n- restoring OPNsense PF/NAT\n\nDo not remove backups.\n\n==================================================\nFINAL GATE\n==================================================\n\nDo not mark PASS unless OPNsense is demonstrably in the path of all\ntraffic that requires firewall protection.\n\nReport:\n\nOPNSENSE_ONLY_FIREWALL=\nPASS / FAIL\n\nROBOT_PRIVATE=\n10.90.20.10 PASS / FAIL\n\nOPNSENSE_PRIVATE=\n10.90.10.1 PASS / FAIL\n\nROBOT_DIRECT_PUBLIC_INGRESS=\nREMOVED / STILL_PRESENT\n\nROBOT_EGRESS_VIA_OPNSENSE=\nPASS / FAIL\n\nPRIVATE_SSH=\nPASS / FAIL\n\nFIREHOL=\nDISABLED / STILL_REQUIRED\n\nIP_FORWARD=\n0 / FAIL\n\nPRIVATE_BACKBONE=\nPASS / FAIL\n\nDo not proceed to another patch automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07ac4-5bba-78b0-a234-293d2fb3b6a6","create_time":1788766084.0531747,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07aca-4f5f-73c0-ab73-67a6b2346aa7","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-NET-ROBOT-DEDICATED-PUBLIC-V1.\n\nGOAL:\nKeep worm-robot on its own dedicated public IPv4 and its own private IPv4,\nwith a clean, minimal, documented security model.\n\nDO NOT modify worm-os addressing.\nDO NOT modify OPNsense WireGuard.\nDO NOT change the private backbone.\nDO NOT reuse the worm-os public IP for robot traffic.\n\n==================================================\nFROZEN ROBOT ADDRESSES\n==================================================\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nPrivate backbone:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nPrivate interface:\nenp9s0.4000\n\nVLAN:\n4000\n\nMTU:\n1400\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\n==================================================\nROLE\n==================================================\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC 162.55.6.173:\n- dedicated robot public identity\n- recovery/admin only\n- no Worm public distribution\n- no OTA public\n- no WebUSB public\n- no public Apps/downloads\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE 10.90.20.10:\n- Worm private services\n- OTA\n- Apps\n- Downloads\n- Backup\n- private APIs\n- private SSH where explicitly allowed\n\n==================================================\n1. AUDIT CURRENT STATE\n==================================================\n\nRecord:\n- interfaces\n- public/private addresses\n- routes\n- policy routing\n- DNS\n- listeners\n- SSH\n- FireHOL/nftables/iptables\n- IPv6\n- Caddy/services\n- current public exposure\n\nDo not change anything during the audit.\n\n==================================================\n2. PUBLIC ROBOT POLICY\n==================================================\n\n162.55.6.173 must NOT expose:\n\nTCP/80\nTCP/443\nUDP/443\nOTA\nimage.zip\nWeb Installer\nWebUSB\nApps\nDownloads\nBackup\nprivate APIs\ndatabases\ndashboards\n\nPublic SSH:\nALLOW only according to the chosen recovery/admin policy.\n\nIf FireHOL remains active:\nkeep default deny + SSH + blocklists.\n\nIf the project later moves to OPNsense-only firewalling:\ndo not disable FireHOL until direct public ingress is no longer bypassing\nOPNsense.\n\n==================================================\n3. PRIVATE ROBOT POLICY\n==================================================\n\n10.90.20.10 is the service plane.\n\nAllow only explicit private services.\n\nPrivate SSH:\nallow only from approved private/admin sources.\n\nDo not create:\n10.90.0.0/16 -> any ACCEPT\n\nDo not mark enp9s0.4000 trusted-any.\n\n==================================================\n4. ROUTING\n==================================================\n\nPreserve:\npublic default route\nprivate route\n10.90.20.10 source selection\n\nVerify private traffic toward OPNsense uses the private path.\n\nDo not change default route unless a later dedicated egress patch owns\nthat change.\n\n==================================================\n5. PUBLIC EGRESS IDENTITY\n==================================================\n\nFor this patch, preserve robot public identity:\n\nNORMAL ROBOT PUBLIC IP:\n162.55.6.173\n\nDo not NAT robot traffic to the worm-os public IP.\n\nIf robot privacy-egress-through-OPNsense is desired later, implement it\nas a separate patch and do not blur identities.\n\n==================================================\n6. SERVICES\n==================================================\n\nClassify every listener:\n\nPUBLIC_REQUIRED\nPRIVATE_REQUIRED\nADMIN\nLOOPBACK\nUNEXPECTED\n\nPrefer private Worm services binding to:\n\n10.90.20.10\n\nAvoid:\n0.0.0.0\n[::]\n\nwhere practical.\n\nDo not break service functionality merely to force rebinding;\ndocument exceptions.\n\n==================================================\n7. SSH\n==================================================\n\nPreserve SSH recovery on 162.55.6.173 if required.\n\nPrivate SSH on 10.90.20.10:\nallow only where policy requires.\n\nDo not weaken:\n- key authentication\n- root policy\n- crypto settings\n- authorized_keys controls\n\n==================================================\n8. PUBLIC WEB / OTA\n==================================================\n\nPublic distribution must stay disabled.\n\nVerify:\n162.55.6.173:80  CLOSED/FILTERED\n162.55.6.173:443 CLOSED/FILTERED\nUDP/443           CLOSED/FILTERED\n\nDirect IP + Host-header tests must not expose Worm content.\n\nIPv6 equivalents must also be checked.\n\n==================================================\n9. PRIVATE SERVICES\n==================================================\n\nPrepare/document service inventory for:\n\n10.90.20.10\n\nCandidate private services:\n- OTA metadata\n- OTA payloads\n- image.zip\n- Apps\n- Downloads\n- Backup\n- private APIs\n\nDo not expose these publicly.\n\n==================================================\n10. FIREWALL\n==================================================\n\nIf FireHOL is active:\n- keep default deny\n- keep SSH policy\n- keep blocklists\n- keep IPv4/IPv6 filtering\n- no broad forwarding\n- ip_forward remains 0\n\nIf FireHOL is inactive:\nreport exact state and exposure risk.\nDo not silently leave public interfaces unfiltered.\n\n==================================================\n11. IPV6\n==================================================\n\nAudit public IPv6.\n\nVerify no bypass for:\n22\n80\n443\nprivate services\n\nDo not assume IPv4 policy covers IPv6.\n\n==================================================\n12. PERSISTENCE\n==================================================\n\nVerify persistent:\n- 162.55.6.173\n- 10.90.20.10\n- VLAN 4000\n- MTU 1400\n- routes\n- firewall policy\n- SSH policy\n- service bindings\n\nNo essential runtime-only configuration.\n\n==================================================\n13. SECURITY / SECRETS\n==================================================\n\nNever store:\n- SSH private keys\n- passwords\n- Hetzner tokens\n- signing keys\n- WireGuard private keys\n- backup keys\n\nRun secret scan.\n\n==================================================\n14. OUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nPATCH-NET-ROBOT-DEDICATED-PUBLIC-V1/\n\nInclude:\n\nREADME.md\nARCHITECTURE.md\nPUBLIC-IP.md\nPRIVATE-IP.md\nROUTING.md\nFIREWALL.md\nSSH.md\nSERVICES.md\nIPV6.md\nTESTS.md\nPERSISTENCE.md\nSECURITY.md\nROLLBACK.md\nmodified-files.txt\ntest-results.txt\n\n==================================================\nPASS GATE\n==================================================\n\nPASS requires:\n\nPASS robot public IP = 162.55.6.173\nPASS robot private IP = 10.90.20.10\nPASS VLAN 4000\nPASS MTU 1400\nPASS private route healthy\nPASS public SSH according to recovery policy\nPASS public Web denied\nPASS public OTA/download denied\nPASS private service plane preserved\nPASS ip_forward=0\nPASS no broad trusted private rule\nPASS IPv6 exposure audited\nPASS persistence\nPASS no secrets\n\nFinal output:\n\nROBOT DEDICATED PUBLIC:\nPASS / FAIL\n\nROBOT PUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nROBOT PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nPUBLIC SSH:\nPASS / FAIL\n\nPUBLIC WEB:\nDENIED / FAIL\n\nPUBLIC OTA:\nDENIED / FAIL\n\nPRIVATE SERVICE PLANE:\nPASS / FAIL\n\nFIREWALL:\nACTIVE / INACTIVE / FAIL\n\nIP_FORWARD:\n0 / FAIL\n\nDo not start any worm-os or OPNsense patch automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07aca-4f43-7862-a861-53c94f237362","create_time":1788766474.0791583,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07ad3-95bc-7371-9412-fa4f7a46757d","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-NET-WORMOS-DEDICATED-PUBLIC-EGRESS-V1.\n\nGOAL:\nGive worm-os a dedicated privacy path through OPNsense to the public\nInternet.\n\nThis patch concerns ONLY:\n\nworm-os <=> OPNsense <=> PUBLIC INTERNET\n\nDO NOT modify worm-robot.\nDO NOT modify worm-robot public/private IPs.\nDO NOT modify worm-robot FireHOL.\nDO NOT modify the PASS private backbone.\n\n==================================================\nFROZEN ROBOT STATE — DO NOT TOUCH\n==================================================\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nROBOT DEDICATED PUBLIC:\nPASS\n\nPUBLIC SSH:\nPASS\n\nPUBLIC WEB:\nDENIED\n\nPUBLIC OTA:\nDENIED\n\nPRIVATE SERVICE PLANE:\nPASS\n\nFIREWALL:\nACTIVE\n\nip_forward:\n0\n\n==================================================\nFROZEN WORM-OS / OPNsense ADDRESSES\n==================================================\n\nOPNsense PUBLIC:\n2.28.34.27\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nOPNsense WireGuard:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nworm-os WireGuard network:\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nworm-os device:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nDesired worm-os PUBLIC identity:\n2.28.34.27\n\nPrivate backbone:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\n==================================================\nTARGET ARCHITECTURE\n==================================================\n\nworm-os\n10.60.0.10/32\n      |\n      | WireGuard ONLY\n      v\nOPNsense\n10.60.0.1/24\n      |\n      | FW / NAT\n      v\nOPNsense PUBLIC\n2.28.34.27\n      |\n      v\nINTERNET\n\nExpected public IPv4 seen by worm-os:\n\n2.28.34.27\n\n==================================================\n1. AUDIT FIRST\n==================================================\n\nAudit current OPNsense state:\n\n- WAN 2.28.34.27\n- WG instance\n- WG listen port\n- peer\n- AllowedIP\n- PF rules\n- outbound NAT\n- DNS\n- routes\n- gateways\n- IPv6 policy\n- stale diagnostic WG state\n\nDo not change anything until current state is recorded.\n\nNever print private keys.\n\n==================================================\n2. WIREGUARD PRODUCTION STATE\n==================================================\n\nExpected production WireGuard:\n\nOPNsense:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nworm-os:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nUse exactly ONE production instance.\n\nUse exactly ONE intended production UDP port.\n\nUse exactly ONE peer for 10.60.0.10/32.\n\nNo duplicate AllowedIPs.\nNo PSK unless explicitly part of the design.\nNo stale wg-ext-test or temporary test instance.\n\nIf current WG production state is not valid:\nrepair only the production WG instance.\nDo not touch private backbone.\n\n==================================================\n3. WORM-OS CLIENT POLICY\n==================================================\n\nProduction target on worm-os:\n\nAddress:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nAllowedIPs:\n0.0.0.0/0\n\nPersistentKeepalive:\n25\n\nDNS:\nprefer OPNsense protected resolver once validated\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:<production-WG-port>\n\nWireGuard must be the privacy path.\n\n==================================================\n4. OPNsense WAN FIREWALL\n==================================================\n\nALLOW publicly:\n\nUDP/<production-WG-port>\ndestination:\n2.28.34.27\n\nDENY public management:\n\nTCP/22\nTCP/80\nTCP/443\n\nDo not expose DNS publicly.\n\nDo not expose private services publicly.\n\n==================================================\n5. OPNsense WG FIREWALL\n==================================================\n\nAllow from:\n\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nto:\n\nInternet\n\nthrough OPNsense.\n\nKeep policy explicit.\n\nDo not create unnecessary access to private management networks.\n\nPreserve intended private access separately.\n\n==================================================\n6. OUTBOUND NAT\n==================================================\n\nConfigure/verify one outbound NAT policy:\n\nSOURCE:\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nINTERFACE:\nWAN\n\nTRANSLATION:\nWAN address\n\nExpected NAT:\n\n10.60.0.10\n ->\nOPNsense\n ->\n2.28.34.27\n\nDo not create duplicate NAT rules.\n\nDo not NAT private-to-private traffic unnecessarily.\n\n==================================================\n7. LIVE WG GATE\n==================================================\n\nBefore testing Internet, require:\n\nHANDSHAKE=RECENT\nRX>0\nTX>0\n\nIf handshake fails:\nSTOP.\n\nDo not continue to NAT/DNS debugging until WG is valid.\n\n==================================================\n8. INTERNET EGRESS TEST\n==================================================\n\nWith real worm-os Android client connected and:\n\nAllowedIPs=0.0.0.0/0\n\nverify:\n\n- HTTPS works\n- TCP works\n- Internet works\n\nUse at least two independent public-IP checks.\n\nExpected:\n\nPUBLIC_IPV4=2.28.34.27\n\nDo not infer PASS only from NAT configuration.\n\n==================================================\n9. DNS\n==================================================\n\nTarget protected resolver:\n\n10.60.0.1\n\nVerify from real worm-os client:\n\n- UDP/53\n- TCP/53 where applicable\n- ordinary hostname resolution\n- no requirement for public WAN DNS exposure\n\nIf 10.60.0.1 is not yet ready:\nmark DNS PENDING and keep a temporary diagnostic resolver only long\nenough to isolate the problem.\n\nProduction target remains protected DNS through OPNsense.\n\n==================================================\n10. IPV6 PRIVACY\n==================================================\n\nAudit Android/Worm OS IPv6.\n\nProduction must not allow direct IPv6 bypass outside OPNsense.\n\nAcceptable production states:\n\nA. IPv6 tunneled through OPNsense\n\nOR\n\nB. IPv6 explicitly blocked for worm-os protected mode\n\nReport:\n\nIPV6_PRIVACY=\nPASS / FAIL / PENDING\n\nDo not mark production PASS if IPv6 leaks.\n\n==================================================\n11. FAIL-CLOSED\n==================================================\n\nTarget behavior:\n\nWireGuard UP:\nInternet available through OPNsense\n\nWireGuard DOWN:\nworm-os privacy-sensitive networking must not silently fall back to\ndirect mobile/Wi-Fi Internet in final system design.\n\nFor current Android app test:\nreport capability/readiness.\n\nFor system worm-os integration:\nmark native fail-closed as later dependency if not yet implemented.\n\n==================================================\n12. PUBLIC IP SEPARATION\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\nworm-os public:\n2.28.34.27\n\nrobot public:\n162.55.6.173\n\nDo not mix them.\n\nworm-os traffic must NOT egress through:\n162.55.6.173\n\nrobot traffic must not be changed by this patch.\n\n==================================================\n13. PRIVATE BACKBONE REGRESSION\n==================================================\n\nMust remain:\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nPRIVATE:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nVerify existing PASS state remains unchanged.\n\nDo not modify FireHOL.\n\n==================================================\n14. PERSISTENCE\n==================================================\n\nVerify persistent:\n\n- OPNsense WG instance\n- WG peer\n- AllowedIP\n- WAN WG rule\n- WG interface PF rules\n- outbound NAT\n- DNS ACL\n- IPv6 policy\n\nNo essential runtime-only commands.\n\n==================================================\n15. SECRET SAFETY\n==================================================\n\nNever print/store:\n\n- OPNsense WG private key\n- worm-os WG private key\n- PSK\n- passwords\n- SSH private keys\n- Hetzner token\n\nPublic WG keys are allowed.\n\nRun secret scan.\n\n==================================================\n16. OUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nPATCH-NET-WORMOS-DEDICATED-PUBLIC-EGRESS-V1/\n\nInclude:\n\nREADME.md\nARCHITECTURE.md\nOPNSENSE-WG.md\nWORMOS-CLIENT.md\nWAN-FIREWALL.md\nWG-FIREWALL.md\nNAT.md\nDNS.md\nIPV6.md\nFAIL-CLOSED.md\nPUBLIC-IP-SEPARATION.md\nREGRESSION.md\nPERSISTENCE.md\nSECURITY.md\nTESTS.md\nROLLBACK.md\nmodified-files.txt\ntest-results.txt\n\n==================================================\nFINAL GATE\n==================================================\n\nDo not mark PASS unless:\n\nPASS OPNsense public 2.28.34.27\nPASS OPNsense WG 10.60.0.1/24\nPASS worm-os 10.60.0.10/32\nPASS production WG handshake\nPASS RX/TX\nPASS AllowedIPs 0.0.0.0/0\nPASS outbound NAT\nPASS Internet\nPASS public IPv4 = 2.28.34.27\nPASS no egress via 162.55.6.173\nPASS OPNsense WAN 22/80/443 denied\nPASS private backbone unchanged\nPASS robot untouched\nPASS persistent configuration\nPASS no secrets\n\nDNS may be:\nPASS / PENDING\n\nIPv6 may be:\nPASS / PENDING\n\nNative system fail-closed may be:\nPENDING\n\nBut production privacy release must not be declared fully ready until\nDNS, IPv6 and native fail-closed are PASS.\n\nFinal output:\n\nWORM-OS DEDICATED PUBLIC EGRESS:\nPASS / PARTIAL / FAIL\n\nWORM-OS PRIVATE:\n10.60.0.10\n\nOPNSENSE WG:\n10.60.0.1\n\nWORM-OS PUBLIC:\n2.28.34.27\n\nROBOT PUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nWG HANDSHAKE:\nPASS / FAIL\n\nNAT:\nPASS / FAIL\n\nINTERNET:\nPASS / FAIL\n\nDNS:\nPASS / PENDING / FAIL\n\nIPV6 PRIVACY:\nPASS / PENDING / FAIL\n\nFAIL-CLOSED:\nPASS / PENDING / FAIL\n\nPRIVATE BACKBONE:\nUNCHANGED / FAIL\n\nROBOT:\nUNTOUCHED / FAIL\n\nDo not start OTA or system-WireGuard implementation automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07ad3-959f-7993-a9d5-fb662859be98","create_time":1788767081.9164922,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07add-462d-7cd3-9b81-f802717e202b","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-WG-PRODUCTION-KNOWN-GOOD-V2.\n\nGOAL:\nReplace ONLY the failing OPNsense worm-os WireGuard instance with a\nfresh production instance built using the exact method previously\nproven by the successful external WireGuard control test.\n\nCURRENT EVIDENCE:\n\nWAN_51820_PACKETS=9\nUDP_RESPONSES_OUT=0\nWG_HANDSHAKE=NEVER\nWG_RX=0\nWG_TX=0\n\nNAT=PASS\nPRIVATE_BACKBONE=PASS\nROBOT=UNTOUCHED\n\nTherefore DO NOT modify:\n- NAT\n- DNS\n- private backbone\n- worm-robot\n- FireHOL\n- 10.90.0.0/16\n- public IPs\n\n==================================================\n1. BACKUP\n==================================================\n\nBackup /conf/config.xml.\n\nRecord current wg0/PF state without printing private keys.\n\n==================================================\n2. REMOVE ONLY FAILED WG PRODUCTION INSTANCE\n==================================================\n\nRemove:\n- current wg0 instance\n- current wg0 server identity\n- current peer association\n\nPreserve:\n- 10.60.0.0/24 design\n- PF egress policy\n- NAT\n- private routing\n\nDo not touch anything outside WireGuard instance ownership.\n\n==================================================\n3. CREATE FRESH KNOWN-GOOD PRODUCTION WG\n==================================================\n\nUse the SAME creation/configuration mechanism that produced the\nprevious successful wg-ext-test handshake.\n\nCreate fresh production instance:\n\nwg0\n10.60.0.1/24\nUDP/51820\n\nGenerate a fresh OPNsense server keypair.\n\nNever print/store the private key outside authoritative OPNsense config.\n\nOutput ONLY:\n\nNEW_OPNSENSE_PUBLIC_KEY=<public key>\n\n==================================================\n4. CLIENT\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT reuse the current Android peer yet.\n\nSTOP after the fresh server is completely live and persistent.\n\nVerify:\n\nWG_INSTANCE_COUNT=1\nWG_ADDRESS=10.60.0.1/24\nLISTEN_PORT=51820\nPEER_COUNT=0\nUDP_51820_BOUND=YES\nPF_51820=PASS\n\nThen print exactly:\n\nCREATE NEW ANDROID PROFILE - WAITING FOR PUBLIC KEY\n\n==================================================\n5. IMPORTANT\n==================================================\n\nDo not proceed further.\n\nThe user will create ONE fresh Android WireGuard profile using:\n\nAddress:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nServer PublicKey:\n<NEW_OPNSENSE_PUBLIC_KEY>\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51820\n\nAllowedIPs:\n10.60.0.0/24,10.90.0.0/16\n\nDNS:\nempty\n\nKeepalive:\n25\n\nAndroid generates its private key locally.\n\nAfter the user supplies the new Android PUBLIC key, a subsequent step\nwill create exactly one peer and perform the handshake.\n\n==================================================\n6. REGRESSION\n==================================================\n\nBefore stopping verify:\n\nPRIVATE_BACKBONE=PASS\nROBOT=UNTOUCHED\nNAT=UNCHANGED\nOPNSENSE_PRIVATE=10.90.10.1\nWORM_ROBOT_PRIVATE=10.90.20.10\nOPNSENSE_PUBLIC=2.28.34.27\n\n==================================================\nFINAL OUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nPRODUCTION_WG_REBUILT=\nNEW_OPNSENSE_PUBLIC_KEY=\nWG_ADDRESS=\nLISTEN_PORT=\nPEER_COUNT=\nUDP_51820_BOUND=\nPF_51820=\nPRIVATE_BACKBONE=\nROBOT=\nNAT=\n\nCREATE NEW ANDROID PROFILE - WAITING FOR PUBLIC KEY\n\nDo not continue automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07add-4612-73c2-bfb3-2183ed41e2b7","create_time":1788767716.9099507,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07ae1-fc50-79d2-97cb-cff4522f956e","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"WORM-V2 IS ENABLED NOW.\n\nDo not change any configuration.\n\nAndroid currently shows:\nTX=2.31 KiB\nRX=0 B\n\nImmediately monitor OPNsense for 30 seconds:\n\n- WAN UDP/51820 inbound\n- WAN UDP/51820 outbound\n- wg show wg0\n- PF rule matching UDP/51820\n\nReport only:\n\nWAN_51820_IN=\nWAN_51820_OUT=\nPF_51820=\nHANDSHAKE=\nWG_RX=\nWG_TX=\n\nDo not modify:\n- keys\n- peer\n- wg0\n- PF\n- NAT\n- DNS\n- FireHOL\n- private backbone\n\nIf WAN_51820_IN > 0 and HANDSHAKE=NEVER:\nSTOP and preserve evidence.\n\nIf HANDSHAKE=RECENT:\nimmediately test:\n10.60.0.1\n10.90.10.1\n10.90.20.10\n10.90.20.10:22"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07ae1-fc35-7610-875e-541bef9fa52a","create_time":1788768025.6800842,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07ae3-aca5-7fe0-8d2f-999234ce137f","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Perform FINAL PF LOCAL-DELIVERY CHECK for UDP/51820.\n\nNO configuration changes.\n\nKnown:\nWAN_51820_IN=6\nWAN_51820_OUT=0\nHANDSHAKE=NEVER\nWG_RX=0\nWG_TX=0\nPF_51820=UNKNOWN\n\nDo NOT touch:\nWireGuard keys\nwg0\nNAT\nDNS\nFireHOL\nprivate backbone\nrobot\n\n1. Inspect the LIVE effective pf ruleset with pfctl.\n\nFind the exact rule applicable to:\n\ninterface: WAN/vtnet0\nprotocol: UDP\ndestination: 2.28.34.27\ndestination port: 51820\n\nDo not rely only on config.xml.\n\n2. Report the exact effective action:\n\nPASS / BLOCK / NO_MATCH\n\nand its packet counter.\n\n3. Inspect pflog while Android tunnel remains enabled.\n\nDetermine whether UDP/51820 is:\nPASS\nBLOCK\nor not logged.\n\n4. Verify 2.28.34.27:51820 is delivered locally and that the\nWireGuard UDP socket is bound.\n\n5. Check for interfering:\nfloating rules\nquick rules\nreply-to\nroute-to\nrdr\nNAT\ninterface groups\n\n6. Do NOT remediate.\n\nReturn exactly:\n\nPF_51820_ACTION=\nPF_51820_COUNTER=\nPFLOG_51820=\nLOCAL_UDP_51820_BOUND=\nLOCAL_DELIVERY=\nREPLY_TO=\nROUTE_TO=\nRDR=\nFAILING_LAYER=\n\nIf PF_51820_ACTION is not PASS:\nFAILING_LAYER=PF\n\nIf PF passes and LOCAL_DELIVERY=YES but WireGuard sends no response:\nFAILING_LAYER=WIREGUARD_AUTH_RUNTIME\n\nNo changes."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07ae3-ac8b-7092-a37d-b9981f860d26","create_time":1788768136.3570037,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07ae6-b4c2-79c2-baaf-52a08b5c0224","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-WG-RUNTIME-CONTROL-PRODUCTION.\n\nPURPOSE:\nProve and repair ONLY the OPNsense WireGuard runtime layer.\n\nKNOWN FACTS:\n\nWAN UDP/51820 = RECEIVED\nPF = PASS\nLOCAL UDP/51820 = BOUND\nLOCAL DELIVERY = YES\nRDR = NO\nROUTE_TO = NONE\n\nBut:\n\nHANDSHAKE = NEVER\nWG RX/TX = 0\nUDP RESPONSE = 0\n\nPrivate backbone = PASS\nRobot = DO NOT TOUCH\n\n==================================================\nDO NOT MODIFY\n==================================================\n\nDo not modify:\n\n- worm-robot\n- FireHOL\n- 10.90.0.0/16\n- 10.90.10.1\n- 10.90.20.10\n- NAT\n- DNS\n- public IPs\n- Android profile/key\n\nAndroid public key remains:\n\nGxW0INqHdogkKP/sTa+KVSOVm1mq8GQIysJehVVsM38=\n\nAndroid address:\n\n10.60.0.10/32\n\n==================================================\n1. BACKUP\n==================================================\n\nBackup current OPNsense configuration.\n\nRecord current production wg0 state.\n\nNever print private keys.\n\n==================================================\n2. CREATE ISOLATED RUNTIME CONTROL\n==================================================\n\nUsing the SAME low-level WireGuard creation method that previously\nproduced the successful wg-ext-test handshake, create a temporary\nexternal control instance.\n\nUse:\n\ninterface:\nwg-runtime-test\n\naddress:\n10.62.0.1/30\n\nport:\n51823\n\nGenerate a fresh temporary server identity.\n\nCreate peer:\n\nPublicKey:\nGxW0INqHdogkKP/sTa+KVSOVm1mq8GQIysJehVVsM38=\n\nAllowedIP:\n10.62.0.2/32\n\nAdd minimum temporary WAN PF allow for UDP/51823.\n\nDo not change production wg0.\n\nOutput:\n\nRUNTIME_TEST_SERVER_PUBLIC_KEY=<public key>\nRUNTIME_TEST_ENDPOINT=2.28.34.27:51823\n\nThen STOP and print:\n\nCONFIGURE ANDROID RUNTIME TEST\n\n==================================================\n3. ANDROID RUNTIME TEST\n==================================================\n\nUser will temporarily create/use a profile:\n\nAddress:\n10.62.0.2/32\n\nPeer PublicKey:\n<RUNTIME_TEST_SERVER_PUBLIC_KEY>\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51823\n\nAllowedIPs:\n10.62.0.0/30\n\nKeepalive:\n25\n\nUse the SAME Android Interface key whose public key is:\n\nGxW0INqHdogkKP/sTa+KVSOVm1mq8GQIysJehVVsM38=\n\nNo DNS.\nNo PSK.\n\n==================================================\n4. LIVE GATE\n==================================================\n\nAfter user confirms profile configured:\n\nmonitor:\n\nWAN UDP/51823\nwg show wg-runtime-test\n\nPrint:\n\nENABLE RUNTIME TEST NOW\n\nPASS requires:\n\nWAN packets > 0\nHANDSHAKE RECENT\nRX > 0\nTX > 0\n\n==================================================\n5. INTERPRETATION\n==================================================\n\nIf runtime-test HANDSHAKE=RECENT:\n\nANDROID=PROVEN\nOPNSENSE_KERNEL_WG=PROVEN\n\nThen compare runtime-test with production wg0 and identify the exact\nruntime/config-generation difference.\n\nDo NOT regenerate Android keys.\n\nDo NOT modify private backbone.\n\nIf runtime-test also receives WAN packets but HANDSHAKE=NEVER:\n\nSTOP.\n\nReport that the failure is common to the current Android identity /\nexternal authentication path.\n\n==================================================\n6. DO NOT CLEAN UP YET\n==================================================\n\nKeep the successful runtime test intact until reviewed.\n\nDo not promote or delete anything automatically.\n\n==================================================\nOUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nRUNTIME_TEST_READY=\nRUNTIME_TEST_SERVER_PUBLIC_KEY=\nRUNTIME_TEST_PORT=51823\nPRODUCTION_WG_UNCHANGED=\nPRIVATE_BACKBONE=\nROBOT=\n\nCONFIGURE ANDROID RUNTIME TEST"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07ae6-b4a7-7820-8b0b-0541ce3dc383","create_time":1788768335.042107,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07aea-2e7a-7bd3-a5fd-3d9928ffa3d4","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Android runtime-test is configured.\n\nAndroid public key:\nGxW0INqHdogkKP/sTa+KVSOVm1mq8GQIysJehVVsM38=\n\nAddress:\n10.62.0.2/32\n\nServer public key:\nUzComED2z/LoaFwRlgRlazynnvv1hSxiMrFNTGaTkGQ=\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51823\n\nAllowedIPs:\n10.62.0.0/30\n\nKeepalive:\n25\n\nConfigure the existing wg-runtime-test peer:\n\nPublicKey:\nGxW0INqHdogkKP/sTa+KVSOVm1mq8GQIysJehVVsM38=\n\nAllowedIP:\n10.62.0.2/32\n\nNO PSK.\n\nDo not modify production wg0 or any other networking.\n\nStart monitoring WAN UDP/51823 and wg-runtime-test.\n\nPrint:\n\nENABLE RUNTIME TEST NOW\n\nThen monitor 30 seconds and report:\n\nWAN_51823_IN=\nWAN_51823_OUT=\nHANDSHAKE=\nRX=\nTX=\nRUNTIME_TEST=\n\nDo not remediate or clean up."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07aea-2e5f-7a02-a505-da79d14a7bf3","create_time":1788768562.81078,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07aeb-e5ad-7613-a605-9c82aa3e9abd","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-WG-PROMOTE-KNOWN-GOOD-PRODUCTION.\n\nWE NOW HAVE CONCLUSIVE EVIDENCE:\n\nwg-runtime-test:\nHANDSHAKE=RECENT\nRX=244\nTX=124\nRUNTIME_TEST=PASS\n\nSame real Android device and Android WireGuard identity.\n\nTherefore:\nANDROID IS PROVEN GOOD.\nOPNsense WireGuard runtime is proven good.\n\nSTOP ALL FURTHER WIREGUARD DEBUGGING.\n\n==================================================\nGOAL\n==================================================\n\nPromote the KNOWN-GOOD wg-runtime-test implementation into the\npermanent worm-os production WireGuard service.\n\nDo NOT attempt to repair the broken production wg0.\n\nUse the known-good runtime-test configuration/creation method and\nknown-good server identity as the production foundation.\n\n==================================================\nFROZEN ARCHITECTURE\n==================================================\n\nOPNsense PUBLIC:\n2.28.34.27\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nPRIVATE BACKBONE:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nWORM-OS WG NETWORK:\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nOPNsense WG:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nworm-os:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nDo not change these.\n\n==================================================\n1. BACKUP\n==================================================\n\nBackup /conf/config.xml.\n\nRecord:\n\n- broken production wg0\n- successful wg-runtime-test\n- PF\n- NAT\n- routes\n\nNever print private keys.\n\n==================================================\n2. PRESERVE KNOWN-GOOD IDENTITY\n==================================================\n\nThe successful wg-runtime-test server keypair is KNOWN-GOOD.\n\nDo NOT regenerate it.\n\nMove/persist that identity into the authoritative production\nWireGuard configuration.\n\nNever print its private key.\n\nThe corresponding public key already supplied to Android remains\nauthoritative.\n\n==================================================\n3. PRODUCTION INSTANCE\n==================================================\n\nCreate final production WireGuard using the SAME known-good\nconfiguration structure/method as wg-runtime-test.\n\nFinal:\n\ninterface:\nwg0\n\naddress:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\npeer Android public key:\nGxW0INqHdogkKP/sTa+KVSOVm1mq8GQIysJehVVsM38=\n\nAllowedIP:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nNO PSK.\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nFor the first production validation, KEEP THE KNOWN-GOOD PORT:\n\nUDP/51823\n\nDo NOT move back to 51820 yet.\n\nEndpoint therefore:\n\n2.28.34.27:51823\n\nWe already proved this external path works.\n\n==================================================\n4. REMOVE BROKEN PRODUCTION\n==================================================\n\nOnly after the known-good production instance is persistent:\n\nremove the BROKEN old wg0/51820 configuration.\n\nRemove obsolete WAN UDP/51820 rule.\n\nVerify UDP/51820 is no longer exposed/listening.\n\nDo not remove the successful server identity.\n\n==================================================\n5. PF\n==================================================\n\nProduction WAN:\n\nALLOW UDP/51823\ndestination 2.28.34.27\n\nKeep:\n\nTCP/22 DENY\nTCP/80 DENY\nTCP/443 DENY\n\nNo public DNS.\n\n==================================================\n6. NAT\n==================================================\n\nPreserve existing PASS NAT:\n\n10.60.0.0/24\n ->\nWAN\n ->\n2.28.34.27\n\nDo not duplicate NAT.\n\n==================================================\n7. PRIVATE ROUTING\n==================================================\n\nPreserve:\n\n10.60.0.0/24\n ->\nOPNsense\n ->\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nNo NAT between WG and private backbone.\n\nDo not modify the PASS backbone.\n\n==================================================\n8. ANDROID FINAL PROFILE\n==================================================\n\nFinal Android production profile:\n\nAddress:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nServer PublicKey:\nUSE THE SAME SERVER PUBLIC KEY THAT JUST PASSED RUNTIME_TEST\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51823\n\nAllowedIPs initially:\n10.60.0.0/24,10.90.0.0/16\n\nKeepalive:\n25\n\nNO PSK.\n\nUse the existing Android private identity whose public key is:\n\nGxW0INqHdogkKP/sTa+KVSOVm1mq8GQIysJehVVsM38=\n\n==================================================\n9. PRIVATE VALIDATION\n==================================================\n\nAfter production persistence, with Android connected:\n\nrequire:\n\nHANDSHAKE=RECENT\nRX>0\nTX>0\n\nThen test:\n\n10.60.0.1\n10.90.10.1\n10.90.20.10\n10.90.20.10:22\n\nVerify return path.\n\n==================================================\n10. INTERNET VALIDATION\n==================================================\n\nONLY after private validation PASS:\n\nchange Android AllowedIPs to:\n\n0.0.0.0/0\n\nDo not change server keys or endpoint.\n\nTest:\n\nInternet\nHTTPS\npublic IPv4\n\nExpected public IPv4:\n\n2.28.34.27\n\nIf Internet fails:\ndebug only PF/NAT/egress.\n\nDO NOT touch WireGuard identity because handshake is already proven.\n\n==================================================\n11. REGRESSION\n==================================================\n\nMust remain:\n\nPRIVATE_BACKBONE=PASS\nROBOT=UNTOUCHED\nROBOT_PUBLIC=162.55.6.173\nROBOT_PRIVATE=10.90.20.10\nOPNSENSE_PRIVATE=10.90.10.1\nip_forward robot=0\n\n==================================================\n12. PERSISTENCE\n==================================================\n\nProduction known-good WireGuard MUST survive service regeneration and\nreboot.\n\nVerify authoritative OPNsense persistent configuration contains:\n\n- production instance\n- known-good server identity\n- 10.60.0.1/24\n- UDP/51823\n- Android peer\n- 10.60.0.10/32\n- PF\n- NAT\n\nNo runtime-only production state.\n\n==================================================\nFINAL\n==================================================\n\nReport:\n\nKNOWN_GOOD_PROMOTED=\nPRODUCTION_PORT=\nPRODUCTION_HANDSHAKE=\nPRODUCTION_RX=\nPRODUCTION_TX=\n\nOPNSENSE_WG=\nOPNSENSE_PRIVATE=\nWORM_ROBOT_PRIVATE=\nPRIVATE_SSH=\nRETURN_PATH=\n\nFULL_TUNNEL=\nPUBLIC_IPV4=\n\nOLD_51820_REMOVED=\nPERSISTENT=\nPRIVATE_BACKBONE=\nROBOT=\n\nWORM_OS_NETWORK=\nPASS / PARTIAL / FAIL\n\nDo not change to another WireGuard port.\nDo not regenerate any WireGuard keys.\nDo not start OTA/system-WireGuard work automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07aeb-e591-79b3-9131-263f7076cbca","create_time":1788768675.2458375,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07af0-9534-7c81-af36-7d7098529223","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Continue validation of PATCH-WG-PROMOTE-KNOWN-GOOD-PRODUCTION.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nProduction WireGuard is now KNOWN-GOOD.\n\nPRODUCTION_HANDSHAKE=RECENT\nRX>0\nTX>0\nPORT=51823\nPERSISTENT=YES\n\nDO NOT:\n- regenerate keys\n- recreate wg0\n- change UDP port\n- change peer\n- change private backbone\n- modify robot\n- modify FireHOL\n\n==================================================\nPHASE 1 — PRIVATE CLIENT VALIDATION\n==================================================\n\nAndroid remains connected with split tunnel:\n\nAddress:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51823\n\nAllowedIPs:\n10.60.0.0/24,10.90.0.0/16\n\nWhile Android is connected, monitor OPNsense and test/trace:\n\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.60.0.1\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.10.1\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.20.10\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.20.10:22\n\nVerify bidirectional return path using captures/counters.\n\nIf any private hop fails:\nfix ONLY the exact PF/routing rule responsible.\nDo not touch WireGuard identity.\n\nRequired:\n\nANDROID_TO_OPNSENSE_WG=PASS\nANDROID_TO_OPNSENSE_PRIVATE=PASS\nANDROID_TO_ROBOT_PRIVATE=PASS\nANDROID_TO_ROBOT_SSH=PASS\nRETURN_PATH=PASS\n\n==================================================\nPHASE 2 — FULL TUNNEL\n==================================================\n\nONLY after Phase 1 PASS:\n\nPrint:\n\nPRIVATE FOUNDATION PASS - SET ANDROID ALLOWEDIPS TO 0.0.0.0/0\n\nWait for user.\n\nDo not alter WireGuard keys/server configuration.\n\nAfter Android uses:\n\nAllowedIPs:\n0.0.0.0/0\n\nverify:\n\nHANDSHAKE remains RECENT\nRX/TX increase\n\nVerify existing NAT:\n\n10.60.0.0/24\n ->\nOPNsense WAN\n ->\n2.28.34.27\n\nThen test Internet egress.\n\nExpected public IPv4:\n\n2.28.34.27\n\nUse two independent external IPv4 observations.\n\n==================================================\nDNS\n==================================================\n\nFor the first full-tunnel test the Android client may temporarily use:\n\nDNS:\n1.1.1.1\n\nThis is diagnostic only.\n\nDo NOT change OPNsense DNS yet.\n\nOnce Internet egress PASS is established, protected DNS 10.60.0.1\nwill be handled separately.\n\n==================================================\nFINAL\n==================================================\n\nReport:\n\nWG_HANDSHAKE=\nANDROID_TO_OPNSENSE_WG=\nANDROID_TO_OPNSENSE_PRIVATE=\nANDROID_TO_ROBOT_PRIVATE=\nANDROID_TO_ROBOT_SSH=\nRETURN_PATH=\n\nFULL_TUNNEL=\nNAT=\nPUBLIC_IPV4=\n\nPRIVATE_BACKBONE=\nROBOT=\nPRODUCTION_WG=\n\nDo not modify production WireGuard.\nDo not start DNS/IPv6/system-WG/OTA work automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07af0-9519-73b0-ad37-de5f6da0ff13","create_time":1788768982.3242114,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07afe-037b-7f02-8ac7-79cca6a2c705","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"PRODUCTION WG IS ALREADY PASS.\nDO NOT CHANGE ANY CONFIGURATION.\n\nMonitor for Android-originated traffic NOW to:\n\n10.60.0.1\n10.90.10.1\n10.90.20.10\n10.90.20.10:22\n\nThe user is generating these tests manually from Android/Termux.\n\nObserve:\n- wg0\n- PF\n- private interface\n- return traffic\n\nReport:\n\nANDROID_TO_OPNSENSE_WG=\nANDROID_TO_OPNSENSE_PRIVATE=\nANDROID_TO_ROBOT_PRIVATE=\nANDROID_TO_ROBOT_SSH=\nRETURN_PATH=\nWG_HANDSHAKE=\nPRIVATE_FOUNDATION=\n\nDo not modify WireGuard, PF, routing, FireHOL, NAT or DNS."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07afe-0345-77b1-ac87-d5387b9ef0b9","create_time":1788769862.5237947,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07b03-fd80-7730-83a8-58fac92dc934","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-NET-WG-TO-PRIVATE-FORWARDING.\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nProduction WireGuard is PASS and FROZEN.\n\nDO NOT:\n- recreate wg0\n- regenerate keys\n- change UDP/51823\n- change Android\n- change NAT Internet\n- modify worm-robot public configuration\n- modify private addressing\n\nCURRENT VERIFIED:\n\nAndroid:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nOPNsense WG:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nWG HANDSHAKE:\nRECENT / PASS\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nPRIVATE BACKBONE:\nPASS\n\nBut Android currently gets:\n\n10.90.10.1 = unreachable\n10.90.20.10 = unreachable\n\n==================================================\nGOAL\n==================================================\n\nEnable routed PRIVATE traffic:\n\n10.60.0.0/24\n       |\n       v\nOPNsense wg0\n       |\n       v\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nwithout NAT between these networks.\n\n==================================================\n1. TRACE FIRST\n==================================================\n\nWhile Android generates traffic, capture simultaneously:\n\nwg0\nOPNsense private interface\nPF logs/counters\n\nTrace:\n\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.10.1\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.20.10\n\nDetermine exact failing hop.\n\n==================================================\n2. OPNsense WG PF POLICY\n==================================================\n\nEnsure effective PF permits required traffic entering from wg0.\n\nAllow:\n\nsource:\n10.60.0.0/24\n\ndestination:\n10.90.10.1\n\nrequired diagnostic/private management traffic.\n\nAllow routed traffic:\n\nsource:\n10.60.0.0/24\n\ndestination:\n10.90.20.10\n\nInitially permit:\nICMP\nTCP/22\n\nDo NOT create:\n10.60.0.0/24 -> any\n\nDo NOT expose WAN management.\n\n==================================================\n3. FORWARDING / ROUTING\n==================================================\n\nOPNsense is the router between:\n\n10.60.0.0/24\nand\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nVerify forwarding is enabled on OPNsense as required by its firewall/router role.\n\nVerify route:\n\n10.60.0.0/24 -> wg0\n\nVerify private route toward:\n\n10.90.20.10\n\nDo not change Hetzner private addressing.\n\n==================================================\n4. NO PRIVATE NAT\n==================================================\n\nVerify there is NO NAT for:\n\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.90.0.0/16\n\nTraffic must remain:\n\nSOURCE=10.60.0.10\n\nwhen observed on the private side.\n\nInternet outbound NAT remains unchanged.\n\n==================================================\n5. RETURN PATH\n==================================================\n\nThis is critical.\n\nVerify worm-robot/Hetzner private routing can return:\n\n10.90.20.10\n ->\n10.60.0.0/24\n ->\nOPNsense\n ->\nwg0\n\nIf worm-robot lacks a return route for 10.60.0.0/24, implement the\nnarrow persistent route required by the frozen architecture.\n\nDo not enable ip_forward on worm-robot.\n\nworm-robot must remain:\n\nnet.ipv4.ip_forward=0\n\n==================================================\n6. FIREHOL\n==================================================\n\nFireHOL remains ACTIVE.\n\nPermit only required private traffic arriving from:\n\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nto:\n\n10.90.20.10\n\nInitially:\n\nICMP for validation\nTCP/22 for private SSH\n\nDo not create trusted-any.\n\nDo not change public FireHOL policy.\n\n==================================================\n7. LIVE TEST\n==================================================\n\nAfter applying the minimum routing/PF changes, ask user to run:\n\nping -c 4 10.60.0.1\nping -c 4 10.90.10.1\nping -c 4 10.90.20.10\n\nMonitor all three paths.\n\nFor TCP/22, use a server-side SYN/counter test if Android nc tooling\nis unreliable.\n\n==================================================\n8. REQUIRED PASS\n==================================================\n\nWG_HANDSHAKE=PASS\nANDROID_TO_10_60_0_1=PASS\nANDROID_TO_10_90_10_1=PASS\nANDROID_TO_10_90_20_10=PASS\nANDROID_TO_ROBOT_TCP22=PASS\nRETURN_PATH=PASS\nPRIVATE_NAT=NONE\nFIREHOL=ACTIVE\nROBOT_IP_FORWARD=0\n\n==================================================\nFINAL\n==================================================\n\nReport:\n\nWG_HANDSHAKE=\nWG_TO_OPNSENSE=\nWG_TO_OPNSENSE_PRIVATE=\nWG_TO_ROBOT_PRIVATE=\nWG_TO_ROBOT_SSH=\nRETURN_PATH=\nPRIVATE_NAT=\nFIREHOL=\nROBOT_IP_FORWARD=\nPRIVATE_WORM_FOUNDATION=\n\nDo not start full-tunnel Internet work automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07b03-fd65-7d93-9e59-740e7391525e","create_time":1788770254.20877,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07b0d-25fc-7240-806e-aef87f530f1b","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"NO MORE CONFIG CHANGES.\n\nProduction WG is PASS.\nPrivate return route has been added.\nFireHOL private ICMP/TCP22 policy is in place.\n\nThe user is NOW generating Android traffic manually.\n\nMonitor continuously:\n\nwg0\nOPNsense private interface\nworm-robot enp9s0.4000\nPF\nFireHOL counters\n\nObserve specifically:\n\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.60.0.1\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.10.1\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.20.10\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.20.10:22\n\nReport:\n\nANDROID_TO_OPNSENSE_WG=\nANDROID_TO_OPNSENSE_PRIVATE=\nANDROID_TO_ROBOT_PRIVATE=\nANDROID_TO_ROBOT_SSH=\nRETURN_PATH=\nPRIVATE_WORM_FOUNDATION=\n\nDo not modify anything."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07b0d-25e3-7e73-bd2c-25a81f323312","create_time":1788770854.396858,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07b12-b525-7f60-b832-62c37b0f425d","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-NET-WG-ROBOT-LAST-HOP.\n\nKNOWN PASS:\n\nAndroid 10.60.0.10 -> 10.60.0.1 = PASS\nAndroid 10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.10.1 = PASS\nProduction WG handshake = PASS\nPrivate backbone OPNsense <-> robot = PASS\n\nONLY FAILURE:\n\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.20.10\n\nDO NOT modify:\n- WireGuard\n- keys\n- UDP/51823\n- Android\n- NAT Internet\n- public IPs\n- robot public services\n\nWhile Android repeatedly pings 10.90.20.10, capture simultaneously:\n\n1. OPNsense wg0\n2. OPNsense private interface\n3. worm-robot enp9s0.4000\n4. FireHOL counters/logs\n\nDetermine:\n\nWG_PACKET_IN=\nOPNSENSE_PRIVATE_PACKET_OUT=\nROBOT_PRIVATE_PACKET_IN=\nROBOT_ICMP_REPLY_OUT=\nOPNSENSE_RETURN_PACKET_IN=\nWG_RETURN_PACKET_OUT=\n\nVerify source remains:\n10.60.0.10\n\nVerify worm-robot return route for:\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nVerify its actual next-hop is correct for the live Hetzner private\nnetwork. Do not assume the previously added 10.90.20.1 gateway is\ncorrect merely because the route exists.\n\nVerify FireHOL explicitly permits:\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.90.20.10 ICMP\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.90.20.10 TCP/22\n\nIf exactly one route or FireHOL rule is wrong:\nfix ONLY that item persistently.\n\nKeep:\nFireHOL ACTIVE\nrobot ip_forward=0\nPRIVATE_NAT=NONE\n\nThen retest:\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.20.10\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.20.10:22\n\nReport:\n\nFAILING_HOP=\nROBOT_ROUTE=\nFIREHOL=\nPING_ROBOT=\nSSH_ROBOT=\nRETURN_PATH=\nPRIVATE_WORM_FOUNDATION=\n\nDo not start full-tunnel work."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07b12-b4e4-75e0-89db-ac2157e75639","create_time":1788771218.7259364,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07b16-13a2-76d1-b96e-cfd0efd4869b","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Perform DETERMINISTIC WORM-ROBOT RETURN-PATH TEST.\n\nNO WireGuard changes.\nNO Android changes.\nNO PF/NAT/DNS changes.\nNO public-network changes.\n\nKnown facts:\n\nWG production = PASS\nAndroid -> 10.60.0.1 = PASS\nAndroid -> 10.90.10.1 = PASS\nAndroid -> 10.90.20.10 = FAIL\n\nCurrent worm-robot route reports:\n\n10.60.0.10 from 10.90.20.10\nvia 10.90.20.1\ndev enp9s0.4000\n\nGoal:\nprove whether 10.90.20.1 is actually the correct next-hop for returning\n10.60.0.0/24 traffic to OPNsense.\n\n1. On worm-robot record:\n\nip route get 10.60.0.10 from 10.90.20.10\nip neigh show dev enp9s0.4000\n\n2. Identify the actual Hetzner private gateway and actual private path\nbetween worm-robot and OPNsense from the live working 10.90 network.\n\nDo not assume 10.90.20.1 is correct.\n\n3. From worm-robot test reachability to:\n\n10.90.20.1\n10.90.10.1\n\nusing source 10.90.20.10 where possible.\n\n4. Capture enp9s0.4000 while worm-robot sends traffic to 10.90.10.1.\n\nDetermine the actual L2/L3 next-hop used by the already-working\nworm-robot -> OPNsense private path.\n\n5. Compare that known-good path with the installed route for\n10.60.0.0/24.\n\n6. If the 10.60.0.0/24 return route uses the WRONG gateway:\nreplace ONLY that route with the correct persistent return route.\n\nDo not change addresses, VLAN 4000, MTU 1400 or default route.\n\n7. Verify FireHOL still contains explicit:\n\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.90.20.10 ICMP\n10.60.0.0/24 -> 10.90.20.10 TCP/22\n\nKeep FireHOL ACTIVE.\nKeep ip_forward=0.\n\nReport:\n\nKNOWN_GOOD_PRIVATE_NEXT_HOP=\nCURRENT_WG_RETURN_NEXT_HOP=\nRETURN_GATEWAY_MATCH=\nRETURN_ROUTE=\nROBOT_TO_OPNSENSE_PRIVATE=\nFIREHOL=\nIP_FORWARD=\n\nIf route was corrected, print:\n\nRETEST ANDROID -> 10.90.20.10 NOW\n\nDo not modify anything else."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07b16-1388-7633-8e7c-aa2a137cb24a","create_time":1788771439.522464,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07b1d-9c38-77a3-ac43-20788e08a915","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-WORMOS-PRIVACY-GATE-V1.\n\nCURRENT FROZEN PASS:\n\nworm-os:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nOPNsense WG:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nProduction endpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51823\n\nAllowedIPs:\n0.0.0.0/0\n\nWG HANDSHAKE:\nPASS\n\nPRIVATE PATH:\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.10.1 PASS\n10.60.0.10 -> 10.90.20.10 PASS\n\nFULL-TUNNEL IPv4:\nPASS\n\nWORM-OS PUBLIC IPv4:\n2.28.34.27 PASS\n\nPRIVATE BACKBONE:\nPASS\n\nROBOT:\nUNTOUCHED\n\n==================================================\nABSOLUTE FREEZE\n==================================================\n\nDO NOT change:\n\n- WireGuard server identity\n- Android peer identity\n- UDP/51823\n- 10.60.0.0/24\n- 10.90.0.0/16\n- NAT that currently passes\n- Hetzner route 10.60.0.0/24 via 10.90.10.1\n- worm-robot\n- FireHOL\n- robot public 162.55.6.173\n- OPNsense public 2.28.34.27\n\nIf any frozen regression occurs:\nSTOP.\n\n==================================================\nGATE 1 — PROTECTED DNS\n==================================================\n\nProduction target:\n\nworm-os\n ->\nWireGuard\n ->\n10.60.0.1:53\n ->\nOPNsense resolver\n ->\nInternet\n\nConfigure/verify OPNsense resolver listens on the required PRIVATE/WG\ninterface only as appropriate.\n\nAllow from:\n\n10.60.0.0/24\n\nto:\n\n10.60.0.1\n\nUDP/53\nTCP/53\n\nDo NOT expose DNS on WAN.\n\nVerify WAN:\n2.28.34.27:53 DENY\n\nThen test from real worm-os client.\n\nRequired:\n\nDNS_UDP53=PASS\nDNS_TCP53=PASS\nDNS_RESOLUTION=PASS\nDNS_WAN_EXPOSURE=DENIED\n\nAfter PASS, Android production DNS becomes:\n\n10.60.0.1\n\nRemove dependency on temporary 1.1.1.1 from the production profile.\n\n==================================================\nGATE 2 — IPv6 PRIVACY\n==================================================\n\nAudit real Android IPv6 while WireGuard protected mode is active.\n\nCurrent tunnel is IPv4-only.\n\nUntil tunneled IPv6 is deliberately implemented, production policy is:\n\nNO DIRECT IPv6 BYPASS.\n\nVerify whether Android can reach Internet over native IPv6 outside\nWireGuard.\n\nIf direct IPv6 is possible:\nimplement the minimum protected-mode policy required to block IPv6\nleakage.\n\nDo not redesign IPv6 infrastructure.\n\nRequired:\n\nIPV6_DIRECT_BYPASS=NO\nIPV6_PUBLIC_LEAK=NO\n\nDo not mark PASS based only on absence of an OPNsense IPv6 address.\nTest from the real client.\n\n==================================================\nGATE 3 — FAIL-CLOSED READINESS\n==================================================\n\nCurrent Android WireGuard app is validation infrastructure.\n\nAudit behavior:\n\nWG ON:\nInternet must work through 2.28.34.27.\n\nWG OFF:\nrecord whether Android falls back to direct mobile/Wi-Fi Internet.\n\nDo NOT intentionally break the user's general phone networking.\n\nFor current Android app validation, classify:\n\nAPP_FAIL_CLOSED=\nPASS / NOT_AVAILABLE / PENDING\n\nThen audit worm-os native/system requirements for final fail-closed:\n\n- Always-on VPN integration\n- lockdown/block-without-VPN behavior\n- boot-before-unlock behavior\n- Wi-Fi/5G handover\n- tunnel restart\n- crash recovery\n- no direct network fallback\n- SELinux requirements\n- ConnectivityService/netd integration\n\nDO NOT implement system WireGuard yet.\n\nOutput exact development blockers.\n\n==================================================\nREGRESSION\n==================================================\n\nAfter DNS/IPv6 work re-test:\n\nWG_HANDSHAKE=PASS\nFULL_TUNNEL_IPV4=PASS\nPUBLIC_IPV4=2.28.34.27\n10.60.0.1=PASS\n10.90.10.1=PASS\n10.90.20.10=PASS\n\nRobot must remain untouched.\n\n==================================================\nOUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nPATCH-WORMOS-PRIVACY-GATE-V1/\n\nInclude:\n\nREADME.md\nDNS.md\nIPV6.md\nFAIL-CLOSED.md\nSYSTEM-WG-READINESS.md\nREGRESSION.md\nSECURITY.md\nTESTS.md\nBACKLOG.md\nmodified-files.txt\ntest-results.txt\n\n==================================================\nFINAL\n==================================================\n\nReport:\n\nWG_PRODUCTION=\nFULL_TUNNEL_IPV4=\nPUBLIC_IPV4=\n\nPROTECTED_DNS=\nDNS_WAN_EXPOSURE=\n\nIPV6_DIRECT_BYPASS=\nIPV6_PRIVACY=\n\nAPP_FAIL_CLOSED=\nSYSTEM_FAIL_CLOSED_READINESS=\n\nPRIVATE_WORM_FOUNDATION=\nPRIVATE_BACKBONE=\nROBOT=\n\nPRIVACY_GATE=\nPASS / PARTIAL / FAIL\n\nDo not modify production WireGuard.\nDo not start system-WireGuard or OTA implementation automatically."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07b1d-9c02-7f00-a0f5-11a80ff04cef","create_time":1788771933.2406182,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07b35-1427-7480-a9d8-933eac25bfbd","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"FINALIZE PATCH-WORMOS-PRIVACY-GATE-V1.\n\nNO CONFIGURATION CHANGES.\n\nThe real Android client is now configured with:\n\nAllowedIPs:\n0.0.0.0/0\n\nDNS:\n10.60.0.1\n\nProduction WG:\n2.28.34.27:51823\n\nMonitor real client traffic to:\n\n10.60.0.1 TCP/UDP 53\n\nVerify DNS queries and replies through wg0.\n\nAlso monitor whether any IPv6 traffic bypasses the protected path.\n\nDo not modify WireGuard, DNS, PF, NAT, routing, robot or backbone.\n\nReport:\n\nPROTECTED_DNS=\nDNS_UDP53=\nDNS_TCP53=\nDNS_RESOLUTION=\nDNS_WAN_EXPOSURE=\n\nIPV6_DIRECT_BYPASS=\nIPV6_PUBLIC_LEAK=\nIPV6_PRIVACY=\n\nWG_PRODUCTION=\nFULL_TUNNEL_IPV4=\nPUBLIC_IPV4=\nPRIVATE_WORM_FOUNDATION=\n\nPRIVACY_GATE=\nPASS / PARTIAL / FAIL"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07b35-140d-7f92-a9d4-42ee08750e8e","create_time":1788773471.2715094,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07cfd-829d-7432-a4a5-3e2456f6dcf6","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Crea e applica una patch dedicata:\n\nPATCH-INFRA-004-opnsense-hetzner-audit\n\nROOT:\n/opt/worm\n\nSCOPO:\n1. correggere il rilevamento stato OPNsense\n2. completare audit Hetzner\n3. NON modificare configurazione infrastrutturale\n\n==================================================\nA. OPNsense HEALTH CHECK\n==================================================\n\nStato attuale:\nWIREGUARD STATUS: PASS\nOPNSENSE STATUS: FAIL\n\nEsegui audit READ-ONLY.\n\nRileva automaticamente:\n- interfaccia WireGuard reale\n- peer associato a OPNsense\n- ultimo handshake\n- route verso OPNsense\n- IP privato OPNsense\n- AllowedIPs effettivi\n\nNon assumere wg0 o IP hardcoded.\n\nDetermina perché OPNsense risulta FAIL.\n\nNon usare solamente ping/ICMP:\nOPNsense può bloccare ICMP.\n\nImplementa health-check a livelli:\n\n1. WireGuard interface UP\n2. peer configurato\n3. handshake recente\n4. route valida\n5. reachability OPNsense con check read-only sicuro\n\nSe ICMP è bloccato ma il percorso VPN è verificabile tramite\nun servizio/endpoint già esistente, considera quello.\n\nNON:\n- modificare OPNsense\n- aggiungere firewall rule\n- cambiare WireGuard\n- cambiare route\n- riavviare servizi di rete\n\nAggiorna:\n/opt/worm/control/api-status/\n\ne l'endpoint:\n/api/status/network\n\nFormato:\n\n{\n  \"status\": \"operational|offline\",\n  \"wireguard\": true,\n  \"opnsense\": true,\n  \"interface\": \"...\",\n  \"handshake\": \"...\",\n  \"last_check\": \"...\"\n}\n\nNon esporre:\n- IP pubblici completi se non necessari\n- private keys\n- preshared keys\n- peer secrets\n- token\n\nFooter dashboard/About/Changelog:\n\nworm OS <=> OPNsense  ●\n\nverde = operational\nrosso = offline\n\n==================================================\nB. HETZNER API AUDIT\n==================================================\n\nUsa la configurazione/API Hetzner già presente.\n\nREAD-ONLY.\n\nNON stampare token.\n\nRileva:\n\nSERVERS\n- robot\n- worm / distribution\n- server ID\n- status\n- location/datacenter\n\nNETWORK\n- private networks\n- private IP\n- subnet\n- association server/network\n\nPUBLIC IP\nPer robot determina:\n- IPv4\n- IPv6\n- Primary IP\n- Floating IP\n- server assignment\n\nMaschera gli IP nell'output umano.\n\nDetermina:\n\nROBOT IP TYPE:\nPRIMARY / FLOATING / UNKNOWN\n\nROBOT IP REASSIGNABLE:\nYES / NO\n\nFIREWALL\n- firewall associati\n- NON modificare rules\n\nSTORAGE\n- volumes\n- snapshots/images utili per mirror\n\n==================================================\nC. MIRROR PLAN\n==================================================\n\nNON creare ancora il mirror.\nNON spostare IP.\n\nSe robot IP = FLOATING:\nprepara solamente il piano API per failover.\n\nSe robot IP = PRIMARY:\nnon tentare reassignment.\nSegnala che il mirror necessita IP pubblico dedicato.\n\nworm.coffee.pm deve restare:\nDISTRIBUTION PLANE\n- OTA\n- Apps\n- API\n- future mirror/failover\n\nworm.estixari.com:\nCONTROL PLANE\n- dashboard\n- changelog\n- About\n- status\n\n==================================================\nD. PATCH ARTIFACT\n==================================================\n\nSalva documentazione e script della patch in:\n\n/opt/worm/patches/PATCH-INFRA-004-opnsense-hetzner-audit/\n\nCrea:\nREADME.md\naudit.sh\nverify.sh\nrevert.sh\n\nrevert.sh deve ripristinare SOLO eventuali modifiche al codice\nhealth-check/dashboard, non modificare infrastruttura.\n\nRegistra la patch nel changelog/timeline Worm.\n\nEsegui secret scan.\n\n==================================================\nOUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nPATCH: PASS/FAIL\n\nWIREGUARD INTERFACE:\nWIREGUARD HANDSHAKE:\nOPNSENSE:\nOPNSENSE ROOT CAUSE:\nSTATUS API:\nFOOTER GLYPH:\n\nHETZNER API:\nROBOT:\nROBOT IP TYPE:\nROBOT IP REASSIGNABLE:\nPRIVATE NETWORK:\nFIREWALL AUDIT:\nSNAPSHOT/MIRROR SOURCE:\n\nMIRROR STRATEGY:\nINFRASTRUCTURE CHANGES: NONE\nSECRET SCAN:\nVERIFY:\nREADY FOR MIRROR STEP: YES/NO"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07cfd-824b-7f02-bc90-316a5bfb74bc","create_time":1788803383.9653797,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07d0c-a6f3-7420-917a-1a9defb0e91b","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Finalizza PATCH-INFRA-004 e consolida il Control Plane sotto /opt/worm.\n\nSTATO:\n- PATCH-INFRA-004: PASS\n- OPNsense: PASS\n- /api/status/network: operational\n- Hetzner API: PASS\n- robot non presente nella Hetzner Cloud API interrogata\n- robot IP reassignment: NON consentito/non dimostrato\n- mirror step: non ancora pronto\n\n1. CONTROL PLANE PATH\n\nAudit:\n  /opt/Android/dashboard\n\nConfrontalo con:\n  /opt/worm/control/\n\nMigra sotto /opt/worm/control/ tutto ciò che serve realmente\na worm.estixari.com.\n\nDopo la migrazione:\n- nessun componente runtime del Control Plane deve dipendere da\n  /opt/Android/dashboard\n- preserva git history e modifiche Worm\n- NON cancellare ancora /opt/Android/dashboard\n\nAggiorna PATCH-INFRA-004 e gli script al path definitivo /opt/worm.\n\n2. ABOUT\n\nIntegra definitivamente:\n  WORM Network Architecture\n\nnella pagina:\n  worm.estixari.com/about\n\nCrea un asset path stabile, ad esempio:\n  /opt/worm/control/dashboard/public/assets/about/worm-network-architecture.png\n\nSe il file immagine non è disponibile localmente, crea esattamente\nquesto target e segnala COPY REQUIRED.\nNON ridisegnare l'immagine.\n\nUI:\n- responsive\n- aspect ratio originale\n- desktop large/full container\n- mobile width 100%\n- tap/click per visualizzazione grande\n\n3. FOOTER STATUS\n\nUsa esclusivamente:\n/api/status/network\n\nVisualizzazione:\n\nworm OS <=> OPNsense   ●\n\n● verde quando status=operational\n● rosso altrimenti\n\nNon mostrare IP nel footer.\n\n4. HETZNER\n\nRegistra il risultato dell'audit:\n\n- Hetzner API: PASS\n- worm-opnsense: presente\n- worm-private: presente\n- robot: non presente nella Cloud API interrogata\n- robot IP type: UNKNOWN\n- robot IP reassignment: NO\n\nNON tentare reassignment.\nNON creare mirror in questo passaggio.\n\n5. CHANGELOG\n\nAggiungi PATCH-INFRA-004 alla timeline/changelog:\n- OPNsense health-check corretto\n- HTTPS private-path health probe\n- Hetzner read-only audit\n- nessuna modifica firewall/networking\n\n6. VERIFY\n\nNon modificare:\n- OPNsense\n- WireGuard\n- firewall\n- routing\n- OTA\n- GrapheneOS build\n\nOutput:\n\nCONTROL ROOT:\nOLD DASHBOARD DEPENDENCY:\nABOUT IMAGE:\nABOUT PAGE:\nSTATUS API:\nFOOTER:\nFOOTER CURRENT STATE:\nPATCH-INFRA-004:\nCHANGELOG:\nHETZNER AUDIT:\nROBOT IP REASSIGNMENT:\nSECRET SCAN:\nREADY TO DEPLOY CONTROL PLANE:"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07d0c-a6cc-7d73-b22e-d0d25bd13356","create_time":1788804376.3072994,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07d3a-3a88-7e80-aa16-993b366d23fe","role":"developer","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<skills_instructions>\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and a short path that can be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `/root/.codex/skills/.system`\n- `r1` = `/root/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote`\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when Codex should create a brand-new image, transform an existing image, or derive visual variants from references, and the output should be a bitmap asset rather than repo-native code or vector. Do not use when the task is better handled by editing existing SVG/vector/code-native assets, extending an established icon or logo system, or building the visual directly in HTML/CSS/canvas. (file: r0/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use for Codex models/pricing, scheduled tasks, skills, settings, setup, troubleshooting, customization, automations, and self-knowledge—including 'you,' 'your,' 'this app,' or 'this coding agent' when they refer to Codex—and for OpenAI APIs/products and ChatGPT Work. Also use for model choice/migration, prompting, SDKs, Responses, Realtime, agents, evals, and Chat/Work/Codex comparisons. Do not use for generic app/software tasks that merely mention Codex. (file: r0/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-creator: Create and scaffold plugin directories for Codex with a required `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, optional plugin folders/files, valid manifest defaults, and personal-marketplace entries by default. Use when Codex needs to create a new personal plugin, add optional plugin structure, generate or update marketplace entries for plugin ordering and availability metadata, or update an existing local plugin during development with the CLI-driven cachebuster and reinstall flow. (file: r0/plugin-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Create or update a Codex skill with appropriately scoped instructions and any needed supporting resources. (file: r0/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from another repo (including private repos). (file: r0/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n- deep-research-work:deep-research: Conduct thorough research using authoritative sources and produce evidence-backed findings with citations. Use this skill only when the user asks specifically for Deep research, uses $deep-research, or selects Deep research in Work mode. Do not use it for ordinary research requests. (file: r1/deep-research-work/0.1.14/skills/deep-research/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-management:plugin-management: Discover and suggest relevant plugins, inspect app permissions and dependencies, and manage plugin connections or removal. Use when the user asks about plugins or when a task would materially benefit from an external app, account, service, or data source that available tools cannot access. (file: r1/plugin-management/0.1.0/skills/plugin-management/SKILL.md)\n### How to use skills\n- Discovery: The list above is the skills available in this session (name + description + short path). Skill bodies live on disk at the listed paths after expanding the matching alias from `### Skill roots`.\n- Trigger rules: If the user names a skill (with `$SkillName` or plain text) OR the task clearly matches a skill's description shown above, you must use that skill for that turn. Multiple mentions mean use them all. Do not carry skills across turns unless re-mentioned.\n- Missing/blocked: If a named skill isn't in the list or the path can't be read, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\n- How to use a skill (progressive disclosure):\n  1) After deciding to use a skill, the main agent must expand the listed short `path` with the matching alias from `### Skill roots`, then open and read its `SKILL.md` completely before taking task actions. If a read is truncated or paginated, continue until EOF.\n  2) When `SKILL.md` references relative paths (e.g., `scripts/foo.py`), resolve them relative to the directory containing that expanded `SKILL.md` first, and only consider other paths if needed.\n  3) If `SKILL.md` points to extra folders such as `references/`, use its routing instructions to identify the files required for the task. The main agent must read each required instruction or reference file itself before acting on it. Do not delegate reading, summarizing, or interpreting skill instructions to a subagent. Subagents may still perform task work when the selected skill allows it.\n  4) If `scripts/` exist, prefer running or patching them instead of retyping large code blocks.\n  5) If `assets/` or templates exist, reuse them instead of recreating from scratch.\n- Coordination and sequencing:\n  - If multiple skills apply, choose the minimal set that covers the request and state the order you'll use them.\n  - Announce which skill(s) you're using and why (one short line). If you skip an obvious skill, say why.\n- Context hygiene:\n  - Progressive disclosure applies to selecting relevant files, not partially reading a selected instruction file. Do not load unrelated references, scripts, or assets.\n  - Avoid deep reference-chasing: prefer opening only files directly linked from `SKILL.md` unless you're blocked.\n  - When variants exist (frameworks, providers, domains), pick only the relevant reference file(s) and note that choice.\n- Safety and fallback: If a skill can't be applied cleanly (missing files, unclear instructions), state the issue, pick the next-best approach, and continue.\n</skills_instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<permissions instructions>\nFilesystem sandboxing defines which files can be read or written. `sandbox_mode` is `workspace-write`: The sandbox permits reading files, and editing files in `cwd` and `writable_roots`. Editing files in other directories requires approval. Network access is restricted.\n# Escalation Requests\n\nCommands are run outside the sandbox if they are approved by the user, or match an existing rule that allows it to run unrestricted. The command string is split into independent command segments at shell control operators, including but not limited to:\n\n- Pipes: |\n- Logical operators: &&, ||\n- Command separators: ;\n- Subshell boundaries: (...), $(...)\n\nEach resulting segment is evaluated independently for sandbox restrictions and approval requirements.\n\nExample:\n\ngit pull | tee output.txt\n\nThis is treated as two command segments:\n\n[\"git\", \"pull\"]\n\n[\"tee\", \"output.txt\"]\n\nCommands that use more advanced shell features like redirection (>, >>, <), substitutions ($(...), ...), environment variables (FOO=bar), or wildcard patterns (*, ?) will not be evaluated against rules, to limit the scope of what an approved rule allows.\n\n## How to request escalation\n\nIMPORTANT: To request approval to execute a command that will require escalated privileges:\n\n- Provide the `sandbox_permissions` parameter with the value `\"require_escalated\"`\n- Include a short question asking the user if they want to allow the action in `justification` parameter. e.g. \"Do you want to download and install dependencies for this project?\"\n- Optionally suggest a `prefix_rule` - this will be shown to the user with an option to persist the rule approval for future sessions.\n\nIf you run a command that is important to solving the user's query, but it fails because of sandboxing or with a likely sandbox-related network error (for example DNS/host resolution, registry/index access, or dependency download failure), rerun the command with \"require_escalated\". ALWAYS proceed to use the `justification` parameter - do not message the user before requesting approval for the command.\n\n## When to request escalation\n\nWhile commands are running inside the sandbox, here are some scenarios that will require escalation outside the sandbox:\n\n- You need to run a command that writes to a directory that requires it (e.g. running tests that write to /var)\n- You need to run a GUI app (e.g., open/xdg-open/osascript) to open browsers or files.\n- If you run a command that is important to solving the user's query, but it fails because of sandboxing or with a likely sandbox-related network error (for example DNS/host resolution, registry/index access, or dependency download failure), rerun the command with `require_escalated`. ALWAYS proceed to use the `sandbox_permissions` and `justification` parameters. do not message the user before requesting approval for the command.\n- You are about to take a potentially destructive action such as an `rm` or `git reset` that the user did not explicitly ask for.\n- Be judicious with escalating, but if completing the user's request requires it, you should do so - don't try and circumvent approvals by using other tools.\n\n## prefix_rule guidance\n\nWhen choosing a `prefix_rule`, request one that will allow you to fulfill similar requests from the user in the future without re-requesting escalation. It should be categorical and reasonably scoped to similar capabilities. You should rarely pass the entire command into `prefix_rule`.\n\n### Banned prefix_rules \nAvoid requesting overly broad prefixes that the user would be ill-advised to approve. For example, do not request [\"python3\"], [\"python\", \"-\"], or other similar prefixes that would allow arbitrary scripting.\nNEVER provide a prefix_rule argument for destructive commands like rm.\nNEVER provide a prefix_rule if your command uses a heredoc or herestring. \n\n### Examples\nGood examples of prefixes:\n- [\"npm\", \"run\", \"dev\"]\n- [\"gh\", \"pr\", \"check\"]\n- [\"cargo\", \"test\"]\n\n\n## Approved command prefixes\nThe following prefix rules have already been approved: - [\"curl\"]\n- [\"true\"]\n- [\"apt-get\"]\n- [\"apply_patch\"]\n- [\"scripts/build-debug.sh\"]\n- [\"/opt/Android/worm-os/scripts/proton-update-checker.py\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-I\"]\n- [\"git\", \"add\"]\n- [\"git\", \"push\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-fsSI\"]\n- [\"./gradlew\", \"lint\"]\n- [\"systemctl\", \"status\"]\n- [\"echo\", \"--- latest ---\"]\n- [\"./gradlew\", \"assembleDebug\"]\n- [\"./gradlew\", \"assembleRelease\"]\n- [\"./gradlew\", \":app:compileDebugKotlin\"]\n- [\"/opt/Android/worm/projectctl\", \"build\"]\n- [\"/opt/Android/Machina/projectctl\", \"build\"]\n- [\"/opt/Android/Machina/projectctl\", \"snapshot\"]\n- [\"gh\", \"pr\", \"view\"]\n- [\"rm\", \"-rf\", \"out\"]\n- [\"set\", \"-euo\", \"pipefail\"]\n- [\"git\", \"status\", \"--short\"]\n- [\"command\", \"-v\", \"apply_patch\"]\n- [\"./gradlew\", \"clean\", \"assembleDebug\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\", \"/opt/archive/DeltaIcon\"]\n- [\"test\", \"-w\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/state/gradle\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/state/gradle\"]\n- [\"./gradlew\", \"--no-daemon\", \"--no-configuration-cache\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/builds/17-08-2026_08-48-AM\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/builds/17-08-2026_09-12-AM\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/builds/17-08-2026_11-01-AM\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/builds/17-08-2026_11-04-AM\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/builds/17-08-2026_11-15-AM\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/builds/17-08-2026_11-27-AM\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/builds/17-08-2026_11-44-AM\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\", \"/opt/android-lab/backups/DeltaIOS/20260816-icons\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/snapshots/17-08-2026_10-59-AM\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/snapshots/17-08-2026_11-13-AM\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/snapshots/17-08-2026_11-26-AM\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/snapshots/17-08-2026_11-32-AM\"]\n- [\"rm\", \"-f\", \"/opt/Android/worm-os/.git/objects/pack/tmp_pack_e94OCp\"]\n- [\"/opt/Android/Machina/projectctl\", \"checkpoint\", \"pre-core-ui-v3-rebuild\"]\n- [\"/opt/Android/toolchains/android/sdk/platform-tools/adb\", \"devices\", \"-l\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"/tmp/approved-v3.tsv\", \"/opt/workspace/DeltaIcon-preview-all/approved-v3.tsv\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\", \"/opt/Android/worm/backups/codex-b00-7h1-p1-p2-motion-24-08-2026_08-53-PM\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"/tmp/deltaicon-bootstrap/build.gradle.kts\", \"/opt/projects/DeltaIcon/build.gradle.kts\"]\n- [\"/bin/bash\", \"-lc\", \"GRADLE_USER_HOME=/opt/Android/worm/.gradle-codex ./gradlew assembleDebug\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"/tmp/deltaicon-bootstrap/gradle.properties\", \"/opt/projects/DeltaIcon/gradle.properties\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"/tmp/DeltaIcon-phase20/app/build.gradle.kts\", \"/opt/projects/DeltaIcon/app/build.gradle.kts\"]\n- [\"/bin/bash\", \"-lc\", \"GRADLE_USER_HOME=/opt/Android/Machina/state/gradle ./gradlew :app:assembleDebug\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"/tmp/deltaicon-bootstrap/app/build.gradle.kts\", \"/opt/projects/DeltaIcon/app/build.gradle.kts\"]\n- [\"nl\", \"-ba\", \"app/build/intermediates/merged_manifest/debug/processDebugMainManifest/AndroidManifest.xml\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"/tmp/17-08-2026_09-12-AM.txt\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/builds/17-08-2026_09-12-AM/17-08-2026_09-12-AM.txt\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"/tmp/17-08-2026_08-29-AM.txt\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/reports/17-08-2026_08-29-AM/17-08-2026_08-29-AM.txt\"]\n- [\"/bin/bash\", \"-lc\", \"GRADLE_USER_HOME=/opt/Android/toolchains/gradle-home ./gradlew --offline :app:assembleRelease\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/outputs/17-08-2026_03-44-AM/app-debug.apk\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/outputs/17-08-2026_03-50-AM/app-debug.apk\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/outputs/17-08-2026_03-54-AM/app-debug.apk\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"/tmp/MACHINA_HOME_SETTINGS_BUGFIX_AUDIT.md\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/docs/MACHINA_HOME_SETTINGS_BUGFIX_AUDIT.md\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"/tmp/17-08-2026_08-29-AM.sha256\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/outputs/17-08-2026_08-29-AM/17-08-2026_08-29-AM.sha256\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"/tmp/17-08-2026_08-26-AM.sha256\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/snapshots/17-08-2026_08-26-AM/17-08-2026_08-26-AM.sha256\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\", \"/opt/workspace/builds/Machina/T20260816160709-B00000001-F00001011-C9688434a-1a27-4d63-9fc9-b997dccb76a5\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"/tmp/MACHINA_HOME_RECONSTRUCTION_LOG.color-only.md\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/docs/MACHINA_HOME_RECONSTRUCTION_LOG.md\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"/tmp/MACHINA_HOME_RECONSTRUCTION_LOG.upper-toggle.md\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/docs/MACHINA_HOME_RECONSTRUCTION_LOG.md\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/builds/17-08-2026_09-12-AM/17-08-2026_09-12-AM.apk\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"/tmp/17-08-2026_09-12-AM.apk.sha256\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/builds/17-08-2026_09-12-AM/17-08-2026_09-12-AM.apk.sha256\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"/tmp/DeltaIcon-phase12/app/src/main/assets/appfilter.xml\", \"/opt/projects/DeltaIcon/app/src/main/assets/appfilter.xml\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"/tmp/deltaicon-bootstrap/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml\", \"/opt/projects/DeltaIcon/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml\"]\n- [\"cp\", \"app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk\", \"/opt/Android/Machina/outputs/17-08-2026_04-4...\n[Some commands were truncated]\n The writable roots are `/opt/Android`, `/tmp`.\n</permissions instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<collaboration_mode># Collaboration Mode: Default\n\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\n\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `<collaboration_mode>...</collaboration_mode>` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\n\n## request_user_input availability\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only when it is listed in the available tools for this turn.\n\nIn Default mode, strongly prefer making reasonable assumptions and executing the user's request rather than stopping to ask questions.\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only for optional questions where the answer would materially improve the quality of the work.\n\nIf `request_user_input` returns no answers, continue with best judgment instead of asking again or treating the turn as blocked.\n\nNever use the `request_user_input` tool for permission requests or permission-related escalations.\n\nIf explicit user input is required for another reason before progress can safely continue, do not use the `request_user_input` tool. Ask the user directly with one concise plain-text question instead. Never write a multiple choice question as a textual assistant message.\n</collaboration_mode>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<apps_instructions>\n## Apps (Connectors)\nApps (Connectors) can be explicitly triggered in user messages in the format `[$app-name](app://{connector_id})`. Apps can also be implicitly triggered as long as the context suggests usage of available apps.\nAn app is equivalent to a set of MCP tools within the `codex_apps` MCP.\nAn installed app's MCP tools are either provided to you already, or can be lazy-loaded through the `tool_search` tool. If `tool_search` is available, the apps that are searchable by `tools_search` will be listed by it.\nDo not additionally call list_mcp_resources or list_mcp_resource_templates for apps.\n</apps_instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<plugins_instructions>\n## Plugins\nA plugin is a local bundle of skills, MCP servers, and apps.\n### How to use plugins\n- Skill naming: If a plugin contributes skills, those skill entries are prefixed with `plugin_name:` in the Skills list.\n- MCP naming: Plugin-provided MCP tools keep standard MCP identifiers such as `mcp__server__tool`; use tool provenance to tell which plugin they come from.\n- Trigger rules: If the user explicitly names a plugin, prefer capabilities associated with that plugin for that turn.\n- Relationship to capabilities: Plugins are not invoked directly. Use their underlying skills, MCP tools, and app tools to help solve the task.\n- Relevance: Determine what a plugin can help with from explicit user mention or from the plugin-associated skills, MCP tools, and apps exposed elsewhere in this turn.\n- Missing/blocked: If the user requests a plugin that does not have relevant callable capabilities for the task, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\n</plugins_instructions>"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07d19-2774-7f13-8196-1f548029d2a3","content_item_kinds":["host_skills.instructions","permissions.instructions","collaboration_mode.instructions","apps.instructions","plugins.usage_instructions"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07d3a-3a88-7e80-aa16-994be88e4ca0","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<environment_context>\n  <cwd>/opt/Android</cwd>\n  <shell>bash</shell>\n  <current_date>2026-09-07</current_date>\n  <timezone>Europe/Berlin</timezone>\n  <filesystem><workspace_roots><root>/opt/Android</root></workspace_roots><permission_profile type=\"managed\"><file_system type=\"restricted\"><entry access=\"read\"><special>:root</special></entry><entry access=\"write\"><path>/opt/Android</path></entry><entry access=\"write\"><special>:slash_tmp</special></entry><entry access=\"write\"><special>:tmpdir</special></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/opt/Android/.git</path></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/opt/Android/.agents</path></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/opt/Android/.codex</path></entry></file_system></permission_profile></filesystem>\n</environment_context>"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07d19-2774-7f13-8196-1f548029d2a3","content_item_kinds":["environments.environment_context"]}},{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a07d19-2792-7c31-b18c-eb6b69d2aa9c","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Implement PATCH-WORMOS-SYSTEM-WIREGUARD-FAILCLOSED-V1.\n\nGOAL:\nIntegrate the already validated production WireGuard privacy path\nnatively into worm-os / ex-GrapheneOS.\n\nThis is CLIENT/SYSTEM development only.\n\nDO NOT modify OPNsense, Hetzner networking, worm-robot, FireHOL,\nproduction WireGuard server configuration, NAT, DNS or routes.\n\n==================================================\nFROZEN NETWORK — ABSOLUTELY DO NOT CHANGE\n==================================================\n\nOPNsense PUBLIC:\n2.28.34.27\n\nProduction WireGuard endpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51823\n\nOPNsense WG:\n10.60.0.1/24\n\nworm-os:\n10.60.0.10/32\n\nProduction DNS:\n10.60.0.1\n\nAllowedIPs target:\n0.0.0.0/0\n\nPrivate backbone:\n10.90.0.0/16\n\nOPNsense PRIVATE:\n10.90.10.1\n\nworm-robot PRIVATE:\n10.90.20.10\n\nworm-robot PUBLIC:\n162.55.6.173\n\nValidated state:\n\nWG_PRODUCTION=PASS\nFULL_TUNNEL_IPV4=PASS\nPUBLIC_IPV4=2.28.34.27\nPROTECTED_DNS=PASS\nIPV6_DIRECT_BYPASS=NO\nIPV6_PRIVACY=PASS\nPRIVATE_WORM_FOUNDATION=PASS\nPRIVATE_BACKBONE=PASS\nNETWORK_PRIVACY_GATE=PASS\n\nUDP/51823, server identity, NAT, DNS and Hetzner routes are FROZEN.\n\n==================================================\nSOURCE TREE\n==================================================\n\nUse the worm-os / GrapheneOS build tree under the project build\nenvironment.\n\nAudit the actual source tree first.\n\nDo not assume package paths or Android framework architecture.\nLocate the real implementation points in the checked-out source.\n\nCompare changes against upstream GrapheneOS/AOSP where appropriate.\n\n==================================================\nSECURITY MODEL\n==================================================\n\nworm-os networking must operate as:\n\nBOOT\n  |\n  v\nNETWORK BLOCKED\n  |\n  v\nWireGuard credentials available\n  |\n  v\nWG interface created\n  |\n  v\nauthenticated tunnel\n  |\n  v\nprotected DNS available\n  |\n  v\nNETWORK ALLOWED\n\nIf WireGuard:\n\n- fails\n- crashes\n- loses handshake\n- loses required route\n- cannot resolve protected DNS\n- changes underlying Wi-Fi/5G network\n- is killed/restarted\n- fails during boot\n\nthen protected traffic MUST NOT silently fall back to direct Internet.\n\nFAIL CLOSED.\n\n==================================================\n1. ARCHITECTURE AUDIT\n==================================================\n\nLocate and document relevant components:\n\n- Android networking framework\n- ConnectivityService\n- netd\n- VPN framework\n- WireGuard/kernel support\n- VpnService / platform VPN implementation\n- always-on VPN handling\n- lockdown/block-without-VPN\n- boot lifecycle\n- user unlock lifecycle\n- Keystore\n- SELinux policy\n- Settings integration\n- SystemUI indicators if relevant\n- Wi-Fi/mobile handover\n- captive portal behavior\n- emergency/system networking exceptions\n- backup/restore behavior\n\nCreate architecture map before implementation.\n\n==================================================\n2. IMPLEMENTATION STRATEGY\n==================================================\n\nPrefer the smallest auditable integration using existing Android\nVPN/lockdown primitives and existing kernel/platform capabilities.\n\nDo NOT create an unnecessary custom networking stack.\n\nReuse upstream security mechanisms where they satisfy the Worm policy.\n\nAny deviation from GrapheneOS/AOSP must be documented.\n\n==================================================\n3. SYSTEM OWNERSHIP\n==================================================\n\nProduction Worm WireGuard must not depend on the user manually opening\nthe WireGuard app.\n\nTarget:\n\nSYSTEM_OWNED_WG=YES\n\nThe system must be capable of bringing up the protected tunnel\nautomatically according to Worm policy.\n\nUser applications must not be able to silently disable the privacy\nboundary.\n\nDo not remove Android's security boundaries to achieve this.\n\n==================================================\n4. DEVICE IDENTITY\n==================================================\n\nDO NOT embed the currently tested Android private key in source,\nrepository, system image, vendor image, OTA package or build logs.\n\nProduction device private identity must be provisioned/generated\nper-device.\n\nPrivate key requirements:\n\n- generated/provisioned on device\n- protected by appropriate Android/Keystore mechanisms where feasible\n- excluded from backup\n- excluded from logs\n- excluded from bugreports where possible\n- never committed to git\n- never included in OTA payload\n- never printed\n\nPublic key may be exported for OPNsense peer provisioning.\n\nDocument lifecycle:\n\nGENERATE\nSTORE\nLOAD\nROTATE\nDELETE/RESET\n\n==================================================\n5. SERVER PARAMETERS\n==================================================\n\nSystem configuration may contain NON-SECRET server parameters:\n\nEndpoint:\n2.28.34.27:51823\n\nServer public key:\nuse the currently validated production server PUBLIC key from the\nauthoritative project record.\n\nDO NOT guess or substitute it.\n\nClient:\n10.60.0.10/32 for the current development device.\n\nDNS:\n10.60.0.1\n\nAllowedIPs:\n0.0.0.0/0\n\nKeepalive:\n25\n\nNO PSK unless a later explicit design requires one.\n\n==================================================\n6. NATIVE FAIL-CLOSED\n==================================================\n\nImplement a native Worm protected-network policy.\n\nBefore WG is ready:\nBLOCK normal application Internet.\n\nAfter WG is ready:\nALLOW normal application traffic only through the protected tunnel.\n\nOn tunnel failure:\nBLOCK again.\n\nNo fallback to:\n- cellular direct IPv4\n- Wi-Fi direct IPv4\n- direct IPv6\n\nFail-closed must cover both IPv4 and IPv6.\n\nDo not implement this only as a UI toggle.\n\nEnforcement must exist in the networking/firewall layer.\n\n==================================================\n7. ENDPOINT BOOTSTRAP EXCEPTION\n==================================================\n\nFail-closed must still permit the minimum traffic required to establish\nWireGuard itself.\n\nAllow only the required bootstrap path to:\n\n2.28.34.27 UDP/51823\n\nover the currently selected underlying physical network.\n\nDo not permit general Internet before the tunnel is established.\n\nIf DNS is unnecessary for the numeric endpoint, do not open bootstrap\nDNS.\n\n==================================================\n8. PROTECTED DNS\n==================================================\n\nAfter tunnel establishment:\n\nsystem DNS:\n10.60.0.1\n\nPrevent ordinary application DNS from bypassing the protected path.\n\nNo fallback to carrier/Wi-Fi DNS for protected networking.\n\nAudit:\nUDP/53\nTCP/53\nPrivate DNS / DoT interactions\nDNS resolver behavior\nnetwork transitions\n\nDo not break Android resolver security unnecessarily.\n\n==================================================\n9. IPv6\n==================================================\n\nCurrent validated production design is IPv4 tunnel with no direct IPv6\negress.\n\nUntil tunneled IPv6 is explicitly implemented:\n\nBLOCK direct IPv6 Internet while Worm protected mode is active.\n\nDo not disable unrelated local IPv6 functionality more broadly than\nnecessary.\n\nNo IPv6 privacy leak.\n\n==================================================\n10. BOOT\n==================================================\n\nRequired state machine:\n\nEARLY_BOOT\n -> FAIL_CLOSED\n\nNETWORK_AVAILABLE\n -> endpoint bootstrap only\n\nWG_STARTING\n -> FAIL_CLOSED\n\nWG_READY\n -> PROTECTED\n\nWG_FAILURE\n -> FAIL_CLOSED\n\nNETWORK_CHANGE\n -> WG_RECONNECTING / FAIL_CLOSED\n\nUSER_UNLOCK must not create an unprotected networking window.\n\nDocument what can and cannot operate before credential unlock.\n\n==================================================\n11. WI-FI / 5G HANDOVER\n==================================================\n\nTest:\n\n5G -> Wi-Fi\nWi-Fi -> 5G\nWi-Fi AP change\ntemporary signal loss\nairplane-mode transition where practical\n\nDuring handover:\n\nno direct Internet leak\n\nExpected:\n\nWG reconnects\nor\nnetwork remains blocked.\n\n==================================================\n12. CRASH / FAILURE TESTS\n==================================================\n\nTest controlled failures:\n\n- WG process/service restart\n- interface removal\n- route loss\n- DNS failure\n- endpoint temporary unreachable\n- underlying network loss\n- peer unavailable\n\nFor each:\n\nDIRECT_IPV4_LEAK=NO\nDIRECT_IPV6_LEAK=NO\n\nNetwork either:\nPROTECTED\nor\nBLOCKED\n\nNever DIRECT.\n\n==================================================\n13. USER CONTROLS\n==================================================\n\nDefine Worm UX state:\n\nPROTECTED\nCONNECTING\nBLOCKED\n\nDo not present a normal \"disable protection and continue directly\"\ncontrol in production Worm mode.\n\nDevelopment builds may expose an explicit developer override if needed,\nbut:\n\n- clearly marked\n- disabled by default\n- not present/enabled in production\n- audited\n\n==================================================\n14. SYSTEM INDICATOR\n==================================================\n\nExpose a simple system-readable state:\n\nWORM_NETWORK_PROTECTED\nWORM_NETWORK_CONNECTING\nWORM_NETWORK_BLOCKED\n\nThis can later drive the Worm glyph/status UI.\n\nDo not make UI state the security enforcement mechanism.\n\n==================================================\n15. SELINUX\n==================================================\n\nAdd only the minimum SELinux permissions required.\n\nNo broad:\npermissive domains\nallow-all networking\nuntrusted_app exceptions\n\nRun neverallow checks.\n\nDocument each new rule.\n\n==================================================\n16. UPDATE / OTA\n==================================================\n\nSystem WG implementation must survive OTA.\n\nDevice private key must survive normal OTA but must NOT be included in\nthe OTA image itself.\n\nFactory reset behavior:\ndelete device identity.\n\nBackup/restore:\ndo not export WireGuard private identity by default.\n\n==================================================\n17. BUILD GATE\n==================================================\n\nBuild the affected components first.\n\nThen perform the appropriate full worm-os build gate.\n\nRequired:\n\nBUILD=PASS\nSELINUX=PASS\nNEVERALLOW=PASS\nNO_SECRET_IN_BUILD=PASS\n\nDo not declare runtime PASS from compilation alone.\n\n==================================================\n18. DEVICE RUNTIME GATE\n==================================================\n\nOn the real development device verify:\n\nSYSTEM_OWNED_WG=PASS\nAUTO_START=PASS\nWG_HANDSHAKE=PASS\n\nPUBLIC_IPV4=2.28.34.27\n\nDNS=10.60.0.1\nDNS_PROTECTED=PASS\n\nPRIVATE_OPNSENSE=10.90.10.1 PASS\nPRIVATE_ROBOT=10.90.20.10 PASS\n\nDIRECT_IPV4_LEAK=NO\nDIRECT_IPV6_LEAK=NO\n\nFAIL_CLOSED_WG_DOWN=PASS\nFAIL_CLOSED_BOOT=PASS\nFAIL_CLOSED_HANDOVER=PASS\n\n==================================================\n19. REGRESSION\n==================================================\n\nVerify server/network frozen state remains:\n\nOPNSENSE=UNCHANGED\nWG_PORT=51823\nPRIVATE_BACKBONE=PASS\nROBOT=UNTOUCHED\n\nDo not remediate server-side from this patch.\n\n==================================================\n20. SECRET SCAN\n==================================================\n\nScan modified source, generated configs, build logs and patch docs.\n\nMust not contain:\n\n- WireGuard private keys\n- PSKs\n- SSH private keys\n- passwords\n- Hetzner tokens\n- signing private keys\n- backup encryption keys\n\nPublic keys are permitted.\n\n==================================================\n21. OUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nPATCH-WORMOS-SYSTEM-WG-FAILCLOSED-V1/\n\nInclude:\n\n00-SUMMARY.md\n01-ARCHITECTURE.md\n02-UPSTREAM-DIFF.md\n03-SYSTEM-WG.md\n04-KEY-LIFECYCLE.md\n05-FAIL-CLOSED.md\n06-BOOT.md\n07-DNS.md\n08-IPV6.md\n09-HANDOVER.md\n10-SELINUX.md\n11-OTA.md\n12-TEST-MATRIX.md\n13-SECURITY.md\n14-ROLLBACK.md\n15-BACKLOG.md\nmodified-files.txt\ntest-results.txt\nfindings.json\n\n==================================================\nFINAL GATE\n==================================================\n\nReport separately:\n\nSOURCE_IMPLEMENTATION=\nPASS / PARTIAL / FAIL\n\nBUILD=\nPASS / FAIL / NOT_RUN\n\nSELINUX=\nPASS / FAIL / NOT_RUN\n\nSYSTEM_OWNED_WG=\nPASS / PENDING / FAIL\n\nAUTO_START=\nPASS / PENDING / FAIL\n\nWG_RUNTIME=\nPASS / PENDING / FAIL\n\nFAIL_CLOSED_IPV4=\nPASS / PENDING / FAIL\n\nFAIL_CLOSED_IPV6=\nPASS / PENDING / FAIL\n\nFAIL_CLOSED_BOOT=\nPASS / PENDING / FAIL\n\nFAIL_CLOSED_HANDOVER=\nPASS / PENDING / FAIL\n\nPROTECTED_DNS=\nPASS / PENDING / FAIL\n\nPRIVATE_ACCESS=\nPASS / PENDING / FAIL\n\nPUBLIC_IPV4=\n2.28.34.27 / PENDING / FAIL\n\nNO_SECRETS=\nPASS / FAIL\n\nSERVER_NETWORK=\nFROZEN_UNCHANGED / FAIL\n\nWORM_OS_SYSTEM_NETWORK=\nPASS / PARTIAL / FAIL\n\nPASS is forbidden until real-device runtime tests pass.\n\nDo not modify OPNsense.\nDo not start OTA de